window.SLATE_DOCS_INDEX = {"en":[{"t":"What SLATE is","u":"01-what-is-slate.html","s":"Start","h":["What SLATE is","From inspiration to delivery","What problems it solves","Core capabilities at a glance","How it relates to external Coding Agents","Design principles","Where your data is","Who it is for","Who it is not for","Glossary","Next steps"],"x":"SLATE (砚 — the inkstone) is an AI collaboration console that runs on your own computer. It grinds rough inspiration into a structured plan, then decides whether to do the work itself or produce a high-quality Prompt for an external Coding Agent. This page gives you the overall picture: what problem it solves, what it can do, and who it suits. What SLATE is SLATE is a local-first AI collaboration tool focused on prompt engineering, context management and organizing project inspiration. It brings mainstream large models, built-in tools, team debates, workflows and whiteboard-style logic chains into a single interface. It has two faces; pick one by scenario: 1. Do it yourself — drive the built-in tools directly to get work done: read the project, write files, run commands, generate documents and diagrams. 2. Produce a Prompt — pack context, constraints and acceptance criteria into a deliverable Prompt and hand it to an external Coding Agent such as Claude Code, Codex or Cursor. Inspiration is for diverging; SLATE is for condensing it into a plan you can actually execute. From inspiration to delivery An idea usually travels from vague to delivered like this: 1. Grind — hand a rough sentence to Grind Mode; the AI asks about goals, audience, deliverables and acceptance criteria, and finally condenses it into an ink draft. 2. Reason it out — drop the ink draft onto the Whiteboard and use cards plus connections to make dependencies and order explicit; you can also let the AI Team debate a round first. 3. Execute — open Target Mode and let the model push forward autonomously through six stages, verifying item by item, and only close out when everything is settled. 4. Hand off — if the work suits an external Coding Agent better, use Prompt Factory to pack context and constraints into one Prompt and hand it over. Each step can be used on its own; you don't have to walk the whole way. What problems it solves An AI tool that gives you nothing but a chat window means the following four things are all on you. SLATE takes them over. Common trouble What SLATE does --- --- Context management Full-text search and export of conversation history, automatic compression past a threshold, long-term memory recalled across sessions, knowledge base injected on demand Prompt engineering Prompt Factory packs the constitution, context and constraints into one Prompt; Grind Mode questions a vague idea into a structured task brief Project-level operations With a project open you can read files, change code, run commands and check Git status, without copying and pasting back and forth Token consumption Smart compression, tiered calls, lightweight models for discussion and heavy models for decisions, every AI-assisted feature individually switchable off Core capabilities at a glance Capability What it is Value in one line --- --- --- Multi-model Chat Mainstream models from home and abroad + any OpenAI-compatible endpoint + local models, unified in one Chat interface Change models without changing tools Agent Autopilot & Target Mode Detects requests like inspecting a project, editing files, running commands or debugging and automatically enters a multi-turn Agent loop; Target Mode is the explicit six-stage, tighter closed-loop version No repeatedly sending \"continue\" — it closes out on its own Grind Mode One rough sentence, three rounds of questions from the AI, ground into a task brief When the idea is still fuzzy, grind it clear before you start MCP tools & Skills Thirty-plus built-in tools, and new capabilities can be added with SKILL.md The model can actually operate your files and command line Actions Write \"the necessary process for doing something\" as a manual, and the model advances along it Settle a repeated process once, then follow it every time Expert Packs Persona + rules + knowledge + skills packed into one package, importable and exportable Configure a domain expert once, reuse it everywhere AI Team & Workflow Multi-role multi-round debate (propose / rebut / decide), plus DAG workflow pipelines Debate the important decisions once, then act Whiteboard Cards plus connections to organise thinking; tool runs are automatically recorded as step cards See the whole reasoning process on one screen Memory & Knowledge Automatically settles conversation points, recalls across sessions, and can capture technical insights when a conversation ends No re-explaining the background every time Prompt Factory One click to integrate project context, task description and constraints into a deliverable Prompt The Prompt you hand to an external Coding Agent comes from here Mobile remote control Open the LAN address in a phone or tablet browser to get the dedicated mobile interface Check progress and give instructions away from your computer How it relates to external Coding Agents SLATE doesn't replace Claude Code, Codex or Cursor — it stands in front of them. - Working directly in SLATE: suited to tasks that need reading a project, running commands and repeated verification, without ever leaving this interface. - Preparing in SLATE: suited to big overhauls you'd hand to an external Agent. It first organises project context, constraints and acceptance criteria into a structurally clear Prompt that you copy out. Both ways of working share the same memory, the same project constitution and the same set of Expert Packs. Design principles - Pure black, white and grey — the interface itself uses no colour gradients or decorative shadows, leaving attention for the content; the only colour is each provider's brand mark, drawn in its official colours and not changed with the theme. - Native stack — zero npm, zero build tools; the interface is the files. - Local-first — all data lives on this machine and passes through no cloud relay. - Token thrift — smart compression, tiered calls, silent handling; not a single request is wasted. - Never hangs — idle watchdog, automatic retry on empty content, request timeouts, and a UI fallback: several layers of defence. Where your data is Conversation history, long-term memory, knowledge documents, Expert Packs, action flows, Scheduled Tasks and all settings are saved as files on this machine. API Keys are stored locally (encrypted at rest) and are used only to call the models you configured. Specifically: - every conversation and every message; - long-term memory entries and the User Profile; - knowledge documents and document archives; - your own Skills, Expert Packs and process manuals; - scheduled / event tasks and their run records; - preferences for models, Approval, themes, typography and so on. Backup, restore and cleanup for all of this is handled in the data-related block of Settings, and you can also export everything to a single file to migrate machines. SLATE itself is a local app, but model inference happens at the model provider you choose. So Chat needs access to the model endpoint you configured; the built-in web search, web fetch and update checks each need network access too. Who it is for - People who have an idea but don't know how to turn it into an executable plan. - People already using Claude Code / Codex / Cursor who want to produce a high-quality Prompt locally first. - People who need an AI workbench that remembers project background long-term and doesn't forget things across sessions. - People who care about data staying on their machine and don't want project content handed to a cloud collaboration platform. - People used to local models (Ollama / LM Studio) who want local and online models in one interface. Who it is not for - People looking for an IDE replacement: SLATE is the collaborator and the dispatcher; it doesn't provide a full IDE with editor, debugger and completion. - People looking for a cloud multi-person collaboration platform: SLATE is a local app, with no account system and no shared cloud space. - People who expect it to work out of the box without bringing their own API Key: model quota is yours to arrange. - People who want to train large models with it: SLATE is a console, not a training framework. Glossary Term Meaning --- --- Agent Autopilot The default auto-advance layer: on detecting an environment-operation request it enters a multi-turn Agent loop and closes out on its own when done Target Mode A strongly closed mode you turn on explicitly, advancing through six stages — \"goal → plan → execute → verify → report → trace\" — with a larger turn budget Grind (磨墨) The process of grinding a rough idea into a structured task brief: three rounds of questioning, up to ten turns Actions Process manuals that state \"the necessary process for doing something\"; the model reads them as needed and advances along them Skill A pluggable capability described by one SKILL.md , injected into context as soon as it is mentioned in Chat MCP The protocol layer through which the model calls external tools; both SLATE's built-in tools and external MCP Servers come in through it Expert Pack A distributable configuration packing persona, rules, knowledge and skills Ink Flow (砚流) When the model writes tool parameters token by token, the preview mechanism that renders the shaping of those parameters live in the interface Next steps Install it, then run it: Install & launch."},{"t":"Install & launch","u":"02-install.html","s":"Start","h":["System requirements","Option 1: Windows installer","Option 2: macOS installer","Option 3: run from source","Choosing between the three","What happens on first launch","First thing after installing","Automatic update check at launch","Uninstalling and your data","If it won't start"],"x":"This page gets SLATE installed and running, and explains what happens on first launch and where your data lives. System requirements Item Requirement --- --- Windows desktop Use the installer directly; no extra runtime needed macOS desktop Use the .dmg directly; no extra runtime needed Running from source Python 3.13 or later Source platforms Windows / Linux / macOS all work Network After the first launch, a network connection is needed to call the models you configured Browser When running from source, open the local address in a fairly recent version of Chrome / Edge / Firefox Option 1: Windows installer Suits most users: install and go, no Python needed. 1. Go to the project's GitHub Releases page and download the latest installer SLATE-Setup-x.x.x.exe . 2. Double-click to run the installer. 3. During installation, choose the interface language: 简体中文 or English . 4. When it finishes, launch from the Start menu or the desktop shortcut. The language is fixed at the install step; the interface and all UI text then use that language throughout. To change it, run the installer again and pick. One installation only ever runs one SLATE. If the window is already open (or already collapsed into the system notification area) and you launch again, you don't get a second window or a second backend: the new launch brings the running window back to the front, then exits itself. The lock goes away with the process, so a crash or being ended from Task Manager never leaves it in a \"can never open again\" state. Option 2: macOS installer On an Apple computer use the .dmg : install and go, also with no Python needed. 1. Go to the project's GitHub Releases page and download the latest SLATE-Setup-x.x.x.dmg (the same directory has SLATE-Setup-x.x.x.dmg.sha256 ; if you want to verify before installing, use shasum -a 256 -c ). 2. Double-click to mount, then drag \"SLATE 砚\" into the \"Applications\" folder. 3. First launch : in Finder, right-click (or long-press) the SLATE icon → \"Open\" → then click \"Open\" once more. 4. After that you can launch it normally by double-clicking. The .dmg is produced directly by CI, with no Apple Developer certificate signature and no notarization, so the first open is stopped by Gatekeeper with \"cannot verify the developer\". Step 3 above — right-click → Open — is the proper way around it, and it only needs doing once. To check that the download is intact, compare it against the .sha256 file of the same name. The installed app keeps conversation history, API Keys, backend logs and the WebView profile in ~/Library/Application Support/SLATE , not inside the .app bundle. Upgrading replaces the whole .app , and your data isn't taken along with it. Running from source still keeps data in the repository's data/ . On a Mac, closing the window quits the app, and launching twice opens two copies. Both will be addressed later. Option 3: run from source For people who want to change a few things themselves, or who use Linux (macOS can run this way too, it's just unnecessary — there's a ready-made .dmg ). Windows: Linux / macOS: Once it has started, open this in a browser: You can also run the desktop entry directly with python desktop.py to open the same app as a desktop window. Choosing between the three Item Windows installer macOS installer From source --- --- --- --- Needs Python No No Yes, Python 3.13+ Platforms Windows macOS Windows / Linux / macOS Upgrading Download the new installer and install over it Download the new .dmg and drag once Pull the latest code and restart Changing the source Not suited Not suited Suited; changes take effect after a restart Who it suits People who just want to draft ideas and run tasks People who just want to draft ideas and run tasks People who want to tinker with the interface and internal flows What happens on first launch 1. After the main window opens, a new user guide appears first, using a few cards to introduce the multi-model Chat, Target Mode, Grind Mode, built-in tools, Memory & Knowledge, and the other panels. 2. Click \"Get Started\" to close the guide and enter the main interface. 3. The main interface's top bar has five tabs: Chat / Whiteboard / Extensions / Factory / Settings , one shown at a time. It opens on \"Chat\" by default, and that area is itself split into two columns: the left sidebar holds Projects and Tasks, the right side is the Chat message stream and the input box, and the right edge of the message area slides out the Task list and the background task panel on demand. Skills, Tools, modes, MCP, Expert Packs and Actions — everything about \"what the model can use\" — lives on the Extensions tab. You only see the new user guide once. To see it again, go to Settings → About and click the \"New user guide\" button. First thing after installing A freshly installed SLATE has no model available yet. Configure an API Key first: 1. Click the Keys button on the right of the top bar, or switch straight to the Settings tab. 2. In \"Model config\", find the model you want to use, fill in the matching API Key and save. 3. To connect another provider's endpoint, click \"+ Add model\" in the same block and fill in the model ID and API address. 4. Back in the Chat interface, pick the model you just configured in the model dropdown in the input area. Configuration details and the list of providers are in Models & API Keys. SLATE itself runs on your machine, but it calls the models you configured to do Chat and inference. So before the first use, make sure this computer can reach the service endpoint you chose and that the API Key has quota available. Without a working model configured you can still see the interface and your history, but messages you send will get no reply. API Keys are stored in the local app configuration; you don't need to copy them into notes or project files. Please avoid writing them into documents that will be committed to a code repository or shared with others; if one leaks, revoke it in the provider's console immediately and generate a new one. Automatic update check at launch SLATE checks for a new version every time it starts: - When a new version is found, a banner appears just below the top bar, with entry points to download and to read the release notes. - The top-right corner of the banner closes it; after choosing \"Ignore This Version\" it stops prompting for that version. - You can also check manually at any time: Settings → About , click \"Check for Updates\", where your current version number is also shown. The installer always comes from the project's GitHub Releases; SLATE never downloads executables from anywhere else. Uninstalling and your data Uninstall : on Windows, uninstall SLATE from the system's \"Apps\" list; the uninstaller removes the application itself. Data : conversation history, memory, knowledge documents, Expert Packs, action flows and all settings are saved as files on this machine and are not uploaded to the cloud. So: - To move to a new computer, export a backup first, then import it on the new machine (see Data, backup & storage). - To clean up completely, it's also best to export a backup once and confirm it is correct before deleting anything. - Uninstalling the app and deleting data are two separate things; handle each according to what you need. If it won't start - Nothing appears after double-clicking the icon: most likely a SLATE is already running (the window may be tucked into the system notification area). Launching again only brings that window back, it doesn't open a second one; look for it in the notification area or the taskbar. - If starting from source fails, first confirm Python is at least 3.13 and the dependencies are fully installed, then run the start script again. - Watch the command line for errors during startup; in most cases the last few lines show the cause. - If the interface opens but messages get no reply, it's most likely a model key or network problem. Go to Settings → API test and use the shortest possible request to verify the link works. If it still doesn't work, check FAQ."},{"t":"Interface overview: tabs and the three-column workspace","u":"03-interface.html","s":"Start","h":["Overall layout","Top bar","Left sidebar","Projects tab","Tasks tab","Summary tab","Middle Chat area","Input area","＋ Mode menu","Theme and appearance","Mobile switches to its own interface automatically","New user guide"],"x":"This page walks you through the main interface from end to end, so you know where each entry point is and what happens when you click it. Remember one sentence first: SLATE is one top bar plus four workspaces ; only one is shown at a time, and clicking a top-bar tab switches between them. Overall layout Area Where What you can do --- --- --- Chat Top-bar tab \"Chat\" Talk to the model, run Agent loops, see tool cards and the Task list, manage conversation history Whiteboard Top-bar tab \"Whiteboard\" Cards plus connections to organise thinking; switch between the Git tree / flow / kanban / outline / Workflow views Extensions Top-bar tab \"Extensions\" Manage what the model can use: seven columns — Skills, built-in tools, modes, new features (self-made tools), MCP, Expert Packs, Actions Factory Top-bar tab \"Factory\" Integrate project context, task and constraints into a deliverable Prompt Settings Top-bar tab \"Settings\" Model keys, Context, Approval mode, Backup, LAN and About Settings is a standalone configuration area and doesn't count as a workspace. What people usually call \"three columns\" is not in the top bar; it's the three columns the Chat workspace splits into itself: the left sidebar holds three tabs — Projects, Tasks and Summary; the middle message stream holds the Chat content and the input box; the right edge slides out the Task list column, the background task panel and the Ink panel on demand. Team Chat is a mode switch inside the message stream, not a fourth workspace. The interface is fairly dense; on first use, walk it in this order: top bar → four workspaces → Chat area left sidebar → message area → input area . Pause at each spot and just see what buttons are there; there's no need to memorize everything at once. Top bar The top bar spans the whole window and splits into three sections: - Left : the app mark (砚 · SLATE). - Centre : five tabs — Chat / Whiteboard / Extensions / Factory / Settings — for switching workspaces. - Right : four round buttons. Top-bar button What it does --- --- Keys Jumps straight to \"Model config\" in Settings, to manage model keys Remote Jumps straight to \"LAN remote control\" in Settings, to see the mobile access address Light/Dark One-click switch between the light and dark themes; while a custom theme is on it dims and locks, and clicking it explains why Settings Opens the Settings page When a new version is found, the banner appears directly below the top bar, with a download entry point and \"Ignore This Version\". \"Keys\" and \"Remote\" at the top right of the top bar are two shortcuts: they open the very same Settings page, just scrolling automatically to the matching block. So when you can't remember where something lives, these two buttons in the top bar are the fastest way in. Left sidebar On the left of the Chat area is the sidebar, with three tabs at the top: Projects / Tasks / Summary . The divider between the sidebar and the message area can be dragged to adjust the width. Projects tab - At the top is the current project name and its action entry points; when the project is a workspace it carries a \"Workspace\" badge. - Below is the project file tree; click a file to preview its content right underneath. - In the registered projects list you can switch projects, or open a new directory. Tasks tab This is the entry point for all your sessions: Element Description --- --- ＋ New Chat Start a new session, shortcut Ctrl + N Sort dropdown Recently updated / By project / By status / Creation time / By usage Manage Bulk manage historical sessions Search box Search historical sessions; a match shows a Context excerpt Session list Each session carries its own status marker There are four status markers in total , and they record \"how the previous session ended\": Marker Meaning --- --- Needs action The previous session was stopped, or there is a next step waiting for your decision Error The previous session ended in failure In progress This session is genuinely generating right now; the icon has a breathing animation Done, unread It finished but you haven't clicked into it yet; clicking into the session clears it The sort preference syncs across devices; status markers only travel on your own machine and never reach another device. Summary tab Summarizes the usage of the current session in one place, so you can see how much this session cost. Middle Chat area On the left of the message area's title bar is a two-mode switch, Chat / Team : everyday Q&A and writing code go in \"Chat\"; to have several roles debate or to run a Workflow, switch to \"Team\". On the right of the title bar is a row of action buttons: Button What it does --- --- Compress context manually Compress the conversation history right away, without waiting for the automatic threshold Memory & profile View and manage long-term memory and the User Profile Expert Pack management Import, export and edit Expert Packs Prompt material Manage reusable prompt snippets Task list Collapse or expand the Task list column on the right of the message area Background tasks See the status and output of background terminal tasks Below the title bar is a usage info bar, showing the Token consumption of this session. The message area body , from top to bottom: - the Chat message stream, each reply tagged with the model name and usage; - tool call cards, showing the Tool name, parameters and execution status; - a collapsible thinking panel that expands live while the model reasons and collapses automatically once thinking is done; - the Task list column on the right, showing progress item by item while a big task runs, and collapsible as a whole; - the background terminal task panel and the Grind Mode Ink panel, which appear only in their own contexts. Input area The input area sits at the bottom of the message area and consists of a multi-line input box row plus a toolbar row. Input box : supports multiple lines. Enter sends, Shift + Enter inserts a new line. Toolbar left side , starting from the \"＋\" button: Control Description --- --- ＋ Mode menu Collects every mode entry point; see the next section Reply mode The mode capsule at the lower left of the input box, deciding how this session works: four levels — Smart / Chat / Economy / Turbo — each with its own icon and colour, and custom modes are possible too; switching only changes this session, while the default level and custom modes are managed in \"Extensions → Modes\" Approval mode When this session should stop and ask you, four levels, changeable at any time: Manual approval / Auto approval / Full access (capsule marked red) / Night mode; pick \"Night mode\" and it asks nothing at all Add attachment Send files along with the message Expert Pack Pick an Expert Pack; its persona and rules apply throughout Model Which model this session uses Reasoning effort Offers levels based on what the model can do, each labelled below with the matching ink tone; levels this model can't handle are greyed out with the reason stated Toolbar right side , in order: polish prompt, voice input and send. While generating, the send button becomes Stop — clicking it really cancels, it doesn't just make the interface go quiet. Clicking Polish prompt (the star to the left of the voice button) hands the half-written draft in the input box to the current model to rewrite, but the rewrite doesn't go straight into the input box: it is first placed side by side with the original in a popup for you to compare, and only clicking Use it writes it back. Clicking Keep original , pressing Esc, or clicking outside the popup all count as keeping the original, and not one character in the input box changes. If you edit the draft again during the few seconds you're waiting (including characters written in live by voice), that result is discarded and won't overwrite what you just wrote. Which model is used can be set separately in Settings → AI-assisted features; turn it off and not a single request is sent. ＋ Mode menu Click the \"＋\" to the left of the input box and a \"Modes & features\" menu pops up, containing four mode rows plus a mention group: This menu is pinned directly above the \"＋\" button and feels the same as the Approval mode and reasoning effort on the same toolbar: pressing \"＋\" again, clicking anywhere outside the menu, or pressing Esc all collapse it. - Grind Mode — grinds a rough idea into a task brief; the small inline text shows the current grinding progress. - Brainstorm — a toggle. - Target Mode — a toggle, the explicit six-stage strong closed loop. - Scheduled Tasks — create and manage scheduled / event tasks. - Mention (@ prefix) — Skill / Tool / MCP / file; clicking any of them pops up the matching candidate list, and the selection is written into the message as a mention. Theme and appearance The \"Light/Dark\" button in the top bar switches between light and dark in one click, effective immediately. This preference syncs along with your settings, so accessing the same SLATE from another device gives you the same theme. To change the whole face, go to Settings → Custom theme : four source colours (background / panel / body text / accent) plus two font sets — body text and code (you can import your own font files too) — plus section opacity, one local background image and mask density, with 10 basic presets you can click straight away; if you have Wallpaper Engine installed, you can even grab the preview image of the wallpaper it is currently using as the background. The other thirty-odd colour tokens are derived from these four source colours, with contrast as the fallback during derivation, so the palette never smears into something unreadable. While a custom theme is on, the light/dark switch is locked — two palettes talking at once would only contradict each other. The button doesn't grey out; clicking it tells you why nothing changed and where to turn it off. Details are in Settings & security approvals. Mobile switches to its own interface automatically When you open the LAN address in a phone or tablet browser, SLATE automatically enters the mobile interface, with five tabs along the bottom. Accessing from a desktop browser still gives you the full desktop interface. See Mobile remote control & LAN access. New user guide A guide pops up on first launch, using cards to introduce the most-used entry points. To see it again, go to Settings → About and click the \"New user guide\" button."},{"t":"Models & API Keys","u":"04-models.html","s":"Start","h":["Where to configure","Preset models","Adding a custom model","Using local models","API Keys and data safety","How to set the context budget","How to choose the thinking effort","Responses API mode","Division of labor between lightweight and heavy models","Switching models and usage display","FAQ"],"x":"This page shows you how to bring models in: how to fill in keys for preset models, how to add a custom endpoint, how to point at local models, and how to read the context budget and usage. Where to configure Two entry points, leading to the same page: - the Keys button on the right of the top bar; - the top-bar Settings tab, then Model config in the left navigation. The \"Model config\" block holds three things: the custom models list, the built-in model keys list, and the Responses API mode toggle. Each model row is followed by a \"Max context\" control (slider + exact number box + probe window button; see \"How to set the context budget\" below). Preset models SLATE ships with a model list, appearing in four groups in the model dropdown of the input area: Group Contains --- --- International models GPT series, Claude series, Gemini series Domestic models DeepSeek, Kimi, Qwen, GLM, Doubao, MiniMax, ERNIE Local models One built-in local model entry, for Ollama / LM Studio Custom Endpoints you added yourself In the dropdown, the dot in front of each entry is the key status: configured is solid, not configured is hollow. Hovering over an option also shows the model ID, the API address and whether that model supports the Responses API. Next to the dot is that provider's brand mark. Built-in models and models you add yourself go through the same judgment: they are recognised by model name , and only when the name doesn't reveal a brand is the API address consulted — which is why deepseek-r1:1.5b running on local Ollama still draws the DeepSeek mark rather than Ollama's. If it can't be recognised, nothing is drawn: better to leave it blank than to slap another provider's logo on it. The marks are each provider's official graphics shipped with the app: the 47 that have colour originals are drawn in the brand's own colours, while the other 11 (OpenAI, Anthropic, xAI, Ollama, Z.ai and others) only ship a monochrome version and are drawn in a neutral grey. Neither follows the theme — the ink and accent colours picked in \"Extensions → Appearance\" can't tint brand colours, and switching light/dark won't smear them into a black blob. Next to GLM (Zhipu) the mark drawn is Z.ai : that company has changed its outward identity to Z.ai and we follow the new mark, for which the app ships only a monochrome version. Which specific models are available depends on what your interface lists. Steps to configure a key for one model: 1. Find it in \"Built-in model keys\". 2. Paste the API Key into the input box and click the save button on the right. 3. A configured input box shows \"Configured\"; clear the content and save again to delete that key. Adding a custom model As long as your provider offers an OpenAI-compatible interface, you can connect it. 1. Click + Add model at the top right of \"Model config\". 2. Fill in these fields: Field Description --- --- Model name / ID The model identifier your provider requires; also the text shown in the dropdown Request mode OpenAI-compatible, Anthropic, or direct curl Base URL Your API address API Key Optional; endpoints that are local or need no auth can be left blank Context Window The context size this model can hold 3. Click \"Add\" to save; it appears in the \"Custom\" group of the model dropdown. After adding an endpoint, first fire the shortest possible request in Settings → API test . Once it works, set it as your regular model — that saves a lot of back-and-forth guessing about \"is the model bad or is the config wrong\". Using local models To keep inference entirely on your machine, use Ollama or LM Studio. - The model list already has a local model entry, pointing by default at the local Ollama compatibility address (default port 11434). - When using LM Studio, put the listening address it shows into that entry's Base URL. - If you changed the port yourself, enter the port actually being listened on. - Local models don't verify an API Key; the dropdown labels them as local models. A local model's address points at your own computer and needs no internet. So it also works offline; conversely, whether the model can run at all and how large a context it can open depend on your machine's graphics card and memory. API Keys and data safety - API Keys are stored locally (encrypted at rest) and used only to call the models you configured. - Keys are never written into conversation records, nor do they land in your working directory along with project files. - They can be carried along when you export a Backup and keep working after importing to a new machine. Don't copy API Keys into project files, notes or screenshots. They spread along with code repositories and chat logs; once you suspect a leak, revoke it in the provider's console immediately and generate a new one. How to set the context budget Every model has its own context budget. The larger you open it, the more material a single request can carry, and the higher the consumption. - The Max context on each row of model config consists of three things: a slider generated from this model's window, an exact number box, and a probe window button. - Choosing Auto takes this model's own nominal window ×0.8 instead of snapping to the public levels. So a local model with only an 8K window gets 6553, rather than not even filling the lowest level and occupying the whole window with no room left to write the reply. - The number box keeps the exact value you enter (131072 is 131072), clamped only within the 1K–4M range; when the chosen value exceeds this model's window it is capped automatically, and the readout shows both the value you chose and the value actually in effect. - Local and custom endpoints (Ollama / llama.cpp / vLLM / OpenAI-compatible) can click probe window to ask that server directly how much it can actually take. The probed value is kept in a separate override table, participates in the auto level and the cap, can be cleared with one click at any time to return to the endpoint's own value, and when nothing can be probed it says plainly \"this endpoint does not expose a context window\" instead of making a number up for you. - Only for a model with neither a nominal window nor a probe result does the auto level fall back to the global \"Context Token limit\" (changed in Settings → Context & Projects ). The threshold that triggers automatic compression and the context bar in the interface are both computed from the same number, so you never get the mismatch of \"the bar is full but nothing compresses\". The context bar can be clicked directly; one click jumps to that model's row. How to choose the thinking effort The level capsule on the left of the input box controls \"how long this session lets the model think\" (Auto / Off / Low / Medium / High), stored separately for each Chat. The scale isn't a fixed table — SLATE sorts endpoints into nine categories by capability, and the popup only offers the levels this model can genuinely accept: - Models that force thinking have no \"Off\" level; the lowest is \"Low\". - Endpoints that can only be switched on or off as a whole, without level adjustment, are annotated next to the scale with \"Low / Medium / High all count as on\". - Levels a model doesn't support are greyed out with the reason stated; the popup says so the moment you switch to that model. - If upstream rejects this field with a 400 naming it, SLATE strips it and resends once — the worst case is that the level doesn't take effect this time, not that the model is unusable. - The small text below a level (Free ink / Clear ink / Light ink / Rich ink / Charred ink) is only a density hint; dragging only previews, and it takes effect and is saved when you let go. Responses API mode There is a toggle in \"Model config\": Enable Responses API mode . It sends requests in the newer API format, and only some models support it. - Off by default; sending requests in the existing format works fine. - Turn it on only when you know for certain that your endpoint supports the Responses API and you need its capabilities. - If unsure, don't turn it on; turned on wrongly, it usually shows up as the request being rejected upstream. Division of labor between lightweight and heavy models Models at different tiers can each do their part, saving money without sacrificing the quality of the conclusions. Scenario Division of labor --- --- AI Team debate Lightweight models handle the discussion and the speaking, heavy models make the final decision AI-assisted features A dozen-plus items such as compression summaries, memory distillation, code understanding and command explanation, each individually switchable and individually assignable to a model Auto-advance review Assign the review model separately in the \"Auto-advance\" block These toggles and assignment entry points are all in Settings; the item-by-item description is in Settings & security approvals. Switching models and usage display - Switching : pick in the model dropdown of the input area; it only affects this session. The last item in the dropdown is \"+ Custom model…\", where you can add one on the spot. - Per-reply usage : every reply is tagged with the model used and the Token usage. - Session usage : the usage info bar below the title bar reflects the session on screen; the Summary tab in the left column gives a summary of a session. - Cumulative usage : Settings → Usage statistics shows the cumulative consumption of all conversations. Several sessions may be running at the same moment; each keeps its own books, and the usage bar only reflects the session in front of you. FAQ I filled in the key but it still errors Go to Settings → API test , select the model in question and click \"Run Test\". This request goes through the same path as a real conversation, so it can tell you directly whether it's \"wrong key\", \"wrong address\", \"insufficient balance\" or \"wrong model name\". The model I want isn't in the dropdown Connect it as a custom endpoint with \"+ Add model\"; there's no need to wait for the list to update. The list shows \"No built-in models\" That means the built-in list couldn't be fetched this time. You can add a custom model by hand as a stand-in first, then check FAQ."},{"t":"Chat & messages","u":"05-chat.html","s":"Start","h":["Starting a Chat","Basic message operations","Editing and deleting messages","Comparing the four ways to input","Attachments and multimodal input","Voice input","Polish prompt","@ mentions","Context and usage","Automatic injection of memory and profile","Historical sessions","Sessions produced by Scheduled Tasks"],"x":"This page covers what you use most in the Chat box: how to start a Chat, how to get a message sent, how to get files and Skills into that one message, and where the Tokens went. Starting a Chat - Click \"＋ New Chat\" at the top of the \"Tasks\" tab in the sidebar, or press Ctrl + N . - Click any session in the sidebar to switch to it; the full history of the current session is laid out again. - The sort dropdown can switch between: Recently updated / By project / By status / Creation time / By usage. The sort preference syncs across devices. - Each session carries a status marker: Needs action (the previous session still has a next step, or was stopped), Error , In progress (breathing animation), Done, unread . Clicking into a session counts as having read it. Enter sends, Shift + Enter inserts a new line. The markers record \"how the previous session ended\": if you later continued it on mobile or in another session, the old marker is voided automatically. Basic message operations - Streaming output : the reply lands character by character, so you can start reading without waiting for the whole thing. - Stop : during generation the send button becomes \"Stop\". Clicking it is a real cancel — the model stream and the streaming channel of tool calls are closed together, and the backend also terminates the running subprocesses and tasks, so you never get \"the interface stopped but the work is still going\". - Regenerate : every assistant reply has \"Regenerate\" below it. It re-requests using the history before this reply, and rewrites everything after this reply. - One-click copy for code blocks : code blocks have a \"Copy\" button at the top right. - Smart scroll following : while the scrollbar is at the bottom the output follows along automatically; when you scroll up to read history you aren't dragged back down. Editing and deleting messages Hovering over a message floats action buttons: - \"Copy\" puts this message's content on the clipboard. - \"Regenerate\" only appears on assistant messages. - \"Edit content\" turns the body into an input box in place, and saving writes back to this message. - \"Delete this message\" asks once first, and deletion cannot be undone. Comparing the four ways to input Input method How to use Best for --- --- --- Text Just type, Enter sends The vast majority of requests Voice Click the microphone button on the right of the input box and dictate When you don't want to type, or want to think out loud as you go Polish prompt Click the star button to the left of the voice button and let the model rewrite the draft When the request is written vaguely and you want clearer wording Attachments Click the paperclip button and choose files Letting the AI read a file you already have @ mention Type @ and pick a candidate, or enter the matching group from the ＋ menu Naming exactly which Tool, Skill or file to use Attachments and multimodal input Click the paperclip button on the left of the input box to add attachments; a single file can be up to 10MB. - Text types ( .md / .html / .css / .js / .py / .json / .yaml / .log and so on): the body is read directly. - Spreadsheet types ( .csv / .tsv ): parsed into structured data instead of one block of text. - Images ( .png / .jpg / .jpeg / .gif / .webp / .bmp / .svg ): converted into multimodal input for the model to look at. - Document types ( .docx / .xlsx / .pdf ): SLATE parses out the paragraphs, tables and body text itself before sending them into the Chat. SLATE parses the file locally into body text or structured data, instead of stuffing the whole raw file into Context. This step happens locally and doesn't cost extra Tokens. Voice input Click the microphone button on the right of the input box once to start dictating, click again to stop. - Based on browser speech recognition, transcribing live as you speak; the transcription lands straight in the input box and doesn't interrupt the draft you already have. - The recognition language follows the interface language: a Chinese interface recognises Chinese, an English interface recognises English. - Requires browser and system support; Chrome / Edge work. The first use needs microphone permission. The button only shows when the current browser supports speech recognition. If it doesn't appear, this environment doesn't support it — type instead. Polish prompt The star button to the left of the microphone button hands the draft already written in the input box to the model to rewrite. It rewrites the message you haven't sent yet — it isn't having the model answer in your place. The flow is: click once → the model rewrites → see the original and the rewrite side by side in a popup → you decide. - Clicking Use it is what writes it back into the input box; then you press Enter to send as usual. - Clicking Keep original , pressing Esc , or clicking outside the popup all count as keeping the original, and not one character in the input box changes. - You can keep typing in the input box during the few seconds of waiting. If you (or voice dictation) change the draft, this rewrite result is discarded and won't overwrite what you just wrote. - When the model wraps its result in a code block, what's taken is the body inside; the pair of backtick fences isn't written back. - Which model is used is set separately here: Settings → AI-assisted features → Prompt polish. Turn it off and clicking the button only says it's off, without sending a single request. The instruction written into the prompt to the model is \"keep the original meaning, don't add goals or details you didn't mention\", and it requires @ mentions, code, paths and numbers to be kept as-is. Rewriting only makes the words clearer; it doesn't swap out the request. @ mentions Typing @ in the input box pops up candidates; you can also click the ＋ menu on the left of the input box and open one category directly from the \"Mention (@ prefix)\" group: - Skill : injects the process defined by SKILL.md into this one message. - Tool : names which built-in Tool to use. - MCP : uses tools provided by an external MCP Server. - File : injects the body (or directory structure) of a project file into this message; available only after a project is open. Process manuals and Expert Packs also appear among the candidates: @ a process id injects the whole process, and @ an Expert Pack name injects its persona, rules and knowledge files. The full list of tools and Skills is in Built-in tools & Skills. Context and usage There is a usage info bar at the top of the Chat area; hovering over it shows who took the Context: Bucket What it holds --- --- System prompt Fixed content such as the role setting and the project constitution Tool catalog Which tools the model can see in this Chat Skill injection Skills and file content brought in by @ mentions Tool calls & results What the model called and what the tools returned Chat messages What you and the model actually said - The denominator of the context bar is the context budget you set for that model, not the model's nominal window size, so it is more like a forecast of \"how far until compression\". - When usage passes the threshold, SLATE automatically compresses earlier conversation into a history summary, keeping the most recent turns as they are. - The nudges the system sends to the model and the tool results fed back (see Agent Autopilot & Target Mode) appear only on the model's side and don't take up the message area; after compression they stay in Context and keep working, and they never suddenly turn into a bubble. - The summary is still sent to the model as full original text, and only folded into one collapsible bar in the message area: click the title to expand, so a long history doesn't push the recent turns off screen. - You can also compress manually: click the compress button on the toolbar, choose Light or Heavy, and start. Level What is kept --- --- Light compression Keeps key details and facts such as file names and numbers that may be referenced, about 50% compression Heavy compression Keeps only core conclusions, key decisions and explicit to-dos, about 80% compression Automatic compression only happens when the threshold is exceeded. If the first half of this session is full of intermediate work — \"reading files, trial and error, back-and-forth confirmation\" — and what you're about to discuss is something else, compressing manually ahead of time pays off more than waiting for it to trigger: the light level keeps file names and numbers, while the heavy level keeps only conclusions and details are lost. Automatic injection of memory and profile Long-term memory and the User Profile are selected and injected into the Chat by SLATE in the background; you don't need to paste them by hand. To view them, add or remove them, or extract memory from the current conversation, see Memory, profile & knowledge. Historical sessions Above the \"Tasks\" tab in the sidebar there is \"Search historical sessions\"; typing a keyword searches full text, a match shows a Context excerpt, and clicking one takes you straight to that session. A new session first takes the first 30 characters of its first message as a placeholder title, and after the first round the model fills in a short name — it only replaces placeholder titles like that; names you changed yourself are left alone (this can be turned off in \"Settings → AI-assisted features\"). The \"Manage\" button on the right enters bulk management, where you can rename, export as Markdown and delete in bulk. Each row's actions also include \"Archive\": it only takes this session off the taskbar, not a single message changes, and to get it back go to \"Settings → Conversation archive\". Backup and export details are in Data, backup & storage. Sessions produced by Scheduled Tasks Scheduled and event tasks, when they finish, automatically land as a standalone session whose title carries a [定时] or [事件] prefix, so you can recognise them directly in the sidebar. To see how tasks are created, see Task list & Scheduled Tasks. When you need the AI to actually change files and run commands, see Agent Autopilot & Target Mode and Grind Mode."},{"t":"Agent Autopilot and Target Mode","u":"06-autopilot.html","s":"Chat & Automation","h":["What Agent Autopilot is","The three run strengths compared","What happens inside the loop","Stop when it's done","Automatic nudging","Continue Autopilot","Ink Flow: what you write is what you see","Stop really cancels","Which files this round changed, laid out when it's done","Target Mode","Six-stage closed loop","Task list","Four ways to exit","Four lines of defence against truncated output","A whole round of thinking, no body text","Cross-project parallelism"],"x":"Whenever your message asks the AI to look at the project, modify files, run commands, troubleshoot a problem or commit code, it enters the Agent loop automatically and comes back when the work is done — no repeated \"continue\" needed. Target Mode is a stricter form of the same loop: six explicit stages and an 80-round hard closed loop. What Agent Autopilot is Autopilot is SLATE's default execution layer; you don't turn it on manually. - It recognises environment-operation requests such as viewing, fixing, running, committing or project files, and enters a multi-round loop on its own. - Ordinary tasks default to at most 24 rounds; full audits, project-level and multi-file tasks default to at most 40 rounds. - The round count is a stop-loss line, not a budget : once the work is delivered and verified item by item the loop should end, rather than \"using up all 24 rounds\". - Every tool call in the chat becomes a card, so you can see which file it read and what command it ran. The three run strengths compared Run strength When to use it Round cap Creates a task list automatically How it exits --- --- --- --- --- Normal chat Q&A, writing, explaining code — no need for the AI to touch your files Does not enter the tool loop No Ends when the reply ends Agent Autopilot Fixing one bug, running a test suite once and fixing the failures, generating a batch of files 24 rounds normally; 40 for full audits / project-level work Recommended for complex tasks, not enforced The model closes out explicitly; if it doesn't, marked as interrupted Target Mode Multi-file rewrites, complete audits, long-chain tasks 80 rounds Yes, and it may not claim completion until everything is resolved Manual stop / rounds used up / list fully resolved / model closes out Target Mode is toggled in the + menu on the left of the input box; send a message after turning it on and it takes effect. What happens inside the loop Each round moves roughly to this rhythm: 1. Observe : read the relevant directories, files, configuration or Git state — no guessing from memory. 2. Execute : call tools to move forward; reads and scans that can be combined into one batch are done in the same round. 3. Verify : after a change, re-read, run a command or run tests; keep fixing while it fails. 4. Report : check results, verification method and remaining risks item by item. Tool results are fed back to the model as hidden context so it can carry on, instead of spreading a pile of raw output across your screen. If the tool calls issued in one round are exactly the same as the previous round, the system blocks that duplicate execution and reminds it to change approach — running the same action again only wastes rounds. Stop when it's done Autopilot does not accept \"stopping halfway\": - The model must close out explicitly (you'll see a close-out call) before the work really counts as finished. - If it exits without going through the completion channel (rounds used up, idling, you pressed Stop), the status is honestly marked \"Interrupted · task not confirmed complete\", and the message area offers a \"Keep going\" entry point. - \"Keep going\" doesn't start over; it resumes from the breakpoint. Automatic nudging When the model talks halfway and doesn't act, the system nudges it on its own: - It only states a forward-looking plan — \"I will check / next I'll modify / shall I continue\" — and issues no tool call: nudge it to call tools directly. - It runs no tool yet says \"done\" verbally: require it to verify first, or to actually do the work. - For several rounds in a row it only describes plans without acting: give a firmer warning and, if necessary, cut losses early to avoid burning quota for nothing. These nudges are only added to the context sent to the model and never take up your message area; even if context compaction happens in between, they don't turn into a bubble that pops up. Continue Autopilot Running out of rounds doesn't mean being done. Continue Autopilot is on by default ; you can turn it off in \"Settings → Auto-advance\". - The model is still working in the final round: push the cap out by 8 rounds so it can finish what's in hand. At most 3 extensions; it never runs on indefinitely. - The model stops empty-handed in the final round: read the remaining items of the task list back to it and urge it to keep going, or to reply explicitly with 【Task complete】. If after 3 pushes it still hits the cap and the session hasn't closed out on its own, you don't have to click manually once it ends: as long as the session isn't on a manual approval tier (auto approval / full access / night mode), SLATE presses \"Keep going\" for you. It waits until the session really goes quiet — compaction and memory distillation still occupy it for a few seconds — then sends that \"continue\", at most 2 automatic continuations per session, with an echo reading \"Rounds exhausted · auto-continued (1/2)\". Manual approval tiers are not pressed automatically: the meaning of that tier is that every step needs a human nod, so it only leaves the \"Keep going\" bar in the message area waiting for you. When quota runs out or the automatic continuation doesn't connect, that manual button is still right there, resuming from the breakpoint instead of starting over. Extensions only relax the cap; they don't remove the stop-loss line. If the task itself needs a longer closed loop, turn on Target Mode directly instead of relying on repeated extensions. Ink Flow: what you write is what you see As the model writes tool arguments token by token, Ink Flow infers in real time from the tool's parameter schema which fields are already written and which one is being written, turning the shaping of the arguments into a visible one-line preview. - It only previews : half-formed arguments never trigger execution. - Body-type fields (file contents, Markdown, HTML and so on) are always folded into line and character counts, never flooding the screen. - Tools with very short arguments get no writing animation, to avoid pointless flicker. Stop really cancels Clicking \"Stop\" does more than quiet the interface: - It closes both the model stream and the streaming channel of tool calls at the same time. - The backend then terminates the corresponding child processes and running tasks. - Cancelled calls are accounted for under a separate status, counted apart from \"completed\", so later you can trace whether this one was stopped or finished. Which files this round changed, laid out when it's done When this round really wrote files to disk (edit / create / append), a card is attached to the end of the message after the model's last sentence. It reports facts only and doesn't sing the model's praises. - The header says how many files this round changed and the line delta for each; click \"Review\" to expand the diff file by file (three listed by default, the rest shown on click). - Click \"Undo\" to restore the files this round wrote to how they were before the round started; files created this round are deleted along with them; a red confirmation comes first. - Files you changed yourself after this round are skipped with the reason stated — undo only restores what it wrote itself and doesn't eat your own changes. - No card is left when nothing was written to disk this round; the same goes for auto-apply being off: that write is still recorded in the list of the round that proposed it, but it only really lands on disk after you click \"Accept\". - Original snapshots are stored in data/round snapshots/ and kept for only 7 days; large files over 2MB aren't stored and are skipped with an explanation on undo. Everything stays on your machine and is never uploaded. - On mobile the list is shown only; undo stays on desktop. Target Mode Target Mode is the explicit hard closed-loop mode: you give the goal, and the model advances through six stages on its own. Six-stage closed loop Goal → Plan → Execute → Verify → Report → Trace. - Every tool result in a round is tagged with the current round (round x/N), so the model can pace what's left. - Stop only interrupts the current round; the Target Mode toggle stays on and standing by, ready to keep running at any time. Task list - Multi-file, multi-step, audit or implementation tasks get a task list automatically; simple single-step tasks can skip it. - The list shows live in the right sidebar of the message area; the \"Task list\" button in the top bar collapses it at any time, giving the width back to the message area. - Once collapsed the button still reports {done}/{total} progress; when the session has no list the button is greyed out. - Until everything is resolved the model may not claim completion; status is written back as soon as each batch is finished. Four ways to exit Target Mode has only these four exit paths: 1. You press Stop manually. 2. 80 rounds are used up. 3. The list is fully resolved. 4. The model closes out explicitly and ends the loop. Model-side failures, zero output and duplicate calls all recover and keep going automatically — one exception doesn't cut the whole session short. Turn it on when the task needs a full closed loop of \"change it, then verify it, then go back and patch it\": multi-file refactors, whole-project audits, long tasks that need fixing while running. If you're changing one file or asking one question, leave it off — Target Mode spends extra rounds on building and closing out the list. Four lines of defence against truncated output When writing very long files the model may be cut off by the output limit; SLATE uses four layers to make sure the file ends up complete: 1. Breakpoint anchor continuation : take the model's own last output as the anchor and let it continue precisely from the breakpoint (at most 6 rounds). 2. Truncation guard : a tool call that isn't closed is refused, and the model is required to split it and retry rather than write half a file. 3. Segmented completion : create and write the first part, then use the append action to fill in the rest; the card marks that this segment is truncated and waiting to be completed. 4. Prompt prevention : the prompt asks it in advance to write large files in batches. A whole round of thinking, no body text There is another kind of truncation unrelated to files: the thinking process and the body share the same output limit, so when thinking fills it up there's no room left to write the body this round. SLATE reports this kind of ending honestly instead of letting it slip past: - When the stream ends naturally after receiving content but has neither a finish marker nor a finish reason, the round is flagged. - At wrap-up it says separately whether \"thinking filled the output limit\" or \"the upstream cut off early\", and offers a clickable \"Continue\" entry point to resume from the current context. - If the body has content but the stream broke early, it is marked as possibly incomplete. - This notice and the \"Continue\" entry point are given in every mode, not only in auto-advance mode — otherwise, with auto-advance turned off, \"it stopped by itself\" would be the only explanation left. Cross-project parallelism Switching projects no longer interrupts a reply that's being generated: the session you left keeps running in the background, and its progress and outcome land in the task centre in the right column. - Within one project it's serial by default (two sessions editing the same files at once makes it hard to trace who broke what). - Across projects, at most two sessions run at once; adjust it to 1–4 in \"Settings → Multitasking & Projects\". - Sends over the limit aren't swallowed: they go into the queue marked \"Queued\" and pick up automatically as soon as the previous one finishes. - Each session counts its own tokens; the usage bar only reflects the session on screen. For how projects and workspaces are organised, see Projects, workspaces and per-project configuration."},{"t":"Grind Mode","u":"07-grind.html","s":"Chat & Automation","h":["What Grind is","How to start","Three-stage questioning","Ink panel","What the ink brief contains","After the ink brief: three destinations","Session persistence","FAQ","When not to use Grind"],"x":"Grind Mode is for the moment when \"I know I need to do something, but I can't say what\": you throw in a rough idea, the AI asks one key question per round, and it finally delivers a structured task brief (the ink brief) that can go straight to execution. What Grind is In normal chat the cost of an unclear requirement is yours to carry — if you can't say it clearly, the AI can only guess. Grind hands the \"think it through\" work to questioning. - You don't have to write a spec up front; a rough outline will do. - The AI asks one question at a time, offers options when it can, and doesn't make you write an essay. - Questioning has a round cap and won't go on forever; when there's enough information it collects the ink early. - Collecting gives you a task brief: goal, deliverables and acceptance criteria all spelled out, ready to run. How to start Three ways in: 1. Type /grind your idea in the input box, for example /grind build a weekly report generator for the team . 2. Click the + button on the left of the input box and choose \"Grind Mode\" under \"Modes & features\". This opens a new Grind session; if you haven't thought of the content yet, the input box is filled with /grind for you to complete. 3. Do nothing special and just type an abstract task — say \"make a website\". In a new session with no conversation yet, SLATE offers a \"Grind suggestion\": you can choose \"Enter Grind\" to refine it first, or \"Send directly\" to send it as usual. Whichever way you enter Grind, SLATE clears the current chat view and starts a separate Grind session; the one currently generating on screen is interrupted. It only stops that one — tasks running in other projects are unaffected. Three-stage questioning Grind follows a fixed three-stage progression, never more than 10 rounds: Stage What the AI does --- --- Intake Restates the core of your idea in one or two sentences, lists 3–5 gaps that need clearing, then asks only the first one Grind Asks about the next most important gap round by round, one question at a time, offering options where it can, with no small talk and no repeating what's already settled Collect Stops asking, summarises the agreement in one or two sentences, then gives the complete structured ink brief Collecting starts in three cases: - You reply directly with something like 收墨 , \"that's enough\", \"that'll do\" or \"good\". - There is already enough information and the AI decides there's no need to ask more, collecting on its own. - The questioning reaches its round cap, and it collects in that round. Grind's question chain follows your previous answer. Put concrete information in your answers (who uses it, when it's needed, what result counts as passing) and the later questions get sharper; reply only \"whatever\" or \"you decide\" and the AI can only circle in place. Ink panel The Ink panel in the sidebar lays out the results of this grinding session in real time: - The panel sits to the right of the message area, in the same column as the task list. - ✔ marks items that are settled, ✘ marks open unknowns that have no home yet. - The top shows counts: N settled · M open, so you can see at a glance how far the draft still is. - The panel updates as the conversation moves on; you don't need to click refresh. - While questioning is in progress it shows the current round (round x of 10); once in the collecting stage it hints that you can reply 收墨 to get the draft right away. - Once the ink brief exists the panel keeps a summary view of the finished draft, so looking back later doesn't lose it. - The \"Grind Mode\" row in the + menu on the left of the input box follows along too; switch back and one glance tells you where this session stands. What the ink brief contains After collecting, the ink brief consists of seven elements, each answering a specific question: Element Question it answers --- --- Goal How will the world be different once this is done Audience Who will use it, who reads this result Deliverables What has to be handed over in the end, how many, in what form Acceptance criteria Which conditions must be met to count as acceptable Boundaries and limits What is not being done, which hard constraints apply (time, technology, compliance) Suggested path What order is recommended for moving forward Open questions Things not yet settled, left for the execution stage to assume reasonably and note Items that weren't discussed clearly are listed honestly under \"Open questions\"; it won't fill them in for you out of thin air. After the ink brief: three destinations Below the ink brief there are three buttons; pick by what you want to do next: Button What it does When to use it --- --- --- Send to Target Mode Turns the ink brief into a task description and opens a Target Mode session that executes on its own You've thought it through and want to start Post to Whiteboard Places the ink brief as a whiteboard card Still in design: you need to draw dependencies, split modules or discuss with people Save as template Saves it into the knowledge base as a reusable task brief template The same kind of request will come again; next time you take it out, tweak it and use it What each of the three buttons does when clicked: - Send to Target Mode : the Target Mode toggle turns on automatically, the ink brief is organised into a task description and sent, and the model starts executing right away. Items the ink brief left open are handed to the model to assume reasonably, with a requirement that it notes the assumptions. - Post to Whiteboard : generates a card titled \"ink brief · your title\", with the goal from the ink brief as its body, ready for you to carry on drawing connections. - Save as template : saved as a knowledge document, visible in the knowledge center, ready to pull out and reuse at any time. To see how it runs after being sent to Target Mode, see Agent Autopilot and Target Mode; to see how cards on the whiteboard connect, see Whiteboard-style logic chains; for where templates are stored, see Memory, profile and knowledge base. Session persistence Grind state follows the session, not the screen: - Refresh the page, switch to another session and come back, and the Ink panel and questioning progress restore automatically. - The same session doesn't ask everything from the start again because it was reopened. - After collecting, reopen this session and the three buttons are still below the ink brief. FAQ I want to start running halfway through the questions — can I? Yes. Reply 收墨 and the draft appears immediately; the buttons below the ink brief appear with it, and clicking \"Send to Target Mode\" starts execution. Will Grind touch my files? No. Grind only produces a text task brief; it doesn't touch files or run commands. The real work happens after you click \"Send to Target Mode\". Can several Grind sessions run at once? Each session keeps its own Grind state, independently of the others; starting Grind in another session doesn't affect the progress of the previous one. When not to use Grind Grind is for ideas that aren't thought through yet. Skip it in these cases: - The requirement is already clear, and you could even write the acceptance criteria yourself — go straight to Target Mode and execute. - It's a one-off question or a small change — just say it clearly as a normal chat. - You already have a ready-made spec document — sending that document as an attachment is less trouble. Grind itself doesn't touch your files or run commands; it only grinds the idea into text. To actually do the work, click \"Send to Target Mode\" after collecting."},{"t":"Built-in tools and skills","u":"08-tools-skills.html","s":"Chat & Automation","h":["Two kinds of capability, first things first","Where to manage them: the Extensions page","Built-in tools at a glance","Key tools","file_edit: you see the change first","terminal: run commands in a restricted sandbox","bg_task: three ways a background terminal task wakes you","Search, Git and the web","How tools get called","Turning tools on and off","Custom Skills","Connecting external MCP servers","Self-evolving tools (tool factory)"],"x":"This page is the capability list: which tools SLATE ships with, how the model uses them, which ones you can turn off, and how to connect more capabilities with SKILL.md and external MCP servers. Two kinds of capability, first things first - Built-in tools : Python capabilities that ship with SLATE, usable out of the box — no configuration, no installation. Reading and writing files, running commands, generating documents and web search all fall in this group. - Skills : external knowledge and procedures defined by SKILL.md . You write, upload or import them from a local path, to tell the model \"which steps this kind of thing should follow\". Built-in tools are the \"hands\", Skills are the \"way of doing it\". Both can be used by the model on demand, and both can be named explicitly with @ . Where to manage them: the Extensions page The \"Extensions\" page in the top bar is the entry point for managing all capabilities; seven columns on the left each manage one kind, and the numbers and the list come from the same source: Column What it manages --- --- Skills Importing, creating and deleting SKILL.md skills, with two sources, \"GitHub\" and \"Discover plugins\" Tools The list of built-in tools (viewable, not deletable); features you turn off don't even appear in the catalogue Modes The four built-in reply modes (read-only) and creating / editing / deleting custom modes: extra prompts, tool allowlist, reasoning effort and round cap New features Tools the tool factory produced itself: disable, view code, check versions, roll back, undo MCP Connections to external MCP servers and the remote tools they bring Expert Packs A role setting + knowledge base + skills combined; you can create one and import a zip Actions Editing, validating and rolling back data/actions/ .yml process specs The \"Tools / Skills\" and \"MCP Server\" blocks that used to be scattered on the settings page have moved wholesale onto this page; the settings page keeps configuration only and no longer doubles as capability management. Built-in tools at a glance Category Tool Purpose --- --- --- Files & code file tree Browse directories: recursive, filter by wildcard, fast scan Files & code file peek Read file contents: detects several encodings automatically, supports a line range or reading from the end Files & code file create Create a new file Files & code file edit Diff-preview editing: exact replacement, replace by line number, insert, delete, copy and paste Files & code file append Append to the end of an existing file, used to continue very long files in segments Files & code code search Global code search within the project: text or regex, narrowable to a subdirectory Files & code repo stats Statistics on project file types, size and count Files & code todo scan Scan the project for TODO / FIXME markers Files & code python api extract Extract the public API docs of a Python library (signatures, class methods, attributes) Terminal & background tasks terminal Restricted sandbox command execution, with multiple sessions and state kept across commands Terminal & background tasks bg task Background terminal tasks: start and go, the model is woken when it finishes Office documents doc write Generate Markdown technical docs or requirement notes Office documents ppt create Generate .pptx presentations Office documents word create Generate .docx Word documents Office documents excel tool Generate .xlsx , read spreadsheets, convert between csv and xlsx Office documents pdf tool PDF metadata, text and table extraction Office documents html render Generate an HTML skeleton Office documents html bundle Inline a page's css / js into a single html file for easy distribution Data & charts json tool JSON validation, formatting, minifying and reading by path Data & charts regex test Regular expression testing with a preview of the matches Data & charts text summarise Text summarising and keyword extraction Data & charts chart create Generate SVG charts: bar / horizontal bar / line / pie Data & charts qrcode create Generate SVG QR codes Search & web web search Web search, no Key required Search & web web fetch Fetch the body text of a web page, supporting JS-rendered pages and PDFs Code & document security code scan Code security scan: hard-coded keys, SQL injection, XSS, weak crypto, leftover debug code Code & document security doc scan Document security scan: PII, credentials, financial data, confidential markers, internal network information Git git tool Read-only Git repository information: branch status, commit log, change statistics, branch and remote lists System & environment system info System self-knowledge: date and time, hardware configuration, battery, network status System & environment css colour Generate CSS colour schemes Automation browser automation Browser automation: navigate, screenshot, click, type, run JS Automation computer use Desktop automation: mouse and keyboard control, window management, image targeting Automation screenshot to code Screenshot to code: analyse a screenshot and generate HTML / CSS that reproduces the look Generation image gen AI image generation; requires a model and API Key configured in Settings Generation video gen AI video generation; requires a model and API Key configured in Settings MCP factory mcp factory Tool factory: generate new tools from a description; output lands in data/evolved/ and can be disabled / rolled back / undone under \"Extensions → New features\" The table above has 36 entries and covers every built-in tool you can see in the tool panel and in the @ mention candidates. file append and file create belong to the same family: one creates and one continues, and together they handle segmented writes of very long files. Key tools file edit: you see the change first - Changes are presented as a diff: file name, +N -M line statistics, red and green added and removed lines — what moved is visible in one screen. - The card has three buttons, \"Accept\", \"Reject\" and \"Copy diff\": only accepting writes to disk, while rejecting just closes the card (this change isn't written, but content already written earlier in the same round isn't rolled back by this card). - When \"Auto-confirm file creation/modification\" is ticked in \"Settings → Context & Projects\" (ticked by default), changes whose preview reports no errors are written directly, the card shows \"Applied automatically\", and Accept and Reject are no longer offered. When part of the content doesn't match, manual confirmation is kept, so that only half a set never gets written. - Files that really landed on disk this round are summarised into a \"Which files this round changed\" card after the model's last sentence, where you can review the diff file by file and undo the whole round; see Agent Autopilot and Target Mode. - Encoding safety: existing encodings such as UTF-8, BOM, GB18030, GBK and UTF-16 are preserved on write, so Chinese text and emoji don't turn into mojibake. terminal: run commands in a restricted sandbox - A single command times out after 30 seconds, output over 50,000 characters is truncated, and command length is capped too. - Several terminal sessions can coexist: create, list, close, and also terminate processes running in a session. - On Windows every command gets its own PowerShell process: multi-line blocks ( foreach / if ) and && / both work; session state such as cd and $env: persists across commands. - Interactive command lines (a bare python / node REPL waiting for input) get no input and either exit or drag on until timeout, so they aren't suitable here. - No command window pops up on screen, and output capture for native commands supports Chinese and emoji. bg task: three ways a background terminal task wakes you Work measured in minutes — compiling, training, starting an experiment server — hands over to it, without holding the conversation: 1. Ask on demand : it returns the task id, the first stretch of output and the log path right away; afterwards use status and log queries to fetch new output. 2. Wake on finish : start the task with notify=true , and when the task ends the result comes back to the conversation with the tail of the output, and the model is woken to carry on. 3. Wake early : attach a regex, and a matching output wakes it early, without waiting for the command to finish. It is a one-shot \"start and go\" task and doesn't accept interactive input; you can give it a timeout so it terminates itself when due, and stop the whole process tree at any time. Task lifetime equals SLATE process lifetime — close SLATE and the task is gone; logs stay on your machine for later inspection. Background tasks go through exactly the same approval tier as the terminal: high-risk commands still pop up to ask you, and catastrophic commands are refused outright. Search, Git and the web - code search : global code search in the project, supporting text and regex, starting from the project root by default, narrowable to a subdirectory. - git tool : read-only — branch and working tree status, recent commits, change statistics, branch and remote lists. It won't commit, push or do other write operations for you; those commands are run by the model in the terminal and are subject to approval too. - web search : web search without a Key, two engines queried concurrently with results merged and de-duplicated, for real-time information. - web fetch : fetch the title, description and body text of a given page, supporting pages that need JS rendering and PDFs. How tools get called - The model calls them on its own : you just describe the need (\"turn this data into a chart\") and the model decides which tool to use; no naming required. - Visible in the chat : every call becomes a tool card showing the tool name, key arguments and execution status, with the result expandable. - High-risk commands need approval : commands and web access both pass through the approval gate. There are four approval tiers — manual approval / auto approval / full access / night mode — switchable at any time within the session; full access closes the command and web gates, but the AI still pops a multiple-choice question at you when it lacks a condition; night mode goes one step further, fully managed — commands, web access and the model's clarifying questions all stop popping windows. Catastrophic commands are blocked unconditionally in every tier. For the rules, see Settings and security approval. - Failures are reported honestly : when a capability can't run (say the runtime is missing a dependency), the tool card shows a failure, and what the model receives is a failure message too — it tells you what's missing and that you need to install it yourself; it won't describe a file it never generated as generated. Installing dependencies is your job in the terminal; the model won't install things on your behalf. - Background tasks have their own panel : running background terminal tasks are listed in the task centre in the right column, where you can view and stop them directly. Turning tools on and off \"Settings → AI assistance\" is the switchboard for every token-consuming feature outside the chat. Three of them are tool-related: - Subagent parallelism ( subagent run ) - AI image generation - AI video generation Turn any one of these three off and the corresponding tool disappears from the tool catalogue — the model can't see it, so it won't call it. Image and video generation also need a model and API Key configured separately in the \"Image generation\" and \"Video generation\" sections. Custom Skills SKILL.md is plug and play. The entry point is \"Extensions\" → \"Skills\" in the top bar; that column holds skills only, while built-in tools sit in the \"Tools\" column next to it, read-only and not deletable. How to write a Skill: - One SKILL.md per directory, and the directory name is the skill name. - The file states the purpose of the skill (the description: line) and the steps, conventions and caveats to follow. - Besides SKILL.md , the same directory can hold any reference documents it needs. Adding and deleting: - \"New skill\" creates one by uploading a file; \"Import skill\" imports from a local path (a directory containing SKILL.md , or a single .md file). - \"GitHub\" imports from a repository; \"Discover plugins\" scans the standard Codex / Claude plugin directories installed on this machine. - Skills can be deleted; once deleted, neither the model nor @ mentions see them any more; built-in tools are unaffected. Connecting external MCP servers In \"Extensions\" → \"MCP\" in the top bar, click \"+ Add MCP Server\" and fill in the name and address to connect external MCP tools. The list shows the connection status, the number of tools it provides and the tool names, and offers connect, disconnect and delete. Once connected, its tools appear in the tool catalogue and in the @ mention candidates, and the model can call them the way it calls built-in tools. Project-level overrides : after opening a project, each server in the list gains an extra project button, where you can \"disable in this project\", \"enable separately in this project\" or \"remove this project's mask\", and can also allow only a few of its tools. Overrides like these affect the current project only; other projects stay as they were; the server's address and key always stay in the global configuration and are never written into your project directory. See Projects, workspaces and per-project configuration. To specify exactly which tool or skill to use, type @ in the input box and pick from the candidates; on send, its definition is injected into that message. For the scope and usage of @ candidates, see Chat and messages. Self-evolving tools (tool factory) mcp factory lets SLATE write the tools it lacks for its own use. Output always lands in the user data area data/evolved/ , two files per tool: <name .py is the generated code and <name .json is the description and parameter list. - Why not write into backend/skills/ : that's the program's own source tree — one upgrade wipes out every self-made tool you've accumulated, and a program shouldn't modify itself at runtime. data/ isn't touched by upgrades, and the list is read as usual after a restart. - Old output is moved over automatically : generated files that historical versions wrote into the source directory are copied into data/evolved/ at startup (copy only; files in the program's own directory aren't deleted). - Where to manage them : \"Extensions\" → \"New features\" in the top bar. Each entry states its current status: normal / disabled / code has a problem (with the syntax error line number) / same name as a built-in tool (already taken over by the built-in). - Disabling removes the whole entry , so it disappears from the model's tool system rather than \"reporting an error at call time\"; you can enable it again at any time. - Overwrite, undo and rollback all back up first to data/evolved/.history/ (at most 5 versions per tool). \"Version history\" in the entry dialog lets you view the original text of any version and roll back; rollback is itself a write, and an older version that doesn't compile isn't allowed to overwrite. - Generated code that doesn't compile never lands on disk at all : the factory only does a compile self-check and never runs it once for you; a create request with the same name as a built-in tool is refused on the spot. - On a name clash the built-in wins : if an upgrade brings a built-in tool with the same name, that self-made code is bumped and marked in the column — visible, undoable at any time, no need to fight the new version. Self-made tools are written by the model, and their capabilities and side effects don't go through the audit that built-in tools get. Before using one, you can open it in the \"New features\" column to read the code; if you're not comfortable, disable it or undo it — the backup is still there, ready to roll back at any time."},{"t":"Actions process specs","u":"09-actions.html","s":"Chat & Automation","h":["What Actions are","How it differs from a Skill","Where the files live","How to write the fields","A minimal example to copy","Hard constraints on the writing format","How the model uses it","Sending a process into the chat manually","Running one on the whiteboard","Editing on the settings page","Letting the model write it for you","Backup and rollback","Per-project overrides","Broken files don't vanish into thin air"],"x":"By the second time you do the same thing you're restating the order, the conventions and the pitfalls you already hit. Actions is the cure: write \"the necessary process for doing something\" down ahead of time as a spec, and the model reads it when needed and follows it — you don't have to brief it every time. What Actions are An Action is a process spec. It states three things: when this thing should be done, in what order it should be done, and what counts as passing. Once written, in agent mode the model first sees an Action catalogue (each entry only has an id, name, one-line description and when it applies); when it judges that this task matches a spec, it reads the full text and actually executes the steps. You define the process; the model is only responsible for following it. How it differs from a Skill Both live in the local data directory and are managed in the same place, but they answer different questions. Aspect Action process spec Skill --- --- --- Nature An executable agreement on steps Knowledge and role Question it answers In what order this should be done How this kind of thing is done, and in whose identity Content form Ordered steps, each may note a suggested tool and a checkpoint A description document, SKILL.md How it's used The model searches the catalogue → reads the full text → executes the steps Once written as a Skill, an @ mention in chat injects it into context Limits Follows a strict writing format; a broken file reports the line number Document form, free writing In one line: a Skill makes the model \"know\", an Action makes the model \"act in your order\". The two can be used at the same time. For the complete list of built-in tools and skills, see Built-in tools and skills. Where the files live One file per process; the file name without .yml is its id (for example, the id of weekly report.yml is weekly report ). An id may only contain a-z0-9 - starting with a lowercase letter, at most 48 characters, and doesn't need to be written inside the file again. - The global one: shared by every project on this machine. - The project one: put it in the current project's .slate/actions/ ; on a name clash it overrides the global one. If you're not sure where to put it, start with global and override it in the project when needed. How to write the fields A spec consists of the following fields, all written by people and read by the model: Field What to write What the model judges from it --- --- --- name Process name, at most 40 characters Recognising it in the catalogue description One-line description, at most 120 characters Whether this spec is worth opening to read in full when When it should apply, at most 200 characters Whether this request should follow this process inputs Inputs to collect from you before starting, at most 8 What to ask you first, then what to do next steps Step list, 1 to 24 steps In what order, which tool to call, what counts as passing output Output requirements: format and destination What is finally delivered, and where it goes tags Tags, at most 8, each at most 24 characters Keywords matched during retrieval Each inputs item can have: Key Description --- --- key Name of the input item, starts with a lowercase letter, only a-z0-9 allowed label The name shown to people; key is used if omitted type One of text / number / textarea / select ; text if omitted required Only true or false options Needed for select only, at most 20 items Each steps item can have: Key Description --- --- title What this step does, required, at most 60 characters tool Suggested tool name, e.g. git tool , doc write detail Specific requirements for this step; multi-line text uses a literal block check Checkpoint for this step, at most 200 characters In output , format states the format of the produced file or body text; destination may only be message (stays in the chat), file (write a file) or board (back to the whiteboard); when it is file you must also give path . A minimal example to copy Save this file as weekly report.yml and it appears in the Action catalogue. Hard constraints on the writing format Actions use a trimmed YAML subset that only supports what you actually need. Syntax outside that range isn't \"guessed at\" — it's refused outright, and you're told which line it's on. - Indentation is fixed at 2 spaces, with at most two levels of nesting. Tab indentation is forbidden. - Arrays use block style: one - per line, and the - at the same level must line up. - Multi-line text uses or - literal blocks, with consistent indentation inside the block. - Booleans accept only true / false . - Anchors and aliases ( & , ), multiple document separators ( --- ), folded scalars ( ), flow style ( [] , {} ) and unclosed quotes are not accepted. - A single file is capped at 64 KB; all detail text together may not exceed 20000 characters. A validation error reads \"Line N: reason\"; fix it and save again. The content of an Action goes into the model's system prompt for the long term, which amounts to putting the Key right in front of the model. When you need credentials, write \"reference an environment variable or file location\", not plain text. The panel warns about suspected credentials but won't stop them for you. How the model uses it In agent mode the model first receives an Action catalogue listing at most 20 entries; beyond that it's told only the count, plus to use Action retrieval to see them all. Its typical behaviour is three steps: retrieve → read the full text → execute the steps. In chat mode no tools are available, and the whole catalogue isn't injected. One point worth stressing: reading a process is not the same as having done it . Where a step names a suggested tool, the model still has to actually call a tool to complete it; it won't claim the work is done just because it \"read this file\". Sending a process into the chat manually If you don't want to wait for the model to retrieve on its own, you can name it directly: - Type @ in the input box and pick an Action from the candidates; the whole process is injected into that message. - Injection is capped at 6000 characters; anything beyond is truncated, with a hint for the model to use the read tool to fetch the whole thing. - Mentions use the id (file name); the display name and tags serve only as search aliases. The use case is when you already know which process to use and just want it followed strictly this one time. Running one on the whiteboard Open the board → \"Workflow\" display mode, and every Action becomes a card on the card wall, with the card face saying how many steps, how many inputs, who wrote it and where the output goes. \"Run this\" on the card sends it into the current chat as an @ mention, \"View steps\" expands the step list in place, and \"Pin\" puts frequently used ones first. The workflow view also shows how far this run has got; for details see Whiteboard-style logic chains. Editing on the settings page \"Extensions\" → \"Actions\" in the top bar: click \"+ New Action\", or click an existing entry to open the editor. The editor validates as you write, and errors point out the line and the reason; you can't save until validation passes. The top of the editor states which one you're editing: the global one, or a project's override. Letting the model write it for you You can also just have the model write the process down — on condition that it declares author: model as required, and the panel marks the file as \"model-written\" accordingly. In \"Ask\" permission mode a dialog shows you the original text before writing, and it only lands on disk when you click allow; switch to \"Auto\" or \"Bypass\" and the dialog no longer appears. In every tier the format check runs before writing, so content that doesn't pass never gets in. Pick a process you've already explained to the model twice or more. Write it once, and from then on it reads it and follows it itself; what you save is every future restatement. Backup and rollback Before every overwrite or deletion, SLATE backs up the original text first, keeping at most 5 versions per file. \"Version history\" in the editor lets you view any version and roll back in one click; a rollback is itself a write and is validated before it lands. Backups follow the destination: the project copy's history stays in the project's own directory and never mixes with the global one. Per-project overrides Place a spec with the same id in an open project ( <project-root /.slate/actions/<id .yml ) and it overrides the global one, while the other processes stay visible as before — processes don't \"all disappear the moment you enter a project\". The list marks which entry is the \"this project\" copy, and the editor states which one you're editing. Delete the project copy and the process with the same name falls back to the global one automatically — this isn't deleting the process outright. For how project and global relate, see Projects, workspaces and per-project configuration. Broken files don't vanish into thin air Break a file and it isn't silently ignored: the Actions list on the settings page still lists it along with \"Line N: reason\"; the tool return the model gets also tells it which ones are temporarily unavailable; and the card wall in the workflow view indicates how many specs failed to parse. When you see a notice like that, fix it back using the line number."},{"t":"Expert Packs","u":"10-experts.html","s":"Chat & Automation","h":["What an Expert Pack is","The five-part structure","Building an Expert Pack from scratch","How to write rules that work","Import and export","Built-in sample: 创意写作导师 (Creative Writing Mentor)","The difference between the three injection routes","When to use an Expert Pack","Maintenance and updates","Things to watch"],"x":"Every time you change scenario you have to explain again \"who you are, what rules you follow, which material you reference\". Expert Packs put all of that into one folder: install once, attach it when needed, and the model works in this expert's identity and tone. What an Expert Pack is An Expert Pack is a distributable role configuration: how a person thinks, what rules they keep, what they know and which methods they use, all packed into one folder. It can live only on your machine, or be zipped up and shared with colleagues. Once loaded it isn't written into the chat history; it's injected as part of the system prompt — so it survives switching sessions and switching models, until you take it off. The five-part structure An Expert Pack consists of five parts: Part Role What to write --- --- --- persona.md Persona: who this expert is Identity and experience, what they're good at solving, how they speak and think rules.md Rules: what must be followed Judgement conventions, output format requirements, things never allowed knowledge/ Knowledge: the material they hold Excerpts of material they'll really use, one topic per file skills/ Skills: the methods they know Step-by-step methodology, checklists, templates data.json Metadata Name, one-line description, version, author knowledge/ and skills/ are both folders and can hold several files; the file name is its label. persona.md decides \"whether it feels like that person\", and rules.md decides \"whether it's stable\". If you only write one, write rules.md first — the persona gives the expression flavour, but the rules are what make it deliver every time. Building an Expert Pack from scratch In the Expert Pack management panel click \"New\", then fill in, in order: 1. Write the name and description : the name is what it shows in each dropdown, and the description is a one-line explanation; both are exported along with the pack. 2. Write the persona (Persona tab): who this expert is, what they're good at, what tone they speak in. Short sentences, concrete. 3. Write the rules (Rules tab): what it must do, what it must never do, what format to output. 4. Add knowledge files (Knowledge tab): upload them one by one; the file name is its label, e.g. \"judgement-conventions.md\", \"common-pitfalls.md\". 5. Add skill documents (Skills tab): put in the methodology, checklists and templates you use often. 6. Save : it takes effect as soon as you save; to give it to someone else, click \"Export\" and save it as a zip. How to write rules that work Rules aren't slogans; they're executable constraints. Rather than \"please be professional\", write clearly \"what must be done, what must not be done, what the output looks like\". A usable set of rules looks roughly like this: Use it as a reference: the more specific the entries and the more they read like a checklist, the more consistently the model performs. Import and export Export is a zip, and import uses the same format, so it can go straight to a colleague. - An imported pack must contain the three files persona.md , rules.md and data.json ; these three may sit inside a single root directory within the zip. - A pack is capped at 50 MB uncompressed and at most 500 files. - What you import is a copy on your machine; changing it doesn't affect the original in someone else's hands. In the Expert Pack management panel, click \"Import\" to choose a zip, and \"Export\" to save the current one as a zip. SLATE ships with a sample pack, \"创意写作导师\" (Creative Writing Mentor), ready to use once installed. Have one conversation with it first to see what persona and rule injection looks like, then break down its way of writing to build your own expert. Built-in sample: 创意写作导师 (Creative Writing Mentor) The sample pack is named \"创意写作导师\" (Creative Writing Mentor), positioned as \"a narrative expert who helps you polish inspiration into a story: structural diagnosis, style advice and concrete rewrite examples\". Its five parts include two knowledge files and one skill document, ready to use as a template: swap persona.md for your role, rules.md for your conventions, and the knowledge files for the material you hold. The difference between the three injection routes The same Expert Pack can be attached from three places, with completely different scope: Injection route Where to pick it Scope What is injected --- --- --- --- The expert dropdown in the chat input area Left of the chat input area Takes effect for the whole conversation Persona + rules + the list of knowledge file names The team member card The \"Expert Pack\" dropdown on each member in team mode Takes effect only for this role's speech Persona + rules Chat @ mention Type @ in the input box and pick an Expert Pack Takes effect only for this one message Persona + rules + the full text of the first 6 knowledge files (up to 4000 characters each) The key difference is in the last two columns: the first two routes give only the names of the knowledge files — the model knows \"this file exists\" but may not be able to read the content; only the @ mention route stuffs the knowledge text into the message, at the cost of a noticeably larger token count for that message. So the division of labour can be: for a stable expert identity, attach it with the dropdown; for it to really open a piece of material this time, use an @ mention. When to use an Expert Pack - Across many sessions, doing the same kind of thing repeatedly : long-term technical design reviews, long-term external copywriting, for example. - With clear conventions and taboos : writing these into rules.md is more reliable than stressing them verbally every time. - To share with others : one zip is the whole thing; whoever receives it imports it and reproduces it. Conversely, if you just want its tone to shift this one time, saying so clearly in the message is enough; there's no need to build a pack for it. Maintenance and updates - Changes take effect on save : after saving in the panel, if this pack is attached to this conversation, the injected content updates in step — no need to pick it again. - Files can be added and removed : knowledge files and skill documents can be uploaded and deleted at any time; deleted ones disappear from the list. - Deletion is irreversible : deleting an Expert Pack asks for one confirmation, and after that it can't be undone; to keep a copy, export a zip first. - A backup is recommended : hand-written rules and organised knowledge material are all hard work, and the exported zip is the simplest backup. Things to watch - The bigger the knowledge files, the more tokens they cost . An @ mention reads at most 6 files, each truncated to 4000 characters; the more you load in, the higher the fixed overhead on every message. - Include only what you will really use : one \"common judgement conventions\" file is more useful than a \"complete collection of material\". - Rules and persona should be short and specific. Written as long prose, the model loses the point instead; short sentences and lists stated clearly make it perform more consistently. - Keep one pack to one kind of role. When you need several roles, make several packs and attach them separately in team mode — that's clearer (see AI Team and Workflows). An Expert Pack attached to a conversation takes effect throughout. If you find its conventions don't suit the current task, switch back to \"No expert\" in the dropdown right away; don't force it in the same conversation — with two sets of conventions mixed together, the model is pulled both ways."},{"t":"AI Team and Workflows","u":"11-team.html","s":"Collaboration & Reasoning","h":["What AI Team solves","How the debate runs","The six speech actions","Roles and the model split","Built-in team presets","Custom teams","The decision waits for your sign-off","Discussion summary and close","Stopping midway","Failure and absence are not speech","Switch away and find it again","Where the discussion record goes","Team Workflow: hand the pipeline to a DAG","Built-in workflow templates","Importing, exporting and deleting workflows"],"x":"What one person — even a model — fails to think of is often caught by a role arguing from another position. Team mode puts several roles into a multi-round debate on the same question and ends with a summary that carries a signed-off conclusion; Workflow mode splits a long task into nodes with dependencies, and whatever can run in parallel runs in parallel. What AI Team solves Its value is not \"a few more voices\" but adversarial pressure : one role is pragmatic, one is inventive, one calls it, and the views push against each other — so what you get back is no longer a first-reaction answer, but one that has been challenged once. Good topics: choosing between approaches, technology selection, risk review, product direction, decisions where both sides need to be seen. How the debate runs In the Chat workspace, switch the mode on the left of the title bar from \"Chat\" to \"Team\" to enter the Team Discussion view: 1. Pick a team preset, or configure the members yourself. 2. Write the question to discuss in the topic input. 3. Choose the number of rounds (default 5; 3 / 5 / 8 / 10 can be selected). 4. Click \"Start discussion\"; members speak in order. In the first round each member makes their own proposal. From then on, every round the members can see all the speech so far and choose to propose, support, oppose, rebut or add, and can also address a named member. The whole process unfolds line by line, round by round, so you can always see who said what. The six speech actions Every speech begins with an action marker; the system classifies speech by it, and the speech counts in the summary come from it too: Action Meaning --- --- 【提案】 Proposes a new idea or plan 【支持】 Agrees with and reinforces an existing view 【反对】 Explicitly disagrees with a view 【反驳】 Rebuts the reasoning behind a view 【补充】 Adds what was missed in the same direction 【决策】 Gives the final plan; the discussion ends there To reply to a specific person, write @member name after the action, for example 【反驳】@性能专家 这个结论忽略了冷启动成本。 The system records it as a reply edge pointing at that person. Every speech is required to be short (one to three sentences): it should read like a debate, not a paper, and it saves tokens. Roles and the model split By default, three members map to three stances: Role Stance Responsibility --- --- --- Analyst Pragmatic Feasibility and cost Creative Inventive Possibility and user experience Decider Synthesis Collects the views and gives the final plan with its reasoning Only the decider can call it: even if another member writes 【决策】, it is treated as an ordinary proposal. The decider can give a 【决策】 in any round to close the discussion; if the rounds run out with no consensus, the decider adds one final decision round. The model split is deliberate: lightweight models in the discussion seats, a heavyweight model in the decision seat . The discussion phase speaks often but briefly, so a lightweight model is enough; the final plan is produced only once, and is worth the stronger model. In every round each member reads all the speech so far. Rounds times members — the cost multiplies. Three to five rounds is usually enough to see where the disagreement lies; keep the number of roles to what you really need. Built-in team presets SLATE ships 9 presets; selecting one replaces the current member list (members can still be adjusted individually afterwards): Preset Members --- --- Classic trio Analyst + Creative + Decider Code review team Security expert + Performance expert + Architect Product brainstorm Product manager + Designer + User advocate + Tech lead Technology selection Frontend expert + Backend expert + Infrastructure expert + Technical director Red vs blue Pro debater + Con debater + Judge Full-stack review Frontend reviewer + Backend reviewer + Database reviewer + Ops reviewer + Technical director Writing workshop Content planner + Copywriter + Editor Startup advisory board Marketing advisor + Technology advisor + Finance advisor + Operations advisor + CEO Academic seminar Methodology expert + Empirical researcher + Critic + Moderator Custom teams Treat a preset as a starting point and edit the member cards one by one: - Name : change the name on the card directly; the debate record uses that name (and @ replies recognise it). - Model : pick a model on each card, grouped by \"foreign / domestic / local / custom\". Models with no key configured are marked with a hint. - Persona : the role-setting text on the card is this member's stance and style; changing it redefines the role. - Expert Packs : each card can take one Expert Pack, binding a persona and rules to that role, see Expert Packs. - Add / remove : \"＋\" at the top adds a member, \"×\" at the card's top right removes one. Members you add yourself are ordinary members by default and do not take part in calling it. To make a member responsible for calling it, use the decider seat from a preset, or change a member to the decider seat and then adjust who fills it. The decision waits for your sign-off \"Decisions need my sign-off\" is on by default: after the decider gives a 【决策】, the discussion does not close by itself — it stops and waits for your signature. - Sign off : ends with that decision, and the summary is generated as usual. - Keep discussing , or closing the dialog without choosing: counts as \"not signed\"; the discussion carries on with \"unsigned\" for another round, and the next member's prompt states that this decision has not been signed off. - Reopening is capped; once the cap is used up the discussion closes itself — every reopen is another round of everyone speaking, and it is not given an opening to burn indefinitely. The sign-off line is a separate record within the discussion: it enters the next member's Context and the backend record, but it does not count as any member's speech, and the star map does not count it towards anyone's viewpoint. In night mode (full autopilot) there is no dialog to interrupt; the decision is adopted directly, but that line is still drawn and states that it was adopted automatically — it never reads as if you had clicked through. If you don't want to be stopped, clear that checkbox in the Team panel to go back to \"the decision ends it\". Discussion summary and close When the discussion ends, a \"debate summary\" is generated automatically, containing: - The topic and the list of participating members. - Speech counts: how many in total, and how many fall under propose / support / oppose / rebut / add / decide. - The final plan and who proposed it; if no decision was ever formed, it says plainly \"no clear decision reached\". Beyond the summary, every line of the debate stays in the discussion area and can be scrolled back through. Stopping midway Click \"Stop discussion\" to interrupt it. Speech already completed is kept as is ; stopping never throws away what the debate has already produced. A session stopped by hand generates no summary — with no conclusion, it does not make one up; but it still goes into team history, marked \"interrupted\", and opening it shows the rounds that did finish. Failure and absence are not speech If a member's request fails this round, or the model they are bound to has no API Key configured, that cell is marked plainly \"failed\" / \"did not participate\": - That error line does not enter the next member's Context — otherwise the model would take an error message as someone's claim and rebut it. - It is not counted in the speech statistics; that member's speech count in the star map is 0. - The backend still keeps a line with error / skipped, so you can trace afterwards who did not speak in which round; these two states can only be marked by the system — a 【失败】 written by a model does not parse. Switch away and find it again The debate keeps running while you are switched to the Chat panel. Waiting for your signature and the closing of this session both go into the task centre on the right, in the same table as background terminal tasks and subagent batches: the row carries the topic, is marked \"waiting for your sign-off\" while it waits, the team button on the row jumps back to this session to review it, and it is this session that the stop button interrupts. Where the discussion record goes Every speech lands in two places at once: - Local history : the \"Team history\" list on the left keeps the last 50 sessions, listing the time, number of speeches and approximate token usage; click one to review it. - Backend team session : one discussion corresponds to one session record, one member roster, and one event ledger record for each member. The backend copy is the data source for the \"team star map\" in the Whiteboard · workflow view: it lays out the most recent discussion by role — who replied to whom, who touched which Tools, who spawned subagents — all taken from real records. If the backend write fails, local history is still there and that list entry is marked \"local record only\"; when this conversation has no team record at all, the star map says so directly rather than drawing a decorative graph. For how to read the star map, see Whiteboard logic chains. Team Workflow: hand the pipeline to a DAG Debate is thinking together; Workflow is working separately. Switch to the \"Workflow\" tab of the Team panel: 1. Pick a Workflow template. 2. Fill in the task requirements below; they are passed to each node as $input . 3. Click \"Run workflow\". Nodes are chained by dependency edges: an upstream output can serve as a downstream input (referenced as $node id ), and nodes with no dependency between them execute at the same time . Once a node runs, its state is echoed live — waiting, running, succeeded, failed, skipped; if a node fails or is skipped, everything downstream of it is skipped automatically rather than forced to run with empty input. You can stop at any time during a run; nodes that have not started are marked skipped. A node's model binding is resolved in this priority order: a bound team member role (inheriting its model and persona) → a model the node specifies itself → the model set for the \"Workflow\" slot under \"Settings → AI-assisted features\" → the current model. When the whole pipeline has run, the output is archived into the knowledge base automatically under a title like \"Workflow output · template name\", viewable in the Memory panel. Built-in workflow templates SLATE ships 8 templates: Template Node pipeline --- --- Standard development Requirements analysis → Task breakdown → Implementation → Code review → Summary & handoff Parallel research & analysis Requirements analysis → Technical research / Risk assessment / Resource assessment → Summary decision Code review Change scan → Quality review / Security review / Performance review → Summary report Document generation Outline planning → Drafting → Summary extraction → Delivery assembly Data analysis Data understanding → Trend analysis / Anomaly detection → Statistical summary → Decision recommendations Research report Topic definition → Background research → Option comparison / Feasibility analysis → Report generation Product requirements Idea sorting → User stories → Feature breakdown → Prioritization → Requirements document Bug investigation Bug description → Log analysis / Code analysis / Environment analysis → Root cause Importing, exporting and deleting workflows Workflow templates are provided as JSON definition files containing nodes, dependency edges and each node's prompt. \"Standard development\" and \"Parallel research & analysis\" are built-in and cannot be deleted; other custom templates can be imported, exported and deleted. Deletion only affects the template — output that has already been produced stays in the knowledge base."},{"t":"Whiteboard logic chains","u":"12-whiteboard.html","s":"Collaboration & Reasoning","h":["What the Whiteboard is for","Basic operations","Card colour is its category","Links with Chat, Team and Ink","Live Mermaid rendering","Six display modes","Git tree view","Tool step cards recorded automatically","Workflow view: four blocks on one screen","Thinking process display"],"x":"When you can't think it through, lay it out and look. The Whiteboard lets you turn ideas, features and to-dos into cards and mark dependencies and data flow with connectors; it is also the dashboard for this conversation — how far a tool has got and how this session is controlled are both visible on the same screen. What the Whiteboard is for Three things: - Externalise thinking : write whatever comes up as a card first — no need to order it yet, connect and tidy it later. - See dependencies : a connector means \"who depends on whom\" and \"where data flows from and to\"; the arrow direction is the order. - Act as the live run : tool execution leaves step cards automatically, and the workflow view can control how this conversation runs, in place. The Whiteboard is linked to the current conversation: a Team Discussion can read the cards on the board as background, and you can also post an ink brief to the Whiteboard to work through it. Basic operations Enter from \"Whiteboard\" in the top bar; it opens on the free canvas. - New card : the \"＋\" button in the top bar, or click \"Root card\" in the flow view to start a new process. - Move and lay out : drag cards directly. \"Auto-arrange layout\" in the top bar arranges them in layers by connector relationships (shortcut Ctrl + L ). - Edit a card : double-click a card to open the edit dialog, where you can change the title, body, colour and connections. - Multi-select : hold Shift (or Ctrl / Cmd ) and click to add to or remove from the selection; with several selected, Ctrl + D duplicates and Delete removes. - Connect : switch the tool to \"Connect\", then click the start card and the end card. A connector is dependency and data flow. - Pen and text notes : the toolbar switches to the pen for freehand drawing; in \"Text\" mode, click a blank spot to write a note. - Hide pen marks : \"Undo stroke\" undoes the last stroke; \"Clear all strokes\" wipes them all at once. - Export and clear : the top bar exports the whole board (cards, links, colours, Mermaid source and JSON); \"Clear board\" removes every card — confirm the prompt before you do. Press Esc at any time to clear the selection and return to the \"Select / Move\" tool. Card colour is its category Colour is not just looks: the kanban view and AI organisation both categorise by it: Colour Meaning --- --- Default Uncategorised Red Risk Orange To do Yellow Idea Green Done Blue Information Purple Creative If unsure, leave it on default and colour things in one pass when you tidy up — in the kanban view, changing column changes colour. Links with Chat, Team and Ink The Whiteboard is not an isolated picture; it connects to several capabilities: - Models can read and write the board directly : reading existing cards, adding cards, updating titles and bodies, changing dependencies, changing colours, plus batch operations and clearing. So you can simply say \"turn these ideas into cards and connect them\", and it will do the arranging. - AI organise into a flow chart : the organise button in the top bar rebuilds card titles, bodies, dependencies and colours as a whole, keeping existing card IDs unchanged. It is \"Whiteboard AI organisation\" under \"Settings → AI-assisted features\", and can be turned off separately or pointed at a specific model. - Team Discussion reads the Whiteboard : when you start a Team Discussion, the cards currently on the board are handed to each member as background, so they discuss what you have already written down instead of guessing again. - ink briefs can be posted here : after Grind Mode collects the ink, click \"Post to Whiteboard to work through\" to lay the task brief out as cards and keep working it, see Grind Mode. Live Mermaid rendering Cards and connectors automatically produce a Mermaid diagram (direction left to right), rendered live into a flow chart by Mermaid.js. The chart button in the top bar shows or hides this rendered area; the diagram is redrawn as soon as a card changes. If rendering fails (for example, a special character in a card title), the panel shows the reason in place, and the Whiteboard itself is unaffected. Six display modes The top bar has five view buttons, plus the default free canvas — six ways to look at it in all. Clicking the currently selected button again returns to the free canvas. Mode What you see When to use --- --- --- Main board (free canvas) Cards, connectors, pen, text notes Divergent tidying, laying things out freely Flow Cards listed in stages by dependency level, each marked with its input and output counts Seeing the order and the merge points Kanban Columns by card colour; drag into another column to recategorise Moving one thing through its states Outline A tree expanded by connector hierarchy, collapsible level by level Long lists, hierarchies Git tree The repository structure of the currently open Project Understanding branches, commits and worktree state Workflow Control bar + process card wall + current run + team star map Watching while it runs The flow and kanban views both carry an \"Arrange\" button that syncs the current view's levels or column order back to the canvas. The outline view lists cards with no connectors separately under \"Unconnected cards\", reminding you there are still isolated ideas. Git tree view You need a Project open first; the Git tree reads the state of that Project's repository. It recognises and draws: - The repository itself, HEAD, the current branch, local branches, remote branches, tags and remotes. - The worktree list, and stashes. - The working tree's three counts: staged, modified, untracked (each with a file count; \"no changes\" when there are none). - Unpushed commits, and the recent commit log. Node positions and the whole view can be dragged; the arrangement you make is stored on this machine, so it is as you left it next time. With no Project open it says so, rather than drawing an empty graph. Tool step cards recorded automatically Every tool execution automatically creates a step card on the canvas; you don't have to note it down. - The card title reads like \"Step N: <what this step does \", the body summarises the step's parameters and is replaced by a result excerpt once the step finishes. - Colour shows state: yellow = running, green = done, red = error ; cancelled is the default grey. - Cards are automatically chained in call order. - State changes only write back to the card body and colour; the number and position stay put, so the board does not jump around mid-run. - When a new run starts, the previous batch of step cards is cleared as a batch, leaving only this round's on the board. In the flow, kanban and outline views, step cards are hidden by default; \"Show tool steps\" at the top right brings them out. They record what this session actually called; they consume no tokens and take no prompt budget. To see what a tool actually did, this is the most reliable place to look. Workflow view: four blocks on one screen Switch to Workflow mode: this screen is both console and live floor. Control bar : stop, resume and finish, auto-advance (the Target Mode switch), reply mode (four built-in levels + custom, any level selectable), thinking effort (auto / off / low / medium / high). The available levels follow the current model's capabilities; a level the upstream does not recognise never appears. These controls do not keep a separate state — they write the same state as the top of the chat box, so the two can never disagree. Next to them is \"Go to Team\", which jumps straight to Team mode. Process card wall : each Action process spec is one card; the card face shows how many steps, how many inputs, who wrote it (you / the model), where the output lands, and the tags. \"Run this\" sends it into the current conversation as an @-mention, \"View steps\" expands the step list in place, and \"Pin\" puts the ones you use most first (with \"Pinned only\" to narrow the wall). If a spec fails to parse, a hint appears here. For how to write a spec, see Actions process specs. Current run : projected straight from the run record — which step is running, how long it has run, whether it succeeded. With no record it says so plainly: this session has no run record yet, or this round is still thinking and has not called a tool. Team star map : lays out the most recent Team Discussion of this conversation by role — who replied to whom, which Tools each touched, which subagents they spawned — all taken from real records. Click a member to see its recent speech sequence. With no team record for this conversation, it says so directly rather than drawing a decorative graph. Thinking process display The model's reasoning and thinking before it acts is shown live in a collapsible panel; once thinking is done it collapses automatically, and clicking the header expands it again to review. It is a separate block and never mixed into the actual answer. Cards, text notes, pen strokes, Git node positions and the workflow view's pin preferences are all stored on this machine and survive refreshes and restarts. They do not travel with the conversation, and they are not overwritten by other Projects."},{"t":"Memory, User Profile and Knowledge Base","u":"13-memory-knowledge.html","s":"Collaboration & Reasoning","h":["Why memory exists","Where the memory panel is","Long-term memory: what is kept, and where it comes from","Correcting and cleaning up memory","User Profile","How memory enters the conversation","Spark: insights from Chat worth keeping","Knowledge base","Vault","How the three kinds of information divide the work","Token cost and switches"],"x":"Why memory exists Having to explain \"I use Python and TypeScript\", \"this repo doesn't want drive-by refactors\" and \"don't write long preambles\" again every time you open a new Chat is pure overhead. Memory & Knowledge turns this kind of information from \"repeated every time\" into \"distilled once, reused long-term\": SLATE accumulates the points itself during Chat, and already knows the background before the next conversation starts. This page covers four things: where long-term memory comes from and how to change it, what the User Profile is, what Spark captures, and how the knowledge base is divided by ownership. Where the memory panel is The \"Memory & Profile\" button in the top bar opens the same panel, which has four tabs: Tab What it manages --- --- Long-term memory The points distilled one by one; add, edit and delete User Profile Structured information such as role, style and tech stack Knowledge Center Search, adding and ownership of knowledge documents Vault Markdown notes, with [[wikilinks]] , tags and backlinks On mobile, manage long-term memory in the \"Memory\" tab at the bottom; see Mobile remote control and LAN access. Long-term memory: what is kept, and where it comes from - Distilled automatically during Chat : after each reply, SLATE quietly reads the recent conversation and distils information worth keeping long-term into memory entries — your preferences, decisions you have made, project background, terminology conventions, explicit constraints. - Add manually : click \"+ Add memory\", write an entry and pick a category. - Extract from Chat : click \"Extract from chat\" to turn the current conversation into candidate memories. - Automatic deduplication : identical or highly similar memories are not added again; the extraction result tells you how many were skipped. Categories: preference / decision / project / terminology / fact / general / other; plus architecture / technology / convention / build / log / configuration for knowledge-type entries. Category only affects organisation and retrieval, not whether an entry is used. Memory is stored on this machine and persists across sessions. Every conversation attaches the most recent batch of memories and the User Profile to the Context, so a new Chat does not need the background explained again. - Long-standing preferences and habits: \"give the conclusion first\", \"don't commit automatically\" - Decisions already made, and the reasoning at the time - Background, terminology and directory conventions unique to the Project - Explicit boundaries: \"this repo takes no new dependencies\" One-off tasks, transient state, small talk, raw tool output and bare code snippets are not worth keeping — automatic distillation filters them out too. Correcting and cleaning up memory Information goes stale, and memory has to be editable too. Three routes: 1. Automatic overwrite and deletion : if in conversation you explicitly correct, overturn or discard an existing memory, distillation rewrites that entry with the new content (overwrite, the entry itself stays), or clears it outright (delete). Each distillation reports honestly how many were \"added / overwritten / deleted / skipped as duplicates\". 2. Distil manually once : click \"Auto-distil profile\" to run it immediately, without waiting for the next conversation. 3. Edit entry by entry : double-click a memory's content to change the text, click the pencil button to change the category, click × to delete. Deleting also clears its mirror document in the knowledge base. A corrected memory takes part in later conversations immediately; no new session needed. User Profile The profile is the more structured part of memory: it does not record a particular sentence, but describes who you are and how you prefer to work together. Field What to fill in --- --- Role / occupation Full-stack developer, product manager… Working style Detail-oriented, rapid prototyping, iterative… Tech stack preferences Python, React, TypeScript… Communication habits Concise and direct, lots of examples… Other Anything you want SLATE to know in advance The profile is distilled automatically from conversations and memory, so usually you only add or correct a field or two; you can also fill it in by hand and click \"Save profile\" — \"Reset profile\" clears every field. It is attached to every conversation alongside long-term memory, with an explicit instruction that the model must not repeat this content back to you. How memory enters the conversation Memory and the profile need no manual \"mounting\": each time you send a message, SLATE picks the most recent batch of long-term memories and the User Profile, attaches them to this conversation's Context, and asks the model to use them to adjust style and content rather than restate them as a topic. The knowledge base works another way — only when automatic retrieval is on and a passage is genuinely relevant to the current question are matching snippets injected, see the next section. Three practical implications: - More memory is not better : attachment applies length truncation, and wordy entries eat into the Context budget. Keep what earns its place, clear out what has expired. - Memory is global : it does not change as you switch Projects — it is there in whichever Project you enter (the knowledge base, by contrast, is divided by ownership). - The profile beats correcting : filling in \"tech stack preference: TypeScript\" once saves you correcting the model in every conversation. Spark: insights from Chat worth keeping Spark is an automatic harvest at the end of a conversation. When you switch sessions, SLATE judges whether the previous one contains technical insight genuinely worth reusing across conversations — - Important technical decisions: which approach was chosen, why, what was traded off - Non-obvious solutions to problems - Reusable code or architecture patterns - Discoveries about key configuration and environment setup - Engineering lessons worth writing down Matching content is filed as a knowledge document titled like \"Spark · something\" and takes part in later retrieval injection; ordinary Q&A, small talk, tool output and ad-hoc debugging are not collected. When something is captured, it tells you how many entries were collected. This pipeline can be turned off under Settings → AI-assisted features → Conversation insight capture. Knowledge base The knowledge base is the \"retrieve on demand\" layer: it does not sit in the Context permanently, and injects snippets only when relevant. - Add : click \"+ Add knowledge\", fill in a title and content; Markdown is supported. - Retrieval : tick \"Search knowledge base automatically during chat\"; \"Snippets\" next to it sets how many can be injected at most each time (1–12, default 5); you can also search by keyword in the search box right away. - Sources : hand-added notes, mirrors of long-term memory, insights captured by Spark and output from team Workflows all flow in over time. - Divided by ownership : adding knowledge with a Project open first asks whether to \"save into this Project\" or \"save as global\". For the same title (case and leading/trailing whitespace ignored) the Project entry overrides the global one, while the rest of the global set stays visible; documents from other Projects do not enter the current view. Full rules in Projects, workspaces and per-project configuration. - Delete : from a Project's view you cannot delete the global copy (that would take away a document other Projects share). Collapse the Project first and delete from the global-only view. Vault The panel's fourth tab is the Vault: a Markdown note library. Folders and notes are arranged as a tree, source on the left and live preview on the right. Notes can carry tags (comma-separated), and you can use [[note name]] to create a wikilink; with a note selected, the bottom lists its backlinks — which notes mention it. The search box finds by title, the tag bar filters by tag. Good for long material you want to keep but that does not belong in knowledge snippets. How the three kinds of information divide the work Long-term memory User Profile Knowledge documents --- --- --- --- Source Distilled automatically during Chat, or added manually Distilled from memory and interaction history, or filled in by hand Added manually, memory mirrors, Spark, Workflow output Stored where On this machine in SLATE, persisted across sessions SLATE's local Settings SLATE's local knowledge base, divided into global / Project ownership When it is used The most recent batch attached to every conversation Attached to every conversation Snippets injected by relevance once automatic retrieval is on How to change it Add, edit and delete in the panel Edit the five fields in the panel, or reset Search, add, delete Token cost and switches Memory distillation, conversation insight and Context compression all call a model themselves, so they are all registered under \"Settings → AI-assisted features\": each can be turned off, or pointed at a cheaper model of its own. The three memory-related slots are automatic memory distillation, manual memory extraction and conversation insight capture. Turned off means not a single model call is sent. See Settings and security approval. Turning off automatic distillation does not affect memory already accumulated, nor manual adding and editing; it only stops new entries being added, overwritten or deleted automatically. Knowledge and Spark entries already in the library still take part in retrieval as usual."},{"t":"Projects, workspaces and per-project configuration","u":"14-projects.html","s":"Projects & Data","h":["What a Project is","Opening, the register and switching","Project strip and file tree","Workspace: several folders in one Project","Per-project constitution","Per-project overrides: Actions, knowledge base and MCP","Better Project Understanding","Code Review","Running in parallel across Projects"],"x":"What a Project is SLATE treats one folder as one Project, both conceptually and operationally. Once you open a directory, the file tree, Git information, the terminal's working directory and the scope of code search all follow it. A Project's identity follows the folder's absolute path: reopening the same folder returns to the same Project, with its sessions, constitution and scene (which session you last stopped at, drafts, the board, the default model) all intact, so history does not split into two piles. Opening, the register and switching - The \"Open Project…\" button in the top-bar Project strip opens a dialog: the top is the \"registered Projects\" list, where every Project you have opened stays — click one to go back and carry on from the last session and draft; below is a directory path input and disk shortcuts. - After opening, the Project name itself is the switcher : click it to pop up the registered Projects, where you can switch to another Project, pin the ones you use, or remove one from recent (removal only drops the index; it deletes no files or sessions). - The × in the Project strip collapses the current Project , it does not delete it: it stays in the register, and its sessions, constitution and files on disk are untouched; switch back from the Project name at any time. - ↻ reopens and refreshes the current Project; a Workspace reopens its host directory. - The number of registered Projects is capped; at the limit, the archived entry that has not been opened for longest is evicted first, and pinned entries are never evicted. Project strip and file tree The Project strip has three more buttons: Better Project Understanding, Code Review, and Project settings (which jumps straight to the constitution section of the Settings page). The file tree on the left supports descending level by level, breadcrumb jumps and \"..\" to go up; click a file to preview it in place, and the ＋ in the preview header inserts a snippet into the Chat input — it takes an excerpt first, so a whole large file is not poured in at once. Workspace: several folders in one Project Some work is inherently spread across directories: one repo for the frontend, one for the backend, another directory for docs. A Workspace brings them together in one Project, so you don't have to keep opening Projects. - In the \"Open Project\" dialog, fill in a name and one folder path per line, then click \"New workspace\". - Member directories appear as pills above the file tree: click one to make it the current working folder ; the × on a pill removes it from the Workspace (files on disk are untouched), and ＋ adds a folder. - A \"Workspace\" badge sits next to the Project name; hover to see how many folders it contains and which one is current. Why work on only one root at a time: file writes, Git operations and terminal commands all need a single definite target. If you were \"on several roots\" at once, where a git status or a file write should land would be a matter of luck. So a Workspace gathers scope , not concurrency — switching the current root is one click, but actions at any moment land clearly in the directory you selected. Per-project constitution The constitution is the Project's rules for the model (technical boundaries, code conventions, architecture constraints). It can belong to one Project alone: - With a Project open, saving the constitution writes it into that Project's .slate/config.json and applies only to that Project; it travels with the repository, so it is still there when handed to a colleague. - When a Project has no constitution of its own, it falls back to the global one. The two store to their own places, and neither overwrites the other. - The line under the constitution box on the Settings page states which one you are changing: \"Editing the constitution of Project 'X'…\" or \"Editing the global constitution…\". \"Project constitution\" on the right of Prompt Factory also reads the currently effective one, see Prompt Factory. Field names and format: rules is an array of strings, one sentence each. Without rules , or with an empty array, no rules are injected. If the content is not valid JSON, the save is rejected and you are told the format is wrong. Per-project overrides: Actions, knowledge base and MCP Same principle as the constitution: one global copy as the base, and the Project's copy overrides it . The three differ in the granularity of the override: Overridden Granularity Where it lives in a Project How to drop the override --- --- --- --- Actions process specs Overridden by id: put a spec of the same name in the Project and this Project uses it; the global one still applies in other Projects .slate/actions/<id .yml Delete the Project copy and you are back to global Knowledge base Override by title + merge: the Project's batch comes first and overrides global documents of the same title, the rest of the global set stays visible Ownership is recorded in the knowledge base, not in your repository Delete the Project's knowledge entry MCP servers Mask: records only whether this machine enables it and which tools are allowed; it does not change the global configuration mcp.servers in .slate/config.json Remove the mask and you are back to following global Lists and editors both mark which copy you are looking at: a Project copy carries a \"This Project\" badge, and the editor states which copy is being changed. The Action editor also has \"Save a copy in this Project\", which saves the global copy you have in hand as a Project override; when you are editing the Project copy, the delete button reads \"Drop override\" and notes that dropping it returns this Project to the global copy. The Project's .slate/config.json records only whether this server is enabled (and optionally a tool allowlist); addresses and keys stay in SLATE's local MCP configuration: tools omitted or empty means everything is allowed. Pushing the whole repository to a remote carries no URL or key with it. Better Project Understanding When you take over a Project, the most time-consuming part is not writing code but working out how this pile of directories actually runs. This feature has AI read the Project within a budget and produce two documents: - Tour · encyclopedia : Project overview, tech stack, annotated directory structure, core modules, entry points and how to start it (the detailed level also maps data flow and call chains). - Rulebook : technical boundaries, code conventions, architecture constraints, run and build commands. Every rule must be derived from evidence that actually exists in the Project, written as directly executable imperatives. The scan budget has three levels: Level Directory tree depth Files read closely Output size --- --- --- --- Brief 2 levels 6 key files about 900 characters Balanced 3 levels 16 key files about 1800 characters Detailed 5 levels 36 key files about 3200 characters Files to read closely are picked by priority: README, dependency manifests and build configuration, and core entry files come first; at the same level, shorter paths win, so deep generated artifacts do not crowd out entry files. Generation can be cancelled at any time; it writes only from the evidence it scanned, and anything it cannot determine is marked plainly \"to be determined\". The result is persisted in the Project's .slate/config.json , so the next time you open the Project you can read it directly without regenerating. The two documents can be switched between and copied separately, and you can change level and regenerate. This is the most expensive AI feature in the whole table; it can be turned off under Settings → AI-assisted features, or pointed at a cheaper model. Code Review Code Review reads git diff and has AI do a structured review along four dimensions. There are three ways to take the diff, suited to different situations: Mode What it reads When to use --- --- --- Unstaged changes What changed in the working tree but has not been git add ed Self-checking the batch in hand before committing Staged changes What has been git add ed and is ready to commit Gatekeeping the batch about to enter a commit Commit range Commits between a start point and an end point (end defaults to HEAD ) Reviewing one commit, or a few changes on a branch Four review dimensions: code quality (naming, style, logic, duplication, error handling), security (injection, sensitive information leakage, authentication and authorisation, input handling), performance (complexity, needless computation, memory risk, N+1 queries) and maintainability (coupling, interface design, comments, test hints). Results come in three views: - Full report : overall assessment + analysis of the four dimensions, formatted in Markdown. - List of line-level comments : cards sorted by severity, in four levels — \"critical / major / suggestion / info\"; clicking 文件:行号 on a card copies the location to the clipboard, so you can jump to it back in your editor. - Four-dimension cards : the four dimensions side by side, each with a summary and its number of related comments. The report can be copied whole; to review again, change the mode or the range. The prerequisites are that the directory really is a Git repository and has changes — if it is not a Git repository it tells you straight away, and with no changes it says plainly \"no review needed\". Very large changes are truncated, with a note in the summary. The model used can be set separately under Settings → AI-assisted features → Code Review. Running in parallel across Projects Switching Projects no longer interrupts a Chat that is generating. The relevant Settings are all under Settings → Multitasking and Projects: Setting Default Description --- --- --- Let it keep running when I switch sessions On The session you leave carries on in the background, its progress and outcome landing in the task centre on the right; turn it off for the old behaviour: switching sessions interrupts this one How many run at once 2 Overall cap for cross-Project parallelism, 1–4 How many per Project 1 Serial by default within a Project, 1–3 A send beyond the cap is not swallowed: it goes into the queue, and both above the input box and in the task centre on the right it states \"queued\" and how many sessions are still waiting; it picks up automatically when the previous one finishes. Under any Settings, the stop button only stops the session on screen. The background session works with its own Project scene — files, terminal, constitution and knowledge base snippets all follow the Project it belongs to, and are not written into the Project you are currently looking at. Each session's tokens are counted separately, and the usage bar reflects only the session on screen. Write into the Project : rules, processes and knowledge that hold only for this one repository — \"this repo uses pnpm\", \"lint must run before any change\", \"the pitfalls of this deployment script\". They travel with the code, and survive a machine change or a handover to a colleague. Keep it global : things that hold across Projects — your preferred way of phrasing, general Workflows, frequently used reference excerpts. The global copy is the fallback for every Project; when a Project has nothing of its own, it is used. The test is simple: if this content were copied into another Project, would it mislead? If yes, write it into the Project."},{"t":"Prompt Factory","u":"15-prompt-factory.html","s":"Projects & Data","h":["What Prompt Factory does","The three areas on screen","Who the output goes to","Assembly order","Task templates","Output tone","What the result looks like","Where the constitution comes from","Division of labour with Grind Mode","Division of labour with Target Mode","How to use the result"],"x":"What Prompt Factory does It takes a task you have already thought through and organises it into a Prompt you can paste straight to an external Coding Agent: Context, project rules, constraints and delivery requirements each in their place, with no need to write it from scratch every time. SLATE's role here is organiser , not executor: it lays out the Context and constraints, and the actual code changes are left to the external tool. The three areas on screen Area What's there How you use it --- --- --- Left workbench Three dropdowns — target Agent / task type / output tone — and three inputs: task description, relevant Context, constraints Where you do most of the writing Right sidebar Project constitution, referenceable files, quality check Tick files, check for gaps Result area below The generated Prompt, plus three buttons: copy, download Markdown, save as material Take it away from here once generated At the top of the workbench there are two more: reference recent Chat, and clear. Who the output goes to You pick the target Agent first; different presets rewrite the # 执行对象 and # 交付要求 sections: Target Agent Positioning appended at generation Delivery requirements --- --- --- Codex Implement, verify and explain the result in the current codebase Make the changes directly, then state the changed files, how they were verified and the remaining risk Claude Code Complete the code changes in the existing engineering style Read the relevant files first, then make the minimal necessary changes, then give the test results Cursor Used as an in-editor task instruction Give clear editing steps, target files and the behaviour to preserve General Agent Hand to any Coding Agent to execute Output an executable plan, the necessary changes and a verification checklist Assembly order After you click \"Generate Prompt\", SLATE assembles the final text in the order below. Empty sections are skipped entirely, so nothing in the result is a placeholder. Order Section What goes in How to write it better --- --- --- --- 1 # 任务 What to do, why, and what counts as done (required) Put the acceptance criteria straight into the task; \"optimise it a bit\" is the easiest way to invite improvisation 2 # 执行对象 Who it goes to, and how they should deliver Decided by the target Agent preset; the preset can be changed 3 # 工作方式 Explore first or get straight to work; whether to audit risk first Decided by \"output tone\": execute directly / audit cautiously / explore then implement; it also always requires \"read the existing code before acting, and prefer the Project's existing patterns\" 4 # 项目宪法 The currently effective constitution rules, numbered one by one Comes from the Project or global copy, see Projects, workspaces and per-project configuration; it does not appear when there are no rules 5 # 重点文件 The list of file paths you ticked Paths cost fewer tokens than pasting content; the external Agent reads them itself 6 # 已知上下文 Errors, interface contracts, key code snippets, a summary of the recent Chat Only what the other side cannot see; this content is carried over as is 7 # 约束条件 What must not change, what must be preserved, which styles or processes to follow Complements the constitution: the constitution governs the long term, this governs this one job 8 # 交付要求 Decided by the preset, plus two fixed items Fixed requirements: do not modify files unrelated to the task; if there are existing uncommitted changes, understand and accommodate them first; when done, state the verification command or why verification was not possible Task templates If you don't want to start from a blank page, pick a task type first; it pre-fills the task description and constraints: Task type Pre-filled skeleton --- --- Feature implementation Implement / expected behaviour / acceptance criteria; constraints: follow the existing architecture and style, keep the change as small as possible, run checks when done Bug fix Symptom / reproduction path / expected result; constraints: locate the root cause first, do not roll back unrelated changes, add verification that covers the problem Code review What to review / what to focus on; constraints: list problems by severity, each with location, risk and a suggested fix Refactoring Refactoring goal / behaviour to keep unchanged / problems you want improved; constraints: external behaviour stays compatible, no large new dependencies, follow existing patterns A template only fills an input that is empty; it never overwrites what you have already written. Output tone \"Output tone\" only affects the # 工作方式 section of the result, independently of task type: Output tone The sentence written into the Prompt --- --- Execute directly Please go straight into execution; no long plan preamble is needed Audit cautiously Please audit the risks and boundaries first, then make changes Explore then implement Please give 2-3 viable directions first, then choose the safest one to execute All three tones append the same requirement: read the existing code before acting, and prefer the Project's existing patterns. What the result looks like The result is a Markdown document assembled in the order above, with sections separated by # headings: What you get is final text: paste the whole thing into the external tool, with no preamble to add. Where the constitution comes from \"Project constitution\" in the right sidebar shows the currently effective rules; clicking \"Insert project constitution\" writes them into the constraints, numbered by item. The rules come from the Project's own .slate/config.json ; if the Project has none, the global copy is used, and the sidebar follows when you switch Projects. The full rules are in Projects, workspaces and per-project configuration. Division of labour with Grind Mode Both turn thoughts into text; the difference is the starting point: Grind Mode Prompt Factory --- --- --- Starting point A rough idea; you don't yet know exactly what you want The task is already clear; you just need a delivery format What it does AI's three-stage questioning (Intake → Grind → Collect), one question at a time, drawing out the gaps Fills known information into a fixed skeleton and assembles final text Output A structured task brief (ink brief): goal / audience / deliverable / acceptance criteria / boundaries / suggested path / open questions A Prompt you can hand straight to an external Agent Next step Send it to Target Mode to execute, post to Whiteboard to work through, or save as a template for reuse Copy, download or save as material, then hand to an external tool to execute One line if you can't tell them apart: if you still can't say what you want, go to Grind Mode; if you can, come to the Factory. Division of labour with Target Mode Target Mode has SLATE do the work itself: it has its own Tools (read and write files, run commands, inspect Git) and runs a six-stage loop until verification passes, see Agent Autopilot and Target Mode. Prompt Factory executes nothing; what it produces is a Prompt for someone else to execute — if you want Claude Code, Codex or Cursor to do the work, use the Factory. How to use the result 1. Check it section by section : the result appears below; read from # 任务 down, focusing on whether acceptance criteria and boundaries are missing. 2. Copy / download / save material : the three buttons at the top right of the result area are copy, download Markdown and save as material. Material goes into your Prompt material library, ready to reuse next time. 3. Iterate : change one input and click \"Generate Prompt\" again — the output is reassembled whole, not appended to. To start over, click \"Clear\". \"Quality check\" on the right gives four live prompts as you write: whether the task is clear enough, whether Context or files are included, whether constraint boundaries are declared, and whether a target Agent is selected. All four lit does not guarantee a good result, but a missing item usually means this Prompt needs another pass. Two shortcuts: \"Reference recent Chat\" puts a summary of the last few messages in the current session into the known Context; \"Insert ticked files\" reads the content of the files ticked on the right (at most 8 at a time, the first 6000 characters of each). The file list reads the current Project directory; with no Project open it prompts you to open one first. External Agents fear two things above all: not knowing when the job counts as done, and not knowing what must not be touched. - Write acceptance criteria as decidable facts : \" npm test is all green\" is clearer than \"tests pass\"; \"the response fields and signature are unchanged\" is clearer than \"stay compatible\". - Write boundaries as explicit prohibitions and protections : \"do not touch files under src/legacy/ \", \"keep the existing public API signatures\", \"add no new dependencies\". - Also state what not to do : saying \"this time change only this one file\" is far safer than letting the Agent guess the scope."},{"t":"Task list and Scheduled Tasks","u":"16-schedule.html","s":"Projects & Data","h":["What the task list is","Reading the list panel","Who builds the list, and how status changes","To-dos and mobile","What Scheduled Tasks are for","Creating a Scheduled Task","Writing a prompt for unattended runs","The three schedules","Event-driven triggers","Where results go","Managing tasks","When it won't run"],"x":"What the task list is When Target Mode runs a multi-step task, the model first builds a TODOLIST: it breaks the big task into small items you can check off, then works through them one by one. The list isn't decoration — until every item is closed out, the model isn't allowed to claim it is done. The list is isolated per Chat: every session has its own, they don't interfere with each other, and it's still there when you reopen that Chat. Reading the list panel The list shows live in the right-hand column of the message area: - The header reads Task list {done}/{total} , with \"blocked n\" and \"in progress n\" beside it, plus a progress bar. - Every item carries a status marker: to-do, in progress, done, blocked. - Whenever an item or a batch is finished, the model syncs the status at once — progress doesn't wait for the wrap-up. The \"Task list\" button in the top bar collapses the whole column and gives the width back to the message area; click it again to expand. Collapsing doesn't make the progress vanish entirely — hovering the button reports {done}/{total} . When this session has no list, the button is greyed out. The collapsed state is remembered on your machine: it survives a refresh and a restart, and the list carrying on won't push the column back open on its own. Who builds the list, and how status changes The list isn't something you maintain by hand. In Target Mode, when a task spans many files or many steps, or is a debugging or implementation job, the model builds the list first and then starts; a simple single-step task skips the list and just does the work, so not seeing a list is normal. Each item's status moves as execution goes on: Status Meaning --- --- To-do Not reached yet In progress Working on this item Done Finished, and verified Blocked Held up by an outside condition (missing dependency, waiting for confirmation, environment not ready); the model explains why in its reply Until every item is closed out, the model isn't allowed to write a wrap-up report; the system reads the remaining items back to it and pushes it to keep going. To-dos and mobile The phone has a \"Tasks\" tab at the bottom (panel title \"Tasks / Scheduled\"), where you can view, create, run now and delete Scheduled Tasks; tasks created on the computer are the same list when you open the phone. See Mobile remote control and LAN access. What Scheduled Tasks are for A Scheduled Task = one preset prompt + one trigger time. When the time comes, SLATE executes it locally: it hands the prompt to the chosen model and runs it once, and the result lands as its own session — you don't have to sit there, and it doesn't take over the Chat you're working in. Creating one takes four things: a name, a prompt (the text sent to the model when the time comes), a model, and a mode. Creating a Scheduled Task The entry point is the ＋ menu to the left of the Chat input (choose \"Scheduled Tasks\"); the panel title is \"Scheduled / Event Tasks\". On mobile it's the \"Tasks\" tab at the bottom, then \"＋ New\". 1. Fill in the task name . It appears in the title of the session that run creates, so make it a name you'll recognise at a glance. 2. Write the prompt : the text sent to the model when the time comes. Say exactly what it should produce. 3. Choose the model : the dropdown groups by domestic / international / local / custom, and defaults to the model you're using now. 4. Choose the mode : daily / once / interval, then fill in the time or the number of minutes. 5. Click \"+ Add task\"; the task appears in the list below and can be disabled or deleted at any time. Writing a prompt for unattended runs Every Scheduled Task run is a new session with no earlier context to lean on; the system prompt for the run also tells the model to carry out the instruction directly and completely and to output the result rather than ask back. So the prompt has to be self-contained: - State the output format : for example \"use Markdown to list three blocks: what was finished yesterday, what wasn't, and the suggested order for today\". - State where the information comes from : for example \"read TODO.md in the working directory and the recent Git commits\", not \"carry on from where we left off\". - Don't write branches that need your answer to move on — nobody is there to answer when the time comes. - Pin length and structure inside the prompt , otherwise how long the output runs depends entirely on how the model feels that day. The three schedules Schedule How the time is counted Good for --- --- --- Once One point in time, run once A one-off reminder or a one-off Summary; the task disables itself after the run Daily One point in time, run every day; if today's time hasn't come yet, wait; if it has and today's run hasn't happened, run A daily Summary, the routine check before you stop work for the day Fixed interval Every N minutes (60 by default, 1 minimum) Short actions you need to watch over and over, such as glancing at the build status every so often Event-driven triggers Besides the clock, a task can be pushed along by an event: it runs when the event happens, and the result is archived as its own session too. Three sources: Event Trigger condition Configurable --- --- --- File change A file under the watched path is created or modified Watched file / directory paths (one per line; empty means the root of the Project the task belongs to); cooldown Git push detection HEAD of the specified repository changes Repository path; cooldown Webhook receive An external system sends a request to this task's callback address Secret (used to check where the request came from, optional; the request body is injected into the prompt as Context) Cooldown is the minimum gap between triggers (60 seconds by default, 10 minimum); its job is to stop one bulk change from firing the task over and over. An event task's prompt automatically carries the trigger Context — the files that changed, the latest commit, the Webhook content — so the model knows what woke it this time. The event type and watched paths are saved with the task and take effect as soon as you change them. On startup SLATE takes a snapshot of file changes and Git state first, so history from before the launch isn't mistaken for a new event. Where results go Every run creates its own session, with a prefix and a time in the title: The session lands under the Project the task belongs to — whichever Project the task was created in, that's where the result hangs; after switching to another Project, filtering by Project won't misplace it. Open the session and you get the full prompt and the model's reply. Managing tasks Every row in the task list shows: schedule · model used · result of the last run (a run writes its time; a failure writes the reason; never run shows \"not run yet\"). - The checkbox enables / disables, so you can park a task without deleting it. - ▶ runs it now instead of waiting for the next time; a task already running isn't triggered twice. - × deletes the task. When it won't run Scheduled Tasks are executed by a local scheduler, so a few limits are worth knowing up front: - They only run while SLATE is running. Nothing runs while the app is closed; after you reopen it, a task whose time has passed today and that hasn't run today runs once to catch up. - The model must have its API Key configured, and must be an OpenAI-compatible model. When the Key can't be had or the model isn't compatible, the reason is written into the task status instead of failing silently. - If \"Scheduled and event tasks\" is turned off under \"Settings → AI assistance\", tasks stop running; trigger records are kept, and the status says it was this switch that turned them off. See Settings and security approvals. Good for : fixed actions that don't need you to make a call halfway. A Summary of yesterday's progress, a periodic check of the repository state, a glance at the build every so often, a nudge when files change — the model can wrap these up in one reply. Not good for : long tasks that need you to come in and choose. A Scheduled Task won't stop to ask you, and won't wait for your confirmation before going on. Writing the decision part as \"list the options and what each one costs\" and keeping the call to yourself is safer than letting it decide for you."},{"t":"Data, backups and storage","u":"17-data.html","s":"Projects & Data","h":["Your data is all on your own machine","Managing chat history","Message-level actions","One-click Backup and restore","Managing storage","Cross-device sync and local preferences","Uninstall or change computers: three steps"],"x":"This page answers three questions: where your data lives, how to back it up and restore it, and which item to touch when storage grows. SLATE is a local-first Tool: Chats, memory and Settings all stay on your own machine, and the API Key is only used when a model is called. Your data is all on your own machine Data sits in two layers: one in the database, one in the files and folders of the data directory. You only need to remember one unit — \"the local data directory\" — and handle the whole directory together when deleting or copying. What is stored Which layer Notes --- --- --- Sessions and messages Local data directory · database Title, message body, model used, Token usage Long-term memory and User Profile Local data directory · database Memory entries distilled automatically, recalled across sessions Prompt material Local data directory · database Snippets saved from Prompt Factory Knowledge base documents Local data directory · database Knowledge snippets and Spark archives Playbooks / Expert Packs / custom Skills Local data directory · files and folders A playbook is a single file; an Expert Pack and a Skill each get a directory Preferences Local data directory · configuration file Theme, models and keys, Approval mode, the various switches Backup files Local data directory · backup folder Every \"Download Backup\" also leaves a copy on this machine The local data directory is your entire estate. Either export a JSON with \"Download Backup\", or copy the whole data directory onto the new machine. Managing chat history In the \"Tasks\" list in the sidebar, every session carries its own information and actions: - Search box : typing filters titles; 2 characters or more also runs a full-text search of the content, and hits are listed as \"content matches · N\" with the session title and an excerpt of the surrounding text — click one to jump straight to that session. - Hover buttons : move the mouse over a session and three buttons appear on the right — rename, export Markdown (labelled MD ), delete ( × ). Export produces a Markdown file with every question and answer, its filename carrying the session title and the date. - Archive : the row actions include \"Archive\"; press it and this session leaves the \"Tasks\" column without a single message touched. The one that is generating can't be archived (stop it first). Archived sessions are restored or deleted one by one under \"Settings → Archived chats\" — restore puts it back in the Tasks column; delete wipes it along with its messages, with no way back. - Sort dropdown : in the sidebar header; switch between recently updated / by Project / by status / creation time / by usage. - Manage : the \"Manage\" button in the sidebar header opens the \"Manage chat history\" dialog, where you tick rows and then \"Delete selected\", or go straight to \"Clear all\"; the \"All\" checkbox at the top selects every current session in one go. Deleting a session, deleting a message and clearing everything can't be undone. Export a copy under \"Data backup\" first. Message-level actions In the message area, every message has a row of small buttons at its top right: Button What it does Applies to --- --- --- Copy Copies the body of this message All messages Edit content Turns it into an input box in place; saving writes it back and re-renders User messages Delete this message Removes this one message from the session User messages Regenerate Asks for an answer again from the history before this message The last assistant message Editing or deleting a message only affects that one message and doesn't touch other sessions. For how Chat itself works, see Chat and messages. One-click Backup and restore One export collects Chats, memory, material and Settings into a single JSON file. What the Backup contains - Every session and message, long-term memory, prompt material, and usage records. - Local preferences: models and API Keys, custom models, theme, Approval mode, AI assistance switches and so on. - So a Backup file contains your API Keys — don't pass it around. When to back up - Before uninstalling, reinstalling or moving to another computer. - Before an irreversible action such as \"Clear chats\". - Before a big tidy-up or a round of session deletions. What importing does - Sessions, messages, memory and material merge by ID: data that already exists is skipped, never overwriting what you have. - Local preferences are replaced by the copy inside the Backup, and when the import finishes the page reloads itself to apply them. - You get one confirmation before the import starts, and it begins only after you confirm. Import merges into the current data; it doesn't clear and restore. Your preferences on this machine are overwritten by the copy in the Backup; if both sides hold model keys you want to keep, the result after import may not be what you expect. If in doubt, export the current Backup first, then import the old one. Managing storage Below \"Settings → Data backup\" is \"Storage\", which first shows the total footprint, then lists the size of each item. Item What is in it What you can do When to use it --- --- --- --- Chat history database Sessions and messages, long-term memory, prompt material, usage Compress, clear chats After deleting a lot of sessions and wanting the space back; or when history is too messy and you want a clean start Built-in browser cache Cache files produced by the built-in browser Clear cache Pages render oddly, or this item has grown noticeably Custom Skills Skill documents you imported or created Can't be cleared Best kept Configuration file Theme, models and keys, the various switches Can't be cleared Best kept Other Knowledge base, logs, playbooks, Expert Packs and so on Can't be cleared Best kept The three actions differ: - Compress : actually gives back to disk the space taken up by deleted data in the database, without touching any existing content. Best right after \"I just deleted a lot of sessions\". - Clear chats : deletes every session and message; long-term memory, the knowledge base and preferences are unaffected. Irreversible, with one confirmation before it runs. - Clear cache : removes the built-in browser's cache files. Files held by a running process are released for good on the next start. Cross-device sync and local preferences If you drive the same SLATE from your phone, some preferences follow you and others deliberately stay on this machine. Preference Cross-device? --- --- Sort order of the sidebar Tasks list Synced Theme and light/dark, general UI mode Synced Models and API Keys, custom models, current model Synced Approval mode, reasoning effort, AI assistance switches Synced Notification switches, web search, image and video generation config Synced Read progress and unread markers (needs action / error / in progress / finished but unread) Local only Collapsed state of the Task list column Local only Switches for Continue Autopilot and parallel multi-tasking Local only Read progress stays local because \"have I looked at this one\" is a feeling of this device: having read it on another device shouldn't turn it read here. Uninstall or change computers: three steps 1. On the old machine open \"Settings → Data backup\" and click \"Download Backup\"; check you have SLATE-backup-<date .json . 2. Install SLATE on the new machine, start it, and go to \"Settings → Data backup\". 3. Click \"Import Backup\", choose that JSON, confirm, and wait for the page to reload itself. If knowledge base documents, playbooks, Expert Packs or custom Skills don't come across after the move, copy the whole local data directory from the old machine to the matching place on the new one — they aren't in that JSON; they're stored as files and folders. If models and API Keys didn't come in with the Backup, just fill them in again under model configuration on the new machine. A Backup is a snapshot of one moment. Importing the same old Backup over and over doesn't merge into \"the latest state\"; it only mixes old and new records. For continuous sync, reach the same machine over LAN remote control instead — see Mobile remote control and LAN access."},{"t":"Mobile remote control and LAN access","u":"18-mobile.html","s":"Mobile & Security","h":["What you can do","How to connect","Why the desktop interface doesn't change","The five bottom tabs","Who does what while you're remote","When to use remote control, and when to walk back to the computer","What mobile Chat can do","What mobile Settings can change","Auth and risk","Troubleshooting a connection"],"x":"This page is for \"I've left the computer but the work isn't finished\": connect a phone or tablet to the same LAN and you can drive the SLATE on your desktop from the browser — the interface switches itself to a dedicated mobile layout. SLATE has LAN access open the moment it starts, so there's nothing extra to configure. What you can do - Watch a running Chat and the progress of its tool calls, and keep talking. - Create, switch, rename and delete sessions. - Add, edit and delete long-term memory, and view and manage to-dos (Scheduled Tasks). - Switch models, enter an API Key, toggle dark mode. - Risky commands and file changes raise a bottom sheet for you to confirm, under the same rules as the desktop. How to connect 1. Put the phone and the computer on the same LAN (the same Wi-Fi). 2. On the computer open \"Settings → LAN remote control\"; you'll see an access address and a QR code. 3. Scan the code, or copy the address into the phone's browser, and the dedicated mobile interface opens. The port in the address is 8001 by default; when that port is taken SLATE walks up to the next free one, so go by the address shown in Settings. The \"Remote\" button in the top bar jumps straight to that Settings block too. Why the desktop interface doesn't change The mobile interface is a separate entry point: only phones and tablets hitting the LAN address land on it. In your computer's browser you still get the full three-column desktop workbench — same features, same layout, same shortcuts, zero regression. Add slate mobile=0 after the address and open it, and you stay on the desktop interface. That's how to get the full layout on a tablet with a screen big enough for it. The five bottom tabs Tab Main purpose What you can do --- --- --- Chat Keep working Watch streaming output, folded thinking cards and tool cards; ask a follow-up or stop the current round Chats Find history The session list; create, switch, rename, delete Memory Manage long-term memory Add, edit, delete and look things up; pick a category when creating Tasks Manage Scheduled Tasks See the next trigger, enable or disable, run now, delete, create Settings Adjust local preferences Model switching, reasoning effort, keys, appearance, remote info Chat : the input is pinned to the bottom; Enter sends, Shift + Enter adds a line, typing @ brings up mention candidates. Messages come as bubbles, the model's thinking sits in a collapsible card, tool calls show as tool cards with status; scrolling up during generation isn't interrupted by auto-scroll. Chats : shares the same backend data as the desktop, so a title changed or a session created on the phone shows up after a refresh on the computer, and the other way round. When a session is continued on the phone, stale desktop markers such as \"finished but unread\" are voided automatically — no out-of-date conclusions left behind. Memory : lists long-term memory one by one; tap in to edit the content or change the category, or delete it outright. When creating an entry, fill in the content and pick a category; from then on it's recalled automatically in Chat. Tasks : one Scheduled Task per row; see its time or interval, enable or disable it for now, tap \"Run now\" to execute once immediately, or delete it. Creating one means filling in name, prompt, model, mode and the time or interval in that order. Settings : the few things you most need on a phone, changed on one screen and taking effect immediately. Who does what while you're remote The mobile side is the remote control, not a second copy of the data: - The data is still one copy: Chats, memory and to-dos all live in the local data directory on the desktop machine; the phone only operates it. - Tasks still run on the computer: model requests and tool execution happen on the computer side, and the phone only starts and watches. So close SLATE on the computer and remote control is over. - Don't let the computer sleep while it runs: sleep cuts off generation and background tasks in flight. - The desktop window doesn't need to stay in the foreground, but the app has to be running. When to use remote control, and when to walk back to the computer Situation Suggestion --- --- A long task is half done and you have to go out Watch progress remotely, and add an instruction if needed You only want to rename something, delete a session, glance at memory Remote control is enough Bulk-cleaning history, pasting large chunks of Context into a template Back to the desktop: bigger screen, faster Managing Skills, editing playbooks, configuring an MCP Server Back to the desktop; these blocks live only in desktop Settings Collaborating, showing the interface to someone else Not advised — the remote address is the key What mobile Chat can do - Streaming output : appears character by character over the same protocol as the desktop; scrolling up isn't interrupted by auto-scroll, and it follows along while you're at the bottom. - Tool loop : shares the same Agent loop logic as the desktop, so the model can observe, execute, verify and report; the interface is the same source, so tool card titles don't degrade into bare English tool names. - Risky command confirmation : when a hard-coded risky rule matches, a bottom sheet appears with the command and the rule it hit, plus a one-line note on the command's purpose generated by the model, and you decide to approve or reject. - File change confirmation : edits and new files show you the diff first; only an accept writes to disk, and a reject writes nothing. - @ mentions : typing @ opens a candidate overlay for Skills, Tools and Project files; arrow keys to choose, Tab or a click to insert. - Stop really cancels : stopping shuts the streaming channel of the tool calls as well, and the backend terminates the matching child process and running tasks. What mobile Settings can change Setting Notes --- --- Model choice Listed by domestic / international / local / custom group; tap to switch, with each model's context budget shown Reasoning effort The levels offered follow the current model's abilities (auto / off / low / medium / high), sharing the same state as the selector by the desktop input Keys Enter or clear the API Key for the current model Appearance Dark mode switch; the top status bar also has a handy theme toggle. When a custom theme is on for the computer, this item says \"locked\", and tapping the toggle only explains why — no colour change Remote info The authorised access address and QR code, copyable in one tap Auth and risk LAN access is let in by the auth code carried inside the address: only a device that comes in through the address shown in Settings (or its QR code) gets access; a request without the auth code is stopped at a \"authorization required\" page. The auth code is kept on the device, so the same browser doesn't need to scan again later. That address with its auth code is the key. Any device that gets it, as long as it's on the same LAN, can open and operate your SLATE — read files, run commands, see every historical session. Don't expose the address in group chats, on forums or in screenshots. When you no longer need remote control, close SLATE on the computer and the address stops working. Security advice : this opens a local service to the LAN, so use it only on a network you trust. On public Wi-Fi such as a café, hotel or airport, close SLATE or don't use remote control. Troubleshooting a connection 1. Are the phone and the computer on the same Wi-Fi? A phone on cellular data, or on a guest network on another band, isn't on the same LAN. 2. Is the computer's firewall blocking the port? The first time LAN access opens, the system may ask about the firewall and you need to choose allow; if you said no back then, let SLATE through in the firewall settings. 3. Does the address use the computer's LAN IP? Use the address shown in Settings, not the loopback address. 4. Is a guest network isolating devices? Plenty of routers have \"AP isolation\" on by default, so devices on the same Wi-Fi can't see each other either; turn it off in the router settings, or switch to a phone hotspot. Matching the symptom gets you there faster: Symptom Most likely cause What to do --- --- --- The page spins forever and won't open The computer isn't running, or the port is blocked by the firewall Check that SLATE is running on the computer; let it through the firewall It opens and stops on \"authorization required\" The address is missing its auth code Copy the full address from Settings again, or scan again A tablet opens the desktop interface The tablet is judged a desktop device Expected behaviour, nothing is lost; for the mobile interface, open the same address in a phone browser It worked yesterday but not today The computer changed network and the IP changed Open Settings again and use the newly shown address Only one phone can't connect Guest network isolation or system rate limiting Check the router's AP isolation, or try a phone hotspot once Other things to check: if Settings says \"remote service not started\", restart the app; when the port in the address is taken by another program, SLATE switches to another one, so always go by the address Settings shows at that moment. Change the computer's Wi-Fi and the router hands out the address again, so the access link changes with it; a taken port changes it too. So after switching networks, don't reuse the old address — look at Settings again before you scan."},{"t":"Settings and security approvals","u":"19-settings.html","s":"Mobile & Security","h":["Settings blocks at a glance","AI assistance","Continue Autopilot","Task completion notifications","Theme and language","The About block","Security and Approval"],"x":"This page explains what each block of the Settings page looks after, and how SLATE asks for your consent before it acts. Settings opens from \"Settings\" in the top bar; the left navigation is generated live from the blocks on the page, one entry per block title, and there's a search field at the top: type a keyword and only blocks containing it remain, with the matching line outlined; press Enter or click to jump there, clear it and everything comes back exactly as it was. Settings blocks at a glance Block What it looks after --- --- Model configuration Add custom models and keys for built-in models, set the Base URL, one \"max context\" row per model (slider + exact number box + probe window), optional Responses API mode API test Send the shortest possible request to check whether a model's key and endpoint work Usage Cumulative Token usage across all Chats, updated as you Chat Context and Projects Context Token cap, single-output cap, whether file writes are auto-confirmed, project constitution Auto-advance The retry policy when a reply seems to stall, plus Continue Autopilot AI assistance Per-item switches and per-item model choice for Token-consuming features outside Chat Notifications Sound and system notification when a big task ends Background tasks Wake-up switches once long commands move to their own channel: waking the model when a task has news, and how many times each Chat may auto-continue Multi-tasking and Projects Whether a session keeps running in the background when you switch away, and how many run at once General UI The minimal Codex-style layout switch (experimental) Custom theme Colours (a whole set derived from four source colours), fonts (system font families + importing your own font files), panel opacity, background image and overlay density, with the current wallpaper fetchable from Wallpaper Engine; 10 base presets, and the light/dark toggle locked while it's on Web search Default search engine, page JS rendering policy Image generation Model and API Key for generation Tools Video generation Model and API Key for generation Tools Approval mode The default Approval level for each Chat Data backup and restore Backup export and import Archived chats Sessions archived out of the \"Tasks\" column, restored or deleted one by one here Storage Storage footprint and management LAN remote control Remote address and QR code Typing game Test your typing speed on the side, with a customisable prompt bank About SLATE Version, check for updates, onboarding, website and GitHub \"What the model can use\" — Skills, built-in Tools, reply modes, new features (Tools the tool factory produces itself), MCP, Expert Packs, Actions — is all managed on the \"Extensions\" page in the top bar; Settings no longer doubles as a list of capabilities. See Built-in tools and Skills. AI assistance Apart from Chat, team Chat and Prompt Factory, every feature that goes off to a model for a result on its own is registered here, 15 in total. Each one can be switched off on its own and can name its own model; with no model named, it follows the current main model. Feature How it triggers When off --- --- --- Context compression Summary Automatic No more automatic compression, and a manual \"Compress\" sends no request either Automatic memory distillation Automatic No more long-term memory distilled automatically Manual memory extraction Manual \"Extract from current chat\" in the memory panel sends no request Conversation insight capture Automatic No more insight cards generated automatically Automatic chat titles Automatic (after the first round) New Chats keep the placeholder title \"first 30 characters of the first message\" and are never named Code understanding docs Manual Scanning a Project no longer produces a guide and rulebook Code review Manual Only the diff is taken; no four-dimension review Whiteboard AI tidy-up Manual Whiteboard content is no longer handed to the model to reorder Prompt polish Manual The button by the input still clicks, but sends no request and only says \"turned off\" Parallel sub-agents Model call The Tool disappears from the tool catalogue, so the model can't see it and won't call it Risky command purpose note Automatic The Approval dialog still stops it, just without the line on the command's purpose Team Workflow execution Manual The Workflow no longer asks the model at each node Scheduled and event tasks Automatic Tasks stop running; trigger records are kept AI image generation Model call The Tool disappears from the tool catalogue AI video generation Model call The Tool disappears from the tool catalogue Above the list a running tally reads \"15 in total, N turned off\". Off means not a single model request goes out — the most direct and most controllable way to throttle. Turning off item by item what you never use saves more than compressing Context afterwards. If you only Chat and execute Tools day to day, compression, distillation, insight, image and video can all be off until you need them. Continue Autopilot Under \"Auto-advance\", on by default. What it does: Autopilot or Target Mode running out of rounds doesn't mean the work is done. If the last round was still executing Tools, the limit is pushed back 8 rounds (3 pushes at most) so the work in hand gets finished; if the last round stopped empty-handed, the items left on the list are read back to the model, pressing it to carry on or to state plainly that the task is done. After the curtain there's one more layer: when a run exits not because it \"completed\" but because it ran out of rounds, and it isn't on a manual Approval level (Auto Approval / Full access / Night mode), SLATE presses that \"continue to the end\" for you — sent once the run has gone completely quiet (compression, distillation and the other wrap-up steps still take a few seconds), at most twice per run, and a toast says \"rounds exhausted · auto-continued (1/2)\". Manual Approval levels are excluded: that level means every step needs a human nod, so it still just hangs the \"continue to the end\" bar there for you. When the quota runs out, or a continuation doesn't connect, the manual button is still right where it was. Turn it off and you're back to the old behaviour: stop when rounds run out, and say so yourself to continue. This switch stays on the local machine and doesn't sync over LAN remote control. For the mechanism, see Agent Autopilot and Target Mode. Task completion notifications When a big task ends (Target Mode, a team discussion, a Workflow finishing) you can get a nudge, and the two switches are independent: Switch Default Notes --- --- --- Play a sound when a task completes On A short chime, no permission needed Send a system notification when a task completes Off Needs notification permission from the browser; until it's granted, the block says so below Turning both off doesn't affect the task itself, it just stops the nudge. Good for hanging a long task in the background while you do something else; with them on you're back at the screen the moment work is done. Theme and language - Light and dark : toggle it with the \"light/dark\" button in the top bar, and mobile has the same button to the right of the top status bar. The choice syncs across devices: switch it on the phone and a refresh on the computer agrees. - Custom theme : this block takes only four source colours — base, panel, body text, accent — and the other thirty-odd colour tokens are derived from them. Why not open them one by one: tuning tokens individually easily produces a mess whose body text can't be read, whereas with only four sources the derivation sorts a light and a dark set by luminance and falls back on contrast, so the palette is bound to hold together. Code blocks keep dark backgrounds and light text under any palette; the danger / done / needs-action status colours switch as a group with the base colour's luminance and don't follow your accent, or red and green would stop meaning what they mean. Model brand marks don't take part in this derivation either: those with a colour original are drawn in brand colours, those without get a fixed neutral grey, and the ink colour you pick can't tint them. - 10 base presets : Paper Ink, Night Gold, Mist Blue, Deep Sea, Pine Ink, Moss Green, Clay, Dusk Purple, Graphite, Movable Type. Clicking a preset fills all four source colours at once, and you can still change just one of them afterwards; change the palette and this set is no longer that preset, with the highlight cleared automatically. - Fonts : one dropdown each for body text and code; the candidates list font families already on the system first (including \"follow system\"); the \"Import font\" button below the dropdown imports your own .woff2 / .woff / .ttf / .otf (a single file, up to 16MB), and once imported it appears in both dropdowns like a system font, with the list reporting each file's size and letting you remove them one by one. Font files exist only in the local data/theme/fonts/ , never enter the chat record and aren't copied to another device; import a single weight and the browser fakes bold and italic. The font stack ends with a generic family fallback, so moving to a machine that has neither installed nor imported the font won't leave you with a blurry default serif. - Panel opacity : one \"panel opacity\" slider (55%–100%, default 100%) controls the opacity of the base colour behind panels, cards, the input and assistant bubbles. 100% is solid, behaving exactly as the old version did, pixel for pixel; lowering it lets the page background show through, and a background image too. Your own bubbles and code blocks always stay solid, so body text never becomes hard to read because of it. It and the overlay density each look after one layer: the panel takes the lower of the two, so the two don't each apply once and wash the panels out. - Background image : pick a local PNG / JPG / GIF / WebP (no more than 4MB); only that one file lives in the local data directory, isn't uploaded over LAN remote control and doesn't enter the Backup. Once it's laid down the interface turns semi-transparent and rides on top of it, and the \"overlay density\" slider adjusts the base colour's opacity (40%–95%, default 72%): below 40% the body text sits straight on the image, above 95% it's as if there were no image at all. Setting and removing the image both go through the \"Remove\" button in the top bar; once removed the base colour is solid again. - Wallpaper Engine : on a machine with Wallpaper Engine installed there's an extra \"Fetch current wallpaper\" button here — it takes the preview of the wallpaper it's using as the background image above, and \"Detect again\" beside it re-identifies it after it was closed or its directory changed. This step is read-only: SLATE only reads the config and preview image Wallpaper Engine writes in its own directory; it doesn't start it or change any of its settings. Change the wallpaper there and this won't follow — click again; video and scene wallpapers give you that static preview, and only a dynamic preview gives you the gif (which keeps moving behind the interface). The status line reports only the wallpaper name, file size and source screen number, never the absolute path on this machine. - Can't switch light/dark while a custom theme is on : the whole token set is derived from those four source colours, and switching the light/dark base again would only set two palettes against each other. So the light/dark button dims and says \"locked\"; clicking it won't change colours but pops a hint telling you where to turn the custom theme off — mobile too. Turn the custom theme off and every trace (injected styles, colours, fonts, background image) is cleared on the spot, and light/dark switching comes back at once. - Interface language : chosen at install — Simplified Chinese or English — and there's no in-app switch while the app runs. The English interface translates the interface copy itself — buttons, block titles, the grey placeholder text in inputs and hover hints all count; model-generated content, the text you type, and code you paste into Chat are always shown as they are and never translated. The brand wordmark \"砚\" stays as it is too. - To change language, run the installer again and pick the other one; to change it on just one device, that too is decided at install time. The background image and the font files under data/theme/fonts/ live only in this machine's data directory; they aren't written into the Backup and don't sync. After moving to another device or reinstalling, the same switch says \"no background image on this machine\" and \"no imported fonts on this machine\" — just pick them again. Colours, opacity and font choices are unaffected and keep working. The About block \"About\" gathers the version and update actions in one place: - Current version : which build you're running now. - Check for Updates : also checked once at startup. When a new version turns up it shows the current and latest versions, a summary of the release notes, and \"Download Update\" and \"Release notes\" buttons, both opening in your system browser. - Onboarding : forgotten how the three-column workspace works? Watch it again here. - Project links : the website and the GitHub repository, again opening in your system browser. Security and Approval SLATE follows the \"no self-directed changes\" rule: say the word and it really changes things; say nothing and it never touches them on its own. To make that rule hold, a few gates stand in front of every action. Approval mode (\"Settings → Approval mode\" sets the default; a single Chat can be changed at any time from the Approval mode selector by the input, and a Chat you've changed stops following the default): Mode Meaning --- --- Manual Approval Running commands and reaching the network both ask you first Auto Approval Only hits on risky rules ask; everything else goes straight through Full access Commands and network access stop asking; but the model still pops a multiple-choice question at you when it lacks a condition. Catastrophic commands are still blocked dead by the backend, and this level is marked red in the selector Night mode Fully hands-off: commands and network access don't ask, and even the model's \"I'd like to ask you something\" doesn't ask — it picks a plan itself and carries on. Good for leaving it to run and checking later Night mode's promise to you is \"nothing is waiting for a human\": the Approval dialog won't wait for your approval, and the multiple-choice question won't wait for you to come back and choose. That's why catastrophic commands stay blocked dead by the backend in every mode — that gate isn't \"ask\", it's \"not allowed\". Risky commands are judged by hard-coded rules, blocked at two layers, frontend and backend: the frontend blocks once, the backend blocks again independently, so even getting past the interface is still caught. On a hit the dialog states the command verbatim and the rule it triggered, plus a one-line note on the command's purpose generated by the model, and you decide to approve or reject; when rejected, the model gets a clear reason and is told not to retry. Catastrophic commands (wiping the root directory, formatting a disk) are forbidden unconditionally in every mode. File edits go through a diff preview: changes appear as a diff card, and only an accept writes to disk; a reject writes nothing. Settings has an \"auto-confirm file creation/modification\" switch, on by default — that is, ordinary writes don't need your accept every time; to look over every single one yourself, turn it off and per-write confirmation comes back. Action Needs Approval Goes straight through --- --- --- Running terminal commands Confirmed by rule under Manual Approval and Auto Approval Full access and Night mode Starting background tasks Same as above Full access and Night mode Web search, page fetching, browser automation Confirmed one by one under Manual Approval Auto Approval, Full access and Night mode The model asking you a question when it lacks a condition (multiple-choice dialog) Waits for your choice under Manual, Auto Approval and Full access Night mode (the model decides for itself instead) Read-only operations such as reading files, listing directories and searching code No Approval Any mode Creating and editing files Depends on the \"auto-confirm file creation/modification\" switch When that switch is on Catastrophic commands Forbidden unconditionally in any mode None Accessing credential files (keys, certificates, environment variable files) Forbidden in any mode None Transparent protection : path traversal is blocked, and sensitive system directories can't be read or written; credential files can't be accessed; Tool output over the limit is truncated automatically, and single-file operations have a size limit too; uploaded file names are sanitised. All of this takes effect quietly in the background, with nothing for you to configure. Auto Approval suits watching the screen when you already know what the model does; Full access goes one step further and doesn't even pop the risky confirmation — it only leaves \"the model asks when it lacks a condition\", which suits letting it act widely while the direction is still yours; Night mode suits leaving the screen and checking the result afterwards — precisely because it won't turn around and ask, it matters more to write the goal and acceptance criteria clearly in the task description, since a wrong direction will simply keep going. If in doubt, use Manual Approval: a click or two more, and every change to your environment passes through your permission. For which Tools exist and how to write a playbook, see Built-in tools and Skills; for Backup and storage cleanup, see Data, backups and storage."},{"t":"FAQ","u":"20-faq.html","s":"FAQ","h":["Quick lookup by symptom","The model can't connect, or the API Key is reported invalid","Why does the model not call Tools and only say what it would do?","A terminal command got blocked","Chinese characters or emoji come out garbled","LAN or phone can't connect to SLATE","There's too much data — how do I clean it up?","How do I save Tokens, or turn off AI features I don't use?","Too many sessions, and I can't bring myself to delete them","Some Tools are missing from the tool list","A custom playbook won't pass validation","How do I stop a debate half way?","Does switching Project interrupt a Chat that's running?","How do I set an access password for mobile?","How do I change the interface language?","How do I migrate data to a new computer or after a reinstall?","How do I use a generated prompt in Claude Code, Codex or Cursor?","What is it good for, and what isn't it good for?","What do those little markers on the task list mean?","A Chat is very long — will it exceed the model window?","How do I use the ink draft Grind Mode produces?","Can long-term memory get things wrong?","Who created the cards on the Whiteboard?","A Scheduled Task didn't run when its time came","I want to write a playbook for a repeated process — where do I start?","Can I use my own Skill with SLATE directly?","Still stuck?"],"x":"This page collects the questions that come up most often in use, answered as \"which page to read first\". Every one links to the page with the full story. Quick lookup by symptom What you see Read this first --- --- Errors, can't connect, Key invalid Models and API Keys The model only says what to do and won't act Agent Autopilot and Target Mode Commands, network access or file writes blocked Settings and security approvals The phone can't connect, remote control won't open Mobile remote control and LAN access Disk use is high, you want to clear history Data, backups and storage Some Tools are missing, you want to save Tokens Settings and security approvals A playbook won't save Actions playbooks New machine, reinstall, moving data Data, backups and storage Plenty of \"nothing happens\" isn't a fault: it may be an Approval dialog waiting for your click, or this run queueing up (queued tasks are visible in the task centre in the right column and above the input). Glance at the screen for anything pending before deciding it's really stuck. The model can't connect, or the API Key is reported invalid First go to \"Settings → Model configuration\" and check that this model has its API Key filled in. When you can't tell whether it's the key or the network, run the shortest request from \"Settings → API test\"; it goes down the same path as a real Chat and reports the result. For a custom endpoint, check that the Base URL and the request mode match (OpenAI-compatible / Anthropic / curl direct). For a local model, check that Ollama or LM Studio is running. See Models and API Keys. Why does the model not call Tools and only say what it would do? First check whether \"Settings → Auto-advance\" has been turned off — it exists precisely to nudge the model into making the Tool call when it only makes a statement. Then check whether Continue Autopilot is on: it keeps a run going at the last moment when rounds run out. Also look for an Approval dialog waiting for \"Approve\" — without approval the model won't go on. The full mechanism is in Agent Autopilot and Target Mode. A terminal command got blocked When a hard-coded risky rule matches (deleting files, privilege escalation, shutdown or restart, force push and the like) a dialog pops up for you to confirm, and before you approve you can read a one-line note on the command's purpose generated by the model. Catastrophic commands are forbidden unconditionally in every mode — that's by design. If the same kind of command keeps asking, switch this Chat or the default level to Auto Approval, which only stops risky ones. See Settings and security approvals. Chinese characters or emoji come out garbled Reading and editing files recognises UTF-8, UTF-8 BOM, GB18030, GBK, UTF-16 and other common encodings automatically, so Chinese, emoji and full-width punctuation are kept exactly as they are; when an old encoding can't express new characters, the Tool upgrades the write encoding safely instead of dropping characters. Terminal output gets encoding-safe handling for native commands, and Windows no longer pops a black window full of mojibake. If you still hit garbling: first check the file hasn't been rewritten by an outside program in another encoding, then read it once more with the file reading Tool; if needed, save the content again as UTF-8. LAN or phone can't connect to SLATE Check four things in order: are the phone and the computer on the same Wi-Fi; is the computer's firewall blocking the port (allow it the first time); is the address the one shown in Settings (it must carry the auth code); has the router turned on a guest network or AP isolation. If Settings says \"remote service not started\", restart the app. See Mobile remote control and LAN access. There's too much data — how do I clean it up? Go to \"Settings → Data backup → Storage\" and look at the total and the breakdown first. After deleting a batch of sessions you can click \"Compress\" to actually get the space back; to start over completely, \"Clear chats\" deletes only sessions and messages and leaves long-term memory and preferences alone. When the built-in browser cache grows, click \"Clear cache\". Export a Backup first. See Data, backups and storage. How do I save Tokens, or turn off AI features I don't use? Go to \"Settings → AI assistance\": it lists the 15 Token-consuming features outside Chat, each switchable on its own, and once off not a single request goes out for it. If you only Chat and run Tools day to day, compression Summary, memory distillation, conversation insight, automatic chat titles, prompt polish, image and video generation can all be off first. A tally above the list shows how many are already off. See Settings and security approvals. Too many sessions, and I can't bring myself to delete them Every row in the \"Tasks\" column has \"Archive\" among its actions: press it and the session leaves the Tasks column without a single message touched — archive isn't delete. The one generating can't be archived; stop it first. To get it back, go to \"Settings → Archived chats\" and \"Restore\" it one by one and it reappears in the Tasks column; the \"Delete\" there is the one that wipes the messages too, with no way back. See Data, backups and storage. Some Tools are missing from the tool list First look at the matching switches under \"Settings → AI assistance\": with sub-agents, image generation and video generation off, the Tools disappear from the tool catalogue outright, so the model can't see them and won't call them. Image and video generation also need a model and API Key set in the \"Image generation / Video generation\" blocks; without them they're unavailable too. If an external MCP Server is attached, its Tools are temporarily gone while the connection is down. See Built-in tools and Skills. A custom playbook won't pass validation Playbooks use a minimal format: 2-space indent, block arrays, literal block scalars. Tab indentation, anchors, multiple documents and the like are all rejected, and the error states \"line N: reason\", so fixing along that line number gets you through. It won't save until validation passes; the editor validates as you write. Full fields and syntax are in Actions playbooks. How do I stop a debate half way? The team panel has a stop button; one click interrupts the remaining speakers, and what has already been said is kept in full — no results lost. The run also goes into team history marked \"aborted\", and you can open it and keep reading — a run with no conclusion never gets one invented for it. After stopping you can keep asking, or open a new run with another set of roles. See AI Team and Workflows. Does switching Project interrupt a Chat that's running? By default, no. The one you left keeps generating in the background, and its progress and outcome land in the task centre in the right column; within one Project runs are serial by default, across Projects they can be parallel, and how many run at once is set under \"Settings → Multi-tasking and Projects\". If you'd rather have the old \"switching interrupts\" behaviour back, turn the switch in that block off. See Projects, workspaces and per-project configuration. How do I set an access password for mobile? SLATE's LAN access isn't protected by \"setting a password\" but by the auth code carried in the address: only a device coming in through the address shown in Settings or its QR code gets access, and access without the auth code is stopped automatically. So the way to protect it is this — don't send the address with its auth code, or the QR code, to anyone; sending it out is handing over the key. When you don't need remote control, close SLATE on the computer and the address stops working. See Mobile remote control and LAN access. How do I change the interface language? The interface language is chosen at install (Simplified Chinese or English), and there's no in-app switch while it runs, so changing it means running the installer again and picking the other language. Switching language only affects interface copy; model output and your input are never translated. For details see Settings and security approvals. How do I migrate data to a new computer or after a reinstall? Three steps: on the old machine, \"Settings → Data backup → Download Backup\" to get a JSON; on the new machine, \"Settings → Data backup\", click \"Import Backup\" and pick it; confirm and the page reloads itself. Import merges by ID, so existing data is skipped, but local preferences are replaced by the copy in the Backup. Knowledge base documents, playbooks, Expert Packs and custom Skills aren't in that JSON — bring the local data directory across as well. See Data, backups and storage. How do I use a generated prompt in Claude Code, Codex or Cursor? Prompt Factory produces a plain-text Prompt you can hand over: copy the whole thing and paste it into the external Coding Agent's dialog or Project instructions file, with no format changes needed. To make it fit a particular Tool's habits, write the delivery requirements and constraints clearly in the factory before generating. See Prompt Factory. What is it good for, and what isn't it good for? It's good for turning an idea into a structured plan, for driving local Tools to get work done, for writing a repeated process down as a playbook you can reuse, and for producing a high-quality Prompt for an external Coding Agent to execute. It isn't good as a pure cloud assistant, for multi-person online collaboration, or for exposing LAN remote control on an untrusted public network. See What SLATE is. What do those little markers on the task list mean? Every session gets a marker for where it stands: needs action (the last run was stopped or there's a next step), error, in progress (with a breathing animation, only while this session is really generating), finished but unread (cleared the moment you open it). The marker records how the last run ended; if the session was continued from the phone's remote control, the old marker is voided automatically. See Interface overview: the three-column workspace. A Chat is very long — will it exceed the model window? It won't overflow straight away. When the context budget is used up, old messages are compressed into one Summary automatically, and both the compression threshold and the usage bar's denominator are calculated from the budget you set for this model. To compress once by hand, click \"Compress\" in the Chat. The budget is adjusted under \"Settings → Context and Projects\" and in \"max context\" on the model row, and a local endpoint's real window can be probed in one click. See Settings and security approvals. How do I use the ink draft Grind Mode produces? An ink draft has three buttons: send it to Target Mode to execute, put it on the Whiteboard to reason it out, or save it as a template for reuse. Want to look before deciding — put it on the Whiteboard; want to start right away — send it to Target Mode. See Grind Mode. Can long-term memory get things wrong? It can, which is why it can be edited and deleted. In the memory panel, edit the content or delete entries one by one; once outdated information is corrected, later Chats recall the new content. With automatic memory distillation on, after every round of reply it quietly reads the recent Chat once, and doesn't repeat within 45 seconds. See Memory, Profile and Knowledge. Who created the cards on the Whiteboard? Two sources: ones the model creates while working (tool execution is recorded as step cards with status colours), and ones you drag out yourself. Cards, connections and step cards can all be edited and cleaned up by you. See Whiteboard-style logic chains. A Scheduled Task didn't run when its time came First check the task is enabled, then check whether \"Scheduled and event tasks\" under \"Settings → AI assistance\" has been turned off — tasks stop running once it's off, but trigger records are kept. The result is archived in its own session with a prefix; find that one in the session list. See Task list and Scheduled Tasks. I want to write a playbook for a repeated process — where do I start? Write it as an Actions playbook; in agent mode the model sees the catalogue automatically, reads the full text when it needs it and then follows the process. A bad version doesn't vanish into thin air — it's listed together with the reason, and every overwrite or delete is kept with a one-click rollback. See Actions playbooks. Can I use my own Skill with SLATE directly? Yes. Write the Skill as a SKILL.md and it's recognised; mention it with @ in Chat afterwards and it's injected into that message's Context. An Expert Pack, by contrast, is the whole set of \"persona + rules + knowledge + Skills\", importable and exportable. See Built-in tools and Skills and Expert Packs. Still stuck? If none of the pages above solved it, file an Issue on the Project's GitHub repository with your OS version, the steps you took and the error text verbatim — that makes it much easier to pin down: - Repository: https://github.com/CaryWang1234/SLATE - You can also open it from \"Settings → About\" with the \"GitHub\" button."}],"zh":[{"t":"认识 SLATE","u":"zh/01-what-is-slate.html","s":"开始使用","h":["SLATE 是什么","从灵感到落地","它解决什么问题","核心能力一览","它和外部 Coding Agent 的关系","设计原则","你的数据在哪里","适合谁","不适合谁","术语速查","下一步"],"x":"SLATE（砚）是一台跑在你自己电脑上的 AI 协作调度台。它把粗糙的灵感研磨成结构化方案，再决定是自己动手执行，还是产出一份高质量 Prompt 交给外部的 Coding Agent。 这页帮你建立整体印象：它解决什么问题、有哪些能力、适合谁用。 SLATE 是什么 SLATE 是本地优先的 AI 协作工具，专注于提示词工程、上下文管理与项目灵感整理。它把主流大模型、内置工具、团队辩论、工作流、白板式逻辑链整合在一个界面里。 它有两副面孔，你可以按场景选： 1. 自己动手 —— 直接驱动内置工具完成任务：读项目、写文件、跑命令、生成文档与图表。 2. 产出 Prompt —— 把上下文、约束、验收标准整合成一份可交付的 Prompt，交给 Claude Code、Codex、Cursor 这类外部 Coding Agent 执行。 灵感负责发散，SLATE 负责把灵感收成一份能落地的方案。 从灵感到落地 一个想法从模糊到交付，通常会走成这样： 1. 磨墨 —— 把一句粗糙想法交给磨墨模式，AI 追问目标、受众、交付物与验收标准，最后收成一份墨稿。 2. 推演 —— 把墨稿投到白板，卡片加连线把依赖与顺序摆清楚；也可以先让 AI 团队辩一轮。 3. 执行 —— 打开目标模式，让模型按六阶段自主推进、逐项验证，直到全部了结才收口。 4. 外送 —— 如果这活更适合外部 Coding Agent，用提示词工厂把上下文与约束整合成一份 Prompt 交出去。 每一步都可以只用其中一环，不必走完全程。 它解决什么问题 只给你一个聊天窗口的 AI 工具，意味着下面四件事都得你自己扛。SLATE 把这些活接了过去。 常见麻烦 SLATE 的做法 --- --- 上下文管理 会话历史全文搜索与导出、超阈值自动压缩、长期记忆跨会话召回、知识库按需注入 提示词工程 提示词工厂把宪法、上下文、约束整合成一份 Prompt；磨墨模式把模糊想法问成结构化任务书 项目级操作 打开项目后可读文件、改代码、跑命令、查 Git 状态，不必复制粘贴来回搬 Token 消耗 智能压缩、分级调用、轻量模型负责讨论重型模型负责决策、AI 辅助功能逐项可关 核心能力一览 能力 是什么 一句话价值 --- --- --- 多模型对话 国内外主流模型 + 任意 OpenAI 兼容端点 + 本地模型，统一在一个对话界面 换模型不用换工具 Agent Autopilot 与目标模式 识别到查项目、改文件、跑命令、排查 bug 这类请求就自动进入多轮 Agent 循环；目标模式是显式六阶段、更强闭环的版本 不用反复发「继续」，干完自己收口 磨墨模式 一句粗糙想法，AI 三段式追问后研磨成任务书 想法还糊的时候，先把它磨清楚再动手 MCP 工具与技能 内置三十多个工具，另可用 SKILL.md 扩展新能力 模型能真的操作你的文件与命令行 Actions 流程说明书 把「干某件事的必要流程」写成一份说明书，模型按流程推进 重复流程沉淀一次，之后照着走 专家包 人格 + 规则 + 知识 + 技能打包成一个包，可导入导出 一个领域专家配一次，处处复用 AI 团队与工作流 多角色多轮辩论（提案 / 反驳 / 决策），以及 DAG 工作流流水线 重要决策先辩一轮，再动手 白板 卡片加连线整理思路，工具执行自动记成步骤卡 把推演过程摊在一屏上看 记忆与知识库 自动沉淀对话要点、跨会话召回，对话结束还能捕获技术洞察 不用每次重新交代背景 提示词工厂 项目上下文、任务描述、约束一键整合为可交付 Prompt 交给外部 Coding Agent 的 Prompt 从这儿出 移动端遥控 手机 / 平板浏览器打开局域网地址即进入专属移动端界面 离开电脑也能看进度、下指令 它和外部 Coding Agent 的关系 SLATE 不替代 Claude Code、Codex、Cursor，它站在它们前面。 - 直接在 SLATE 里干活：适合需要读项目、跑命令、反复验证的任务，全程不用离开这个界面。 - 在 SLATE 里备料：适合交给外部 Agent 的大改造。它先把项目上下文、约束条件、验收标准整理成一份结构清晰的 Prompt，你复制走即可。 两种用法共用同一份记忆、同一份项目宪法与同一批专家包。 设计原则 - 纯黑白灰视觉 —— 界面本身不用彩色渐变与装饰性阴影，注意力留给内容；唯一带颜色的是各家模型的品牌 mark，按官方原色画，不跟主题改。 - 原生技术栈 —— 零 npm、零构建工具，界面即文件。 - 本地优先 —— 所有数据存在本机，不经过任何云端中转。 - Token 节约 —— 智能压缩、分级调用、静默处理，不浪费每一次请求。 - 永不卡死 —— 空闲看门狗、零内容自动重试、请求超时、界面兜底多层防线。 你的数据在哪里 对话历史、长期记忆、知识文档、专家包、动作流程、定时任务与各项设置，全部以本机文件形式保存。API Key 保存在本机（本地加密存储），只用于调用你自己配置的模型。 具体包括： - 每一场对话与每一条消息； - 长期记忆条目与用户画像； - 知识文档与文档归档； - 自建技能、专家包、流程说明书； - 定时 / 事件任务与执行记录； - 模型、审批、主题、排版等各项偏好。 这些内容的备份、恢复与清理，统一在设置里的数据相关区块处理，也可以一键导出成一个文件换机迁移。 SLATE 本身是本地应用，但模型推理发生在你选择的模型服务商那里。所以对话时需要能访问你配置的模型端点；内置的联网搜索、网页抓取、更新检查也各需要网络。 适合谁 - 手上有想法、但不知道怎么把它写成可执行方案的人。 - 已经在用 Claude Code / Codex / Cursor，想先在本地产出一份高质量 Prompt 的人。 - 需要一个长期记住项目背景、跨会话不忘事的 AI 工作台的人。 - 在意数据留在本机、不想把项目内容交给云端协作平台的人。 - 习惯本地模型（Ollama / LM Studio），想把本地与在线模型放在同一个界面里的人。 不适合谁 - 想找一个替代 IDE 的人：SLATE 负责协作者与调度，不提供编辑器、调试器、补全等完整 IDE 能力。 - 想找云端多人协作平台的人：SLATE 是本机应用，没有账号体系与云端共享空间。 - 希望开箱即用、不想自备 API Key 的人：模型额度需要你自己准备。 - 想用它做大模型本身的人：SLATE 是调度台，不是训练框架。 术语速查 术语 含义 --- --- Agent Autopilot 默认的自动推进层：识别到环境操作类请求就自动进入多轮 Agent 循环，干完自动收口 目标模式 显式开启的强闭环模式，按「目标 → 计划 → 执行 → 验证 → 汇报 → 追溯」六阶段推进，轮数预算更大 磨墨 把粗糙想法研磨成结构化任务书的过程，三段式追问，最多十轮 Actions 流程说明书，写明「干某件事的必要流程」，模型按需读取后照流程推进 Skill 用一份 SKILL.md 描述的可插拔能力，聊天中提及即注入上下文 MCP 模型调用外部工具的协议层，SLATE 内置工具与外部 MCP Server 都由此接入 专家包 人格、规则、知识、技能打包成的可分发配置 砚流 模型逐 token 写出工具参数时，界面实时演出参数成形过程的预览机制 下一步 装好它，然后跑起来：安装与启动。"},{"t":"安装与启动","u":"zh/02-install.html","s":"开始使用","h":["系统要求","方式一：Windows 安装包","方式二：macOS 安装包","方式三：从源码运行","三种方式怎么选","首次启动会发生什么","安装后第一件事","启动时自动检查更新","卸载与你的数据","启动不上怎么办"],"x":"这页带你装好 SLATE 并跑起来，同时说明首次启动会发生什么、你的数据放在哪里。 系统要求 项目 要求 --- --- Windows 桌面版 直接使用安装包，无需额外准备运行环境 macOS 桌面版 直接使用 .dmg ，无需额外准备运行环境 源码运行 Python 3.13 或更高版本 源码运行平台 Windows / Linux / macOS 都可以 网络 首次启动后需要联网调用你配置的模型 浏览器 使用源码方式时，用较新版本的 Chrome / Edge / Firefox 打开本机地址 方式一：Windows 安装包 适合大多数使用者，装完即用，不需要装 Python。 1. 前往项目的 GitHub Releases 页面，下载最新版安装包 SLATE-Setup-x.x.x.exe 。 2. 双击运行安装程序。 3. 安装过程中选择界面语言： 简体中文 或 English 。 4. 安装完成后从开始菜单或桌面快捷方式启动。 语言在安装那一步就定下来了，之后界面与提示文字全程使用该语言。想换语言，重新运行一次安装程序选择即可。 同一份安装只会跑一个 SLATE。窗口已经开着（或已经缩进系统通知区域）时再启动一次，不会多出一个窗口、也不会多起一份后端：新启动的那个会把正在跑的窗口唤回前台，然后自己退掉。锁随进程消失，崩溃或被任务管理器结束都不会留下\"再也打不开\"的状态。 方式二：macOS 安装包 苹果电脑用 .dmg ，装完即用，同样不需要装 Python。 1. 前往项目的 GitHub Releases 页面，下载最新版 SLATE-Setup-x.x.x.dmg （同目录有 SLATE-Setup-x.x.x.dmg.sha256 ，想核对一下再装可以用 shasum -a 256 -c ）。 2. 双击挂载，把「SLATE 砚」拖进「应用程序」文件夹。 3. 首次启动 ：在访达里右键（或长按）SLATE 图标 → 「打开」 → 再点一次「打开」。 4. 此后就能正常双击启动。 .dmg 由 CI 直接产出，没有 Apple 开发者证书签名，也没有做公证，所以第一次打开会被 Gatekeeper 拦一句\"无法验证开发者\"。上面第 3 步的右键 → 打开就是绕过它的正规做法，只需要做一次。想校验下载是否完整，比对同名的 .sha256 文件即可。 装机版把对话历史、API Key、后端日志和 WebView 的个人资料都放在 ~/Library/Application Support/SLATE ，不在 .app 包里。升级就是整个替换 .app ，你的数据不会被一起带走。源码运行仍然把数据放在仓库的 data/ 。 Mac 上关掉窗口就是退出应用；连续启动两次会开出两份。这两条后续再补。 方式三：从源码运行 适合想自己改点东西、或使用 Linux 的人（macOS 也能这样跑，只是没必要——有现成的 .dmg ）。 Windows： Linux / macOS： 启动完成后，在浏览器里打开： 也可以直接运行桌面入口 python desktop.py ，以桌面窗口的方式打开同一个应用。 三种方式怎么选 对比项 Windows 安装包 macOS 安装包 从源码运行 --- --- --- --- 需要 Python 不需要 不需要 需要 Python 3.13+ 适用平台 Windows macOS Windows / Linux / macOS 升级方式 下载新版安装包覆盖安装 下载新版 .dmg 拖一次 拉取最新代码后重启 改动源码 不适合 不适合 适合，改完重启即生效 适合谁 只想起草想法、跑任务的人 只想起草想法、跑任务的人 想折腾界面与内部流程的人 首次启动会发生什么 1. 主窗口打开后，会先弹出一份 新手引导 ，用几张卡片带你认识多模型对话、目标模式、磨墨模式、内置工具、记忆与知识库、其他面板这几个入口。 2. 点「开始使用」关闭引导，进入主界面。 3. 主界面顶栏有五个页签： 对话 / 黑板 / 扩展 / 工厂 / 设置 ，每次显示其中一块。默认停在「对话」，这一块内部再分左右两栏：左侧边栏放项目与任务，右侧是对话消息流与输入框，消息区右缘按需滑出任务清单与后台任务面板。技能、工具、模式、MCP、专家包、Actions 这些「模型能用什么」都收在「扩展」那一页。 新手引导只看一次。之后想重看，去 设置 → 关于 ，点里面的「新手引导」按钮。 安装后第一件事 SLATE 装好时还没有任何模型可用，先去配置 API Key： 1. 点顶栏右侧的 密钥 按钮，或直接切到 设置 页签。 2. 在「模型配置」里找到你要用的模型，填入对应的 API Key 并保存。 3. 想接入别家端点，点同一区块的「+ 添加模型」，填写模型 ID 与 API 地址。 4. 回到对话界面，在输入区的模型下拉框里选中刚配置好的模型。 配置细节与各家模型清单见模型接入与 API Key。 SLATE 本体跑在本机，但它要调用你配置的模型来完成对话与推理。所以第一次使用前，请确认这台电脑能访问你选择的服务端点，并且该 API Key 有可用额度。没有配好模型时，你能看到界面与历史记录，但发出去的消息不会得到回复。 API Key 保存在本机的应用配置里，不需要你另外记到笔记或项目文件里。请避免把它写进会提交到代码仓库、或会分享给别人的文档中；一旦泄漏，请立刻到服务商后台吊销并重新生成。 启动时自动检查更新 SLATE 每次启动都会检查一次新版本： - 发现新版本时，顶栏下方会出现一条提示横幅，上面给出下载与查看发行说明的入口。 - 横幅右上角可以关闭，选择「忽略该版本」后不再为这个版本反复提示。 - 也可以随时手动检查： 设置 → 关于 ，点「检查更新」，那里同时显示你当前的版本号。 安装包来源始终是项目的 GitHub Releases，SLATE 不会从别处下载可执行文件。 卸载与你的数据 卸载 ：Windows 下用系统的「应用」列表卸载 SLATE 即可，卸载程序负责移除应用本体。 数据 ：对话历史、记忆、知识文档、专家包、动作流程与各项设置都以本机文件形式保存，不上传云端。所以： - 想换电脑，先做一次导出备份，在新机器上导入即可（见数据、备份与存储）。 - 想彻底清理，也建议先导出一次备份确认无误，再动手删除。 - 卸载应用与删除数据是两件事，请按你的需要分别处理。 启动不上怎么办 - 双击图标后什么都没出现：多半已经有一个 SLATE 在跑（窗口可能正缩在系统通知区域）。再启动一次只会把那个窗口唤回来，不会开出第二个；到通知区域或任务栏里找它。 - 源码方式启动失败时，先确认 Python 版本不低于 3.13，依赖已经安装完整，再重新运行一次启动脚本。 - 命令行里留意启动过程的报错信息，多数问题在最后几行就能看出原因。 - 界面能打开、但消息发出去没有回复，多半是模型密钥或网络问题。去 设置 → API 测试 ，用一条最短请求验证链路是否可用。 仍然不通时，对照常见问题排查。"},{"t":"界面总览：页签与三栏工作区","u":"zh/03-interface.html","s":"开始使用","h":["整体布局","顶栏","左侧边栏","项目页签","任务页签","总结页签","中间对话区","输入区","＋ 模式菜单","主题与外观","移动端会自动换一套界面","新手引导"],"x":"这页把主界面从头到尾认一遍，让你知道每个入口在哪、点下去会发生什么。 先记住一句话：SLATE 由 一个顶栏 + 四块工作区 组成，每次只显示一块，点顶栏页签即切换。 整体布局 区域 位置 能干什么 --- --- --- 对话 顶栏页签「对话」 与模型对话、跑 Agent 循环、看工具卡片与任务清单、管理会话历史 黑板 顶栏页签「黑板」 卡片加连线理思路，切换 Git 树 / 流程 / 看板 / 纲要 / 工作流等视图 扩展 顶栏页签「扩展」 管理模型能用什么：技能、内置工具、模式、新功能（自产工具）、MCP、专家包、Actions 七栏 工厂 顶栏页签「工厂」 把项目上下文、任务、约束整合成一份可交付的 Prompt 设置 顶栏页签「设置」 模型密钥、上下文、审批模式、备份、局域网与关于 设置是一页独立的配置区，不算工作区。 习惯上说的「三栏」不在顶栏，而是 对话 这块工作区自己分出来的三列： 左侧边栏 放项目、任务与总结三页签， 中间消息流 放对话内容与输入框， 右缘 按需滑出任务清单栏、后台任务面板与墨迹面板。团队对话是消息流里的模式切换，不是第四块工作区。 界面元素比较密集，第一次用建议按这个顺序逛一遍： 顶栏 → 四块工作区 → 对话区左侧边栏 → 消息区 → 输入区 。每个位置停一下，看看有什么按钮就够了，不必一次记住全部。 顶栏 顶栏横贯整个窗口，分三段： - 左侧 ：应用标识（砚 · SLATE）。 - 中央 ：五个页签 —— 对话 / 黑板 / 扩展 / 工厂 / 设置 ，用来切换工作区。 - 右侧 ：四个圆形按钮。 顶栏按钮 作用 --- --- 密钥 直接跳到设置里的「模型配置」，管理模型密钥 遥控 直接跳到设置里的「局域网遥控」，查看手机端访问地址 明暗 一键切换亮色 / 暗色主题；自定义主题开着时会变淡并锁定，点它会说明原因 设置 打开设置页 发现新版本时，提示横幅会出现在顶栏正下方，带下载入口与「忽略该版本」。 顶栏右上的「密钥」和「遥控」是两份快捷方式，点下去打开的还是同一个设置页，只是自动滚到对应区块。所以记不住位置时，从顶栏这两个按钮进最快。 左侧边栏 对话区左侧是边栏，顶部有三个页签： 项目 / 任务 / 总结 。边栏与消息区之间的分隔条可以拖动，用来调整宽度。 项目页签 - 顶部是当前项目名与操作入口；项目是工作区时会有「工作区」标记。 - 下面是项目文件树，点文件可以在下方直接预览内容。 - 在册项目列表里可以切换项目，也可以打开一个新的目录。 任务页签 这里是你所有会话的入口： 元素 说明 --- --- ＋ 新建对话 开一场新会话，快捷键 Ctrl + N 排序下拉 最近更新 / 按项目 / 按状态 / 创建时间 / 按用量 管理 批量管理历史会话 搜索框 搜索历史会话，命中即显上下文摘录 会话列表 每条会话带自己的状态标志 状态标志一共四种 ，记的是「上一场是怎么结束的」： 标志 含义 --- --- 需要操作 上一场被停止，或还有下一步等你决定 出错 上一场以失败告终 进行中 这一场此刻真的在生成，图标带呼吸动画 已完成未查看 跑完了但你还没点进去看过；点进会话即清除 排序偏好跨设备同步；状态标志只在你自己的机器上流转，不会跑到别的设备。 总结页签 把当前这一场会话的用量汇总在一处，方便你看这场花掉了多少。 中间对话区 消息区的标题栏左侧是 对话 / 团队 两个模式切换：日常问答、写代码在「对话」里；要让多个角色辩论或跑工作流，切到「团队」。 标题栏右侧是一排动作按钮： 按钮 作用 --- --- 手动压缩上下文 不等自动阈值，立刻把历史对话压缩一次 记忆与画像 查看和管理长期记忆、用户画像 专家包管理 导入、导出、编辑专家包 提示词素材 管理可复用的提示词片段 任务清单 收起或展开消息区右侧的任务清单栏 后台任务 查看后台终端任务的状态与输出 标题栏下方是一条用量信息栏，显示这一场会话的 Token 消耗。 消息区主体 从上到下依次是： - 对话消息流，每条回复带模型名与用量； - 工具调用卡片，显示工具名、参数与执行状态； - 思考过程折叠面板，模型推理时实时展开，思考完成后自动折叠； - 右侧的任务清单栏，大任务跑起来时逐条显示进度，可整栏收起； - 后台终端任务面板与磨墨墨迹面板，只在对应场景出现。 输入区 输入区在消息区底部，由一行多行输入框加一行工具栏组成。 输入框 ：支持多行。 Enter 发送， Shift + Enter 换行。 工具栏左侧 ，从「＋」按钮开始依次是： 控件 说明 --- --- ＋ 模式菜单 集中所有模式入口，见下一节 回复模式 输入框左下的模式胶囊，决定这一场怎么干活：内置智能 / 对话 / 经济 / 狂暴四档，各有图标与颜色，也可是自定义模式；切换只改这一场，默认档与自定义模式在「扩展 → 模式」里管 审批模式 这一场什么时候该停下来问你，四档可随时改：手动审批 / 自动审批 / 完全访问（胶囊标红）/ 夜间模式；选到「夜间模式」就什么都不问了 添加附件 附带文件一起发送 专家包 选一个专家包，人格与规则全程生效 模型 当前这一场用哪个模型 推理强度 按模型能力给出可选档位，档位下方标出对应墨色；这个模型接不住的档位会置灰并写明原因 工具栏右侧 依次是优化提示词、语音输入与发送。生成过程中，发送按钮会变成 停止 ——点下去是真的取消，不是只让界面安静下来。 点 优化提示词 （语音按钮左边那颗星）会把输入框里写了一半的草稿交给当前模型改写一遍，但改写结果不直接进输入框：先与原文并排放在弹窗里给你比对，只有点 采用 才写回。点 保留原文 、按 Esc、点弹窗外面，都算不采用，输入框一个字都不会变。等结果的那几秒如果你又改了草稿（包括语音实时写进去的字），这次结果会被放弃，不会盖掉你新写的内容。用哪个模型可以在设置 → AI 辅助功能里单独指定，关掉后一次请求都不发。 ＋ 模式菜单 点输入框左侧的「＋」，弹出「模式与功能」，里面是四行模式加一组提及： 这个菜单钉在「＋」按钮的正上方，和同一条工具栏上的审批模式、推理强度是一个手感：再按一次「＋」、点菜单以外的任何地方、或按 Esc，都会把它收起。 - 磨墨模式 —— 把粗糙想法研磨成任务书，行内小字会显示当前研磨进度。 - 头脑风暴 —— 一个开关。 - 目标模式 —— 一个开关，显式六阶段强闭环。 - 定时任务 —— 创建与管理定时 / 事件任务。 - 提及（@ 前缀） —— 技能 / 工具 / MCP / 文件，点任意一项会弹出对应的候选列表，选中后以提及的形式写进消息。 主题与外观 顶栏的「明暗」按钮一键切换亮色与暗色，切换立即生效。这个偏好跟着你的设置一起同步，换一台设备访问同一个 SLATE 也是同一个主题。 想彻底换一张脸，去 设置 → 自定义主题 ：四个源色（底色 / 面板 / 正文 / 强调）加正文与代码两套字体（也能导入你自己的字体文件），再加板块透明度、一张本机背景图与遮罩浓度，10 套基础预设可以直接点；装了 Wallpaper Engine 的话还能把它正在用的那张壁纸预览图取过来当背景。其余三十几个颜色令牌由这四个源色推导，推导时按对比度兜底，所以配色不会糊成一片读不清。 开启自定义主题期间明暗切换会被锁住——两套色同时说话只会互相矛盾。按钮不会变灰，点下去会告诉你为什么没换，以及去哪里关掉它。相关细节见 设置与安全审批。 移动端会自动换一套界面 用手机或平板浏览器打开局域网地址时，SLATE 会自动进入移动端界面，底部换成五个 Tab。桌面浏览器访问仍然得到完整桌面界面。详见移动端遥控与局域网访问。 新手引导 首次启动会弹出一份引导，用卡片介绍最常用的几个入口。想重看，去 设置 → 关于 ，点「新手引导」按钮。"},{"t":"模型接入与 API Key","u":"zh/04-models.html","s":"开始使用","h":["在哪配置","预设模型","添加自定义模型","使用本地模型","API Key 与数据安全","上下文预算怎么定","思考强度怎么选","Responses API 模式","轻量模型与重型模型的分工","切换模型与用量显示","常见问题"],"x":"这页教你把模型接进来：预设模型怎么填密钥、自定义端点怎么加、本地模型怎么指过去，以及上下文预算和用量怎么看。 在哪配置 两个入口，通向同一页： - 顶栏右侧的 密钥 按钮； - 顶栏页签 设置 ，再在左侧导航里选 模型配置 。 「模型配置」区块里有三块内容： 自定义模型 列表、 内置模型密钥 列表，以及 Responses API 模式开关。每一行模型后面都跟着一个「最大上下文」（滑杆 + 精确数字框 + 探测窗口按钮，见下文「上下文预算怎么定」）。 预设模型 SLATE 自带一份模型清单，分四组出现在输入区的模型下拉框里： 分组 包含 --- --- 国际模型 GPT 系列、Claude 系列、Gemini 系列 国产模型 DeepSeek、Kimi、Qwen、GLM、Doubao、MiniMax、ERNIE 本地模型 内置一条本地模型条目，供 Ollama / LM Studio 使用 自定义 你自己添加的端点 下拉里每一条前面的圆点是密钥状态： 已配置 为实心， 未配置 为空。鼠标停在选项上还能看到模型 ID、接口地址与该模型是否支持 Responses API。 圆点旁边是这一家的品牌 mark。内置模型和你自己添加的模型走同一套判定：按 模型名称 认，名字里认不出品牌时才看 接口地址 ——所以 deepseek-r1:1.5b 跑在本机 Ollama 上仍然画 DeepSeek 的标，而不是 Ollama 的。认不出来就什么也不画，宁可留白，也不会拿别家的 logo 顶上去。mark 是随包分发的各家官方图形：47 家有彩色原件的直接按品牌自己的颜色画，另外 11 家（OpenAI、Anthropic、xAI、Ollama、Z.ai 等）包里只有单色版，画一枚中性灰。两支都不跟主题走——「扩展 → 外观」里挑的墨色与强调色染不到品牌色，明暗模式切换下也不会糊成一团黑。GLM（智谱）旁边画的是 Z.ai 的标：这家把对外标识换成了 Z.ai，我们跟着新标走，包里它只有单色版。 具体有哪些型号以你界面里列出的为准。给某个模型配密钥的步骤： 1. 在「内置模型密钥」里找到它。 2. 在输入框里粘贴 API Key，点右侧的保存按钮。 3. 已配置的输入框会提示「已配置」；清空内容再保存即可删除该密钥。 添加自定义模型 只要你的服务商提供 OpenAI 兼容接口，就能接进来。 1. 点「模型配置」右上角的 + 添加模型 。 2. 填写这些字段： 字段 说明 --- --- 模型名称 / ID 服务商要求的模型标识，也是下拉框里显示的字 请求模式 OpenAI 兼容、Anthropic、或 curl 直连三种 Base URL 你的接口地址 API Key 可选；本地或免鉴权的端点可以留空 Context Window 该模型能容纳的上下文规模 3. 点「添加」保存，它会出现在模型下拉框的「自定义」分组里。 新加一个端点后，先去 设置 → API 测试 打一发最短请求。通了再把它设成常用模型，能省下不少「是模型不行还是配置写错了」的来回猜。 使用本地模型 想让推理完全留在本机，用 Ollama 或 LM Studio 都可以。 - 模型清单里已经有一条 本地模型 条目，默认指向本机 Ollama 的兼容地址（默认端口 11434）。 - 用 LM Studio 时，把它显示的监听地址填进该条目的 Base URL。 - 端口如果你自己改过，请按实际监听的端口填写。 - 本地模型不校验 API Key，下拉里会标注为本地模型。 本地模型的地址指向你自己的电脑，不需要外网。所以断网也能用它；反过来，模型跑不跑得动、上下文能开多大，取决于你这台机器的显卡与内存。 API Key 与数据安全 - API Key 保存在本机（本地加密存储），只用于调用你自己配置的模型。 - 密钥不会写进对话记录，也不会随项目文件一起落到你的工作目录里。 - 备份导出时可以一并带走，导入到新机器后继续使用。 不要把 API Key 抄进项目文件、笔记或截图里。它会随代码仓库、聊天记录一起传播出去；一旦怀疑泄漏，立刻到服务商后台吊销并重新生成一把。 上下文预算怎么定 每个模型都有自己的上下文预算。开得越大，单次请求能带的材料越多，消耗也越高。 - 模型配置里每一行的 最大上下文 由三件组成：按这个模型的窗口生成的滑杆、一个精确数字框、一个 探测窗口 按钮。 - 选 自动 时按这个模型自己的标称窗口 ×0.8 取值，不再吸附到公共档位。只有 8K 窗口的本地模型因此拿到 6553，而不是凑不满最低一档、把整个窗口占满到没地方写回复。 - 数字框保留你填的精确值（131072 就是 131072），只在 1K–4M 区间内夹取；选的值超出这个模型的窗口时会自动封顶，读数上会同时显示你选的值与实际生效的值。 - 本地与自定义端点（Ollama / llama.cpp / vLLM / OpenAI 兼容）可以点 探测窗口 ，直接问这台服务器它实际能吃多少。探到的值单独存一张覆盖表，参与自动档与封顶，随时可一键清除，回到端点自带的值；探不到时如实说明「这个端点没有暴露上下文窗口」，不会替你编一个数。 - 既没填标称窗口、也探测不到的模型，自动档才回落到全局的「上下文 Token 上限」（在 设置 → 上下文与项目 里改）。 自动压缩的触发阈值与界面上那根上下文条，都按同一个数字计算，不会出现「条满了却不压缩」的错位。上下文条可以直接点，点一下跳到对应模型的这一行。 思考强度怎么选 输入框左侧的档位胶囊管的是「这一场让模型想多久」（自动 / 关 / 低 / 中 / 高），每个对话各存一份。刻度不是一张固定表——SLATE 按端点能力分了九类，弹窗里只出这个模型真能接的档： - 强制思考的模型没有「关」这一档，最低就是「低」。 - 只能整体开关、不能调档的端点，刻度旁注明「低 / 中 / 高都按开」。 - 不支持思考的模型相关刻度直接置灰，并写明原因；切到该模型那一刻弹窗里会提示。 - 上游若以 400 点名这个字段，SLATE 会剥掉它重发一次——最坏是这次档位不生效，而不是模型用不了。 - 档位下方的小字（随墨 / 清墨 / 淡墨 / 浓墨 / 焦墨）只是浓淡提示；拖动时只预览，松手才生效并落盘。 Responses API 模式 「模型配置」里有一个开关： 启用 Responses API 模式 。它用新版 API 格式发请求，仅部分模型支持。 - 默认关闭，按现有格式发送请求即可正常工作。 - 只有当你明确知道自己的端点支持 Responses API、并且需要它的能力时才打开。 - 不确定就不开；开错了通常表现为请求被上游拒绝。 轻量模型与重型模型的分工 不同档位的模型可以各司其职，既省钱又不牺牲结论质量。 场景 分工 --- --- AI 团队辩论 轻量模型负责讨论与发声，重型模型负责最终决策 AI 辅助功能 压缩摘要、记忆蒸馏、代码理解、命令说明等十几项，逐项可关、可单独指定模型 自动推进审阅 在「自动推进」区块里单独指定审阅模型 这些开关与指定入口都在设置里，逐项说明见设置与安全审批。 切换模型与用量显示 - 切换 ：在输入区的模型下拉框里选，只影响这一场会话；下拉框最后一项是「+ 自定义模型…」，可以就地新增。 - 单条用量 ：每条回复都会标出所用模型与 Token 用量。 - 本场用量 ：标题栏下方的用量信息栏反映屏幕上这一场；左栏的 总结 页签给出一场的汇总。 - 累计用量 ： 设置 → 用量统计 ，看全部对话的累计消耗。 同一时刻可能有多场会话在跑，每场各记各的账，用量条只反映你眼前这一场。 常见问题 密钥填了但还是报错 去 设置 → API 测试 ，选中出问题的模型，点「开始测试」。这条请求走的是真实对话的同一条链路，能直接区分「密钥不对」「地址不对」「余额不足」还是「模型名写错了」。 下拉框里没有我要的模型 用「+ 添加模型」按自定义端点接入即可，不必等清单更新。 列表显示「暂无内置模型」 说明内置清单这次没取到。可以先手动添加自定义模型顶上，再对照常见问题排查。"},{"t":"对话与消息","u":"zh/05-chat.html","s":"对话与自动化","h":["开一场对话","消息基本操作","编辑与删除消息","四种输入方式对比","附件与多模态输入","语音输入","优化提示词","@ 提及","上下文与用量","记忆与画像的自动注入","历史会话","定时任务产出的会话"],"x":"这一页讲你在对话框里最常用到的操作：怎么开一场对话、怎么把消息发出去、怎么把文件与技能塞进这一条消息，以及 Token 都花在了哪里。 开一场对话 - 侧栏「任务」页签顶部点「＋ 新建对话」，或按 Ctrl + N 。 - 点侧栏里任意一条会话即切换过去，当前会话的完整历史重新铺开。 - 排序下拉可切换：最近更新 / 按项目 / 按状态 / 创建时间 / 按用量。排序偏好跨设备同步。 - 每条会话带一个状态标志： 需要操作 （上一场还有下一步或被停过）、 出错 、 进行中 （呼吸动画）、 已完成未查看 。点进会话即视为看过。 发送时 Enter 发送， Shift + Enter 换行。 标志记的是\"上一场怎么结束的\"：如果你后来在手机端或另一条会话里继续过它，旧标志会自动作废。 消息基本操作 - 流式输出 ：回复逐字落下，不必等整段结束就能开始读。 - 停止 ：生成期间发送按钮变成「停止」。点它是真取消——模型流与工具调用的流式通道一起关闭，后端也随之终止正在跑的子进程与任务，不会出现\"界面停了、活还在干\"。 - 重新生成 ：每条助手回复下方都有「重新生成」。它会以这条回复之前的历史重新请求，并重写这条回复之后的内容。 - 代码块一键复制 ：代码块右上角有「复制」按钮。 - 智能滚动跟随 ：滚动条在底部时输出自动跟着走；你往上翻看历史时不会被强行拉回底部。 编辑与删除消息 鼠标移到消息上会浮出操作按钮： - 「复制」把这条消息的内容放进剪贴板。 - 「重新生成」只出现在助手消息上。 - 「编辑内容」让正文就地变成输入框，保存后写回这条消息。 - 「删除此消息」会先问一次，删除后不可恢复。 四种输入方式对比 输入方式 怎么用 适合什么 --- --- --- 文字 直接打字， Enter 发送 绝大多数需求 语音 点输入框右侧的麦克风按钮口述 不想打字、边说边理思路 优化提示词 点语音按钮左边的星形按钮，让模型把草稿改写一遍 需求写得含糊，想让措辞更明确 附件 点回形针按钮选择文件 让 AI 读一份现成的文件 @ 提及 输入 @ 选候选，或从 ＋ 菜单进入对应分组 明确指定用哪个工具、技能或哪份文件 附件与多模态输入 点输入框左侧的回形针按钮添加附件，单个文件最大 10MB。 - 文本类（ .md / .html / .css / .js / .py / .json / .yaml / .log 等）：直接读取正文。 - 表格类（ .csv / .tsv ）：解析成结构化数据，而不是一整块文本。 - 图片（ .png / .jpg / .jpeg / .gif / .webp / .bmp / .svg ）：转成多模态输入交给模型看。 - 文档类（ .docx / .xlsx / .pdf ）：由 SLATE 自己解析出段落、表格与正文后再送入对话。 SLATE 在本地把文件解析成正文或结构化数据，而不是把整份原始文件塞进上下文。这一步在本地完成，不额外多花 Token。 语音输入 输入框右侧的麦克风按钮点一下开始口述，再点一下停止。 - 基于浏览器语音识别，边说边实时转写，转写结果直接落进输入框，不会打断你已有的草稿。 - 识别语言跟随界面语言：中文界面按中文识别，English 界面按英文识别。 - 需要浏览器与系统支持，Chrome / Edge 可用；首次使用需授权麦克风。 按钮只在当前浏览器支持语音识别时才显示。如果它没出现，说明这套环境不支持，请改用打字输入。 优化提示词 麦克风按钮左边那颗星形按钮，把输入框里已经写好的草稿交给模型改写一遍。它改的是 你这条还没发的话 ，不是让模型替你回答。 流程是：点一下 → 模型改写 → 弹窗里左右并排看原文与改写 → 自己决定。 - 点 采用 才写回输入框，然后你照常按 Enter 发送。 - 点 保留原文 、按 Esc 、点弹窗外面，都算不采用，输入框一个字都不会变。 - 等结果的那几秒输入框还能继续打字。如果你（或语音听写）改了草稿，这次改写结果会被放弃，不会盖掉你新写的内容。 - 模型用代码块包住结果时，取的是里面的正文，回填进来的不会有那对反引号围栏。 - 用哪个模型在这里单独指定：设置 → AI 辅助功能 → 提示词优化。关掉它，点按钮只会提示一句已关闭，一次请求都不发。 提示词里写给模型的要求是\"保持原意、不要新增你没提到的目标与细节\"，并且要求 @ 提及、代码、路径、数字原样保留。改写只是把话说清楚，不是把需求换掉。 @ 提及 在输入框输入 @ 会弹出候选；也可以点输入框左侧的 ＋ 菜单，在「提及（@ 前缀）」分组里直接打开某一类： - 技能 ：把 SKILL.md 定义的流程注入这一条消息。 - 工具 ：指定使用哪个内置工具。 - MCP ：使用外部 MCP Server 提供的工具。 - 文件 ：把项目文件的正文（或目录结构）注入这条消息，打开项目后才可用。 候选里还会出现流程说明书与专家包： @ 一个流程 id 会注入整份流程， @ 一个专家包名会注入它的人格、规则与知识文件。 完整的工具与技能清单见 内置工具与技能。 上下文与用量 对话区顶部有一条用量信息栏，鼠标悬停可以看到上下文被谁占走了： 分桶 装的是什么 --- --- 系统提示词 角色设定、项目宪法等固定内容 工具目录 这一场对话里模型能看到哪些工具 Skill 注入 被 @ 提及带进来的技能与文件内容 工具调用与结果 模型调了什么、工具回了什么 对话消息 你和模型真正说过的话 - 上下文条的分母是你为该模型设定的上下文预算，不是模型标称的窗口大小，所以它更像\"距离压缩还有多远\"的预告。 - 占用超过阈值时，SLATE 会自动把较早的对话压成一段历史摘要，最近几轮保持原样。 - 系统发给模型的催办与工具结果投喂（见 Agent Autopilot 与目标模式）只出现在模型那一侧，不占消息区；压缩之后它们仍在上下文里继续生效，也不会突然变成一条气泡。 - 摘要发给模型时仍是完整原文，只在消息区折成一条折叠条：点标题才展开，长历史不会把最近几轮挤出屏幕。 - 也可以手动压缩：点工具栏的压缩按钮，选轻度或重度两档后开始。 档位 保留什么 --- --- 轻度压缩 保留关键细节与可能被引用的文件名、数值等事实，约 50% 压缩 重度压缩 只留核心结论、关键决策与明确待办，约 80% 压缩 自动压缩只在超出阈值时发生。如果这一场的前半段夹着大量\"读文件、试错、来回确认\"的中间过程，而你接下来要谈的是另一件事，提前手动压一次比等它自动触发更划算：轻度档能保住文件名与数值，重度档只留结论、细节会丢。 记忆与画像的自动注入 长期记忆与用户画像由 SLATE 在后台挑选相关条目注入对话，不需要你手动粘贴。查看、增删与从当前对话提取记忆，见 记忆、画像与知识库。 历史会话 侧栏「任务」页签上方有「搜索历史会话」，输入关键词即全文搜索，命中会显示上下文摘录，点一下直达那条会话。新建的会话先拿首条消息的前 30 字当占位标题，跑完首轮由模型补一个短名字——只替换这种占位标题，你自己改过的名字不动（这一项可以在「设置 → AI 辅助功能」里关掉）。右侧「管理」按钮进入批量管理，可重命名、导出为 Markdown、批量删除。每一行的操作里还有一颗「归档」：它只让这一场从任务栏离场，消息一条不动，要拿回来去「设置 → 会话归档」。数据备份与导出细节见 数据、备份与存储。 定时任务产出的会话 定时与事件任务执行完会自动落成一条独立会话，标题带 [定时] 或 [事件] 前缀，方便你在侧栏里直接认出它们。任务怎么创建见 任务清单与定时任务。 需要 AI 自己动手改文件、跑命令时，可以看 Agent Autopilot 与目标模式 与 磨墨模式。"},{"t":"Agent Autopilot 与目标模式","u":"zh/06-autopilot.html","s":"对话与自动化","h":["Agent Autopilot 是什么","三种运行强度对比","循环里会发生什么","干完了就停","自动催办","Continue Autopilot","砚流：落笔即所见","停止是真取消","本轮改了哪些文件，说完就摆出来","目标模式","六阶段闭环","任务清单","四种退出方式","输出截断四层防线","一整轮只有思考、没有正文","跨项目并行"],"x":"只要你的消息是在要求 AI 查看项目、修改文件、运行命令、排查问题或提交代码，它就会自动进入 Agent 循环，把活做完再回来，不必反复发送「继续」。目标模式是同一套循环的更严格形态：显式六阶段、80 轮强闭环。 Agent Autopilot 是什么 Autopilot 是 SLATE 的默认执行层，不需要你手动开启。 - 识别到查看、修复、运行、提交、项目文件一类环境操作类请求，就自动进入多轮循环。 - 普通任务默认最多 24 轮；全面排查、项目级、多文件任务默认最多 40 轮。 - 轮数是 止损线，不是预算 ：交付并逐项验证通过后循环就该结束，而不是\"用满 24 轮\"。 - 对话里的每一条工具调用都会变成卡片，你能看到它读了哪个文件、跑了什么命令。 三种运行强度对比 运行强度 什么时候用 轮数上限 是否自动建任务清单 怎么退出 --- --- --- --- --- 普通对话 问答、写作、解释代码，不需要 AI 动你的文件 不进入工具循环 否 回复结束即结束 Agent Autopilot 修一个 bug、跑一次测试并修错、生成一批文件 普通 24 轮；全面排查 / 项目级 40 轮 复杂任务建议建，不强制 模型显式收口；未收口则标为中断 目标模式 多文件改造、完整排查、长链路任务 80 轮 会，且未了结不得宣称完成 手动停止 / 轮数用完 / 清单全部了结 / 模型收口 目标模式在输入框左侧的 ＋ 菜单里开关，打开后再发消息即生效。 循环里会发生什么 每一轮循环大致按下面的节奏推进： 1. 观察 ：读取相关目录、文件、配置或 Git 状态，不凭记忆猜。 2. 执行 ：调用工具推进，能合并成一批的读取与扫描会在同一轮里做完。 3. 验证 ：改完必须重新读取、运行命令或跑测试，失败就继续修。 4. 汇报 ：逐条核对结果、验证方式与剩余风险。 工具结果会作为隐藏上下文回灌给模型，让它接着往下走，而不是把一堆原始输出摊在你的屏幕上。 如果某一轮发出的工具调用与上一轮完全相同，系统会拦截这次重复执行并提醒它换思路——同样的动作再跑一遍只会浪费轮数。 干完了就停 Autopilot 不接受\"说到一半就停\"： - 模型必须显式收口（你会看到一次收口调用）才算真正干完。 - 没走完成通道就退出（轮数用完、空转、你点了停止），状态会如实标成「已中断 · 任务未确认完成」，并在消息区给出「继续跑完」入口。 - 「继续跑完」不会重头再来，它从断点接着推进。 自动催办 模型话说到一半不动手时，系统会主动催它： - 只说\"我将检查 / 接下来修改 / 是否继续\"这类前瞻计划，且没有发起任何工具调用——催它直接调工具。 - 没有执行任何工具却口头说\"已完成\"——要求它先验证，或者真正动手。 - 连续多轮只描述计划不执行——给出更明确的警告，必要时提前止损，避免空耗额度。 这些催办只加进发给模型的上下文，不会占用你的消息区；哪怕中间发生过上下文压缩，它们也不会变成一条冒出来的气泡。 Continue Autopilot 轮数用完不等于干完。 Continue Autopilot 默认开启 ，在「设置 → 自动推进」里可以关掉。 - 最后一轮模型还在动手：把上限往后推 8 轮，让它把手上的活做完。最多追加 3 次，不会无限续跑。 - 最后一轮模型空手停笔：把任务清单里剩下的项目念回给它，催它继续推进，或明确回复【任务完成】。 推完 3 次还是到了上限、这一场并没有自己收口时，散场之后不必再手动点：只要这场不在手动审批档（自动审批 / 完全访问 / 夜间模式），SLATE 就替你按下「继续跑完」。它会等这一场真正空下来——压缩与记忆蒸馏这些收尾还要占着这场几秒——再发那句「继续」，一场最多自动续 2 次，并给一句「轮数用尽 · 已自动续跑（第 1/2 次）」的回声。手动审批档不自动按：那一档的语义就是每一步都要人点头，它只把「继续跑完」条挂在消息区等你。额度用尽或自动续没接上时，那颗手动按钮仍在原地，从断点接着推进，不会重头再来。 追加只放宽上限，不会取消止损线。若任务本身需要更长的闭环，直接开目标模式，而不是靠反复追加。 砚流：落笔即所见 模型逐个 token 写工具参数时，砚流会按工具的参数结构实时推断哪些字段已经写完、正在写哪个字段，把参数成形的过程演成一行可见的预览。 - 它 只做预览 ：半成品参数永远不会触发执行。 - 正文类字段（文件内容、Markdown、HTML 等）一律折叠成行数与字数，不会刷屏。 - 参数很短的工具不会起笔动画，避免无意义的闪烁。 停止是真取消 点「停止」不只是让界面安静下来： - 同时关闭模型流与工具调用的流式通道。 - 后端随之终止对应的子进程与在跑任务。 - 被取消的调用以独立状态记账，与「已完成」分开统计，事后可以回溯这一笔是停的还是做完的。 本轮改了哪些文件，说完就摆出来 这一轮真的往磁盘写过文件（编辑 / 新建 / 追加）时，模型说完最后一句话后，消息末尾会挂出一张卡片。它只报事实，不替模型表功。 - 抬头是这一轮改了几个文件、各自行数增减；点「审阅」逐文件展开 diff（默认列三条，其余点一下再显示）。 - 点「撤销」把这一轮写过的文件还原成本轮开始 之前 的样子，本轮新建的文件一并删掉；执行前先过一道红色确认。 - 你在本轮之后又自己改过的文件会被跳过并写明原因——撤回只还原它自己写过的那份，不吃掉你自己的修改。 - 本轮没往磁盘写任何东西时不留卡片；关掉了自动应用也一样：那次写入仍然记在它提出那一轮的清单里，但要等你点「接受」之后才真的落盘。 - 原文快照存在 data/round snapshots/ ，只保留 7 天；超过 2MB 的大文件不存原文，撤回时会跳过并说明。全部留在本机，不上传。 - 移动端只展示这份清单，撤销留在桌面端做。 目标模式 目标模式是显式的强闭环模式：你给目标，模型按六阶段自主推进。 六阶段闭环 目标 → 计划 → 执行 → 验证 → 汇报 → 追溯。 - 每轮工具结果都会标注当前轮次（第 x/N 轮），模型据此把握剩余节奏。 - 停止只中断当轮，目标模式开关保持开启待命，随时可以接着跑。 任务清单 - 多文件、多步骤、排查或实现类任务会自动建立任务清单；简单单步任务可跳过。 - 清单在消息区右侧栏实时展示，顶栏的「任务清单」按钮可以随时折叠它，把宽度还给消息区。 - 折叠后按钮上仍报 {done}/{total} 进度，这场没有清单时按钮置灰。 - 未全部了结，模型不得宣称完成；每完成一批立即回写状态。 四种退出方式 目标模式只有这四种退出路径： 1. 你手动点停止。 2. 80 轮用完。 3. 清单全部了结。 4. 模型显式收口结束循环。 模型侧失败、零输出、重复调用都会自动恢复推进，不会因为一次异常就中断整场。 当任务需要\"改完还要验证、还要回头补\"的完整闭环时才开：多文件重构、全项目排查、需要边跑边修的长任务。只改一个文件、问一个问题就别开，目标模式会为建立和核销清单多花掉几轮预算。 输出截断四层防线 写超长文件时模型可能被输出上限截断，SLATE 用四层防线保证文件最终是完整的： 1. 断点锚点续写 ：把模型自己最后输出的内容作为锚点，让它从断点精确接续（最多 6 轮）。 2. 截断守卫 ：工具调用没闭合就拒绝执行，要求模型拆分重试，而不是写进半份文件。 3. 分段补齐 ：先用创建写入前半部分，再用追加动作把剩余部分补齐，卡片上会标出这一段是截断的、等待后续补齐。 4. 提示词预防 ：在提示里事先要求它分批写入大文件。 一整轮只有思考、没有正文 还有一种截断跟文件无关：思考过程与正文共用同一个输出上限，思考吃满时这一轮就没地方写正文了。这类结局 SLATE 会如实报出来，不再让它悄悄过去： - 流收到内容后自然结束、却既没有结束标记也没有结束原因时，这一轮会被标记。 - 收尾时分开说明是「思考占满输出上限」还是「上游提前中断」，并给出可点的「继续」入口，从当前上下文接着跑。 - 正文有内容但流提前断掉的，标注为可能不完整。 - 这套提示与「继续」入口在任何模式下都给，不只是自动推进模式——否则关掉自动推进时，就只剩「它自己停了」这一种解释。 跨项目并行 切换项目不再打断正在生成的对话：你离开的那一场会在后台继续跑，进度与结局落在右栏的任务中心。 - 同一项目内默认串行（两场同时改同一批文件坏了难查是谁写的）。 - 跨项目最多两场同时运行，可在「设置 → 多任务与项目」里调到 1–4。 - 超出上限的发送不会被吞掉，而是进队列并标出「排队中」，前一场一收工自动接上。 - 每条会话的 Token 各记各的，用量条只反映屏幕上这一场。 项目与工作区的组织方式见 项目、工作区与分项目配置。"},{"t":"磨墨模式","u":"zh/07-grind.html","s":"对话与自动化","h":["磨墨是什么","怎么启动","三段式追问","墨迹面板","墨稿包含什么","墨稿之后：三个去处","会话持久化","常见问题","什么时候不适合用磨墨"],"x":"磨墨模式解决的是\"我知道要做点什么，但说不清\"的时刻：你扔进一句粗糙想法，AI 一轮问一个关键问题，最后交付一份结构化的任务书（墨稿），可以直接送去执行。 磨墨是什么 普通对话里，需求不清的代价由你自己承担——说不清楚，AI 就只能猜。磨墨把\"想清楚\"这件事交给追问来完成。 - 你不必一开始就写规格，说个大概就行。 - AI 一次只问一个问题，能出选项就给选项，不让你写小作文。 - 追问有轮数上限，不会无限聊下去；信息够了会提前收墨。 - 收墨得到的是任务书：目标、交付物、验收标准都写明白，可以直接开跑。 怎么启动 三种进入方式： 1. 在输入框输入 /grind 你的想法 ，例如 /grind 给团队做一个周报生成器 。 2. 点输入框左侧的 ＋ 按钮，在「模式与功能」里选「磨墨模式」。此时会开一场新的磨墨会话；如果还没想好内容，输入框会自动填好 /grind 等你补完。 3. 什么都没做，直接输入一个抽象任务——比如\"制作一个网站\"。在还没有对话内容的新会话里，SLATE 会弹出「磨墨建议」，你可以选「进入磨墨」先细化，或选「直接发送」照常发送。 不管用哪种方式进入磨墨，SLATE 都会清空当前对话视图、另起一场磨墨会话，屏幕上正在生成的那一场会被中断。它只停当前这一场，别的项目里跑着的任务不受影响。 三段式追问 磨墨按固定的三段式推进，全程不超过 10 轮： 阶段 AI 会做什么 --- --- 接墨 用一两句话复述你的想法核心，列出 3–5 个需要厘清的缺口，然后只问第一个 磨墨 逐轮追问下一个最重要的缺口，一次一问，能出选项就给选项，不寒暄也不重复已确认的内容 收墨 停止追问，先用一两句话总结共识，再给出完整的结构化墨稿 进入收墨有三种情况： - 你直接回复「收墨」「够了」「就这样」「可以了」这类话。 - 信息已经足够，AI 认为不必再问，主动收墨。 - 追问轮数达到上限，本轮直接收墨。 磨墨的追问链是按你的上一句答案往下走的。回答里给具体信息（谁用、什么时候要、什么样的结果算通过），后续问题就会越来越准；只回\"都行\"\"你看着办\"，AI 只能停在原地打转。 墨迹面板 侧栏的墨迹面板把这场研磨的成果实时摊开： - 面板就在消息区右侧，与任务清单同一列。 - ✔ 标记已经定下来的项，✘ 标记还没着落的未知项。 - 顶部给出统计：已定 N 项 · 待定 M 项，一眼看出离成稿还有多远。 - 面板随对话推进自动更新，不需要你点刷新。 - 追问进行中会标出当前轮次（第几轮 / 共 10 轮）；进入收墨阶段则提示可以回复「收墨」直接出稿。 - 拿到墨稿后，面板保留成稿的汇总视图，事后回看不会丢。 - 输入框左侧 ＋ 菜单里的「磨墨模式」一行也会跟着变，切回来扫一眼就知道这场研磨到哪一步了。 墨稿包含什么 收墨后给出的墨稿由七个要素组成，每个要素回答一个具体问题： 要素 回答什么问题 --- --- 目标 这件事做成之后，世界有什么不同 受众 谁会用、谁在看这份成果 交付物 最后要交出哪些东西，几个、什么形态 验收标准 满足哪些条件才算合格 边界与限制 哪些不做、有哪些硬约束（时间、技术、合规） 建议路径 建议按什么顺序推进 遗留问题 还没定下来的事，留给执行阶段合理假设并注明 没聊清楚的项会如实列进「遗留问题」，不会凭空替你填。 墨稿之后：三个去处 墨稿下方有三个按钮，按你接下来想干什么选： 按钮 做什么 什么时候用 --- --- --- 送入目标模式 把墨稿整理成任务描述，直接开一场目标模式自主执行 想清楚要动手做了 投到白板 把墨稿放成一张白板卡片 还在设计阶段，要画依赖、拆模块或跟人讨论 存为模板 存进知识库，作为可复用的任务书模板 同类需求还会再来一次，下次取出来改改就能用 三个按钮点下去分别会发生什么： - 送入目标模式 ：目标模式开关会自动打开，墨稿被整理成任务描述发出去，模型随即开始执行。墨稿里没定的项会交给模型自行合理假设，并要求它注明假设。 - 投到白板 ：生成一张标题为「墨稿 · 你的标题」的卡片，正文是墨稿目标，可以直接接着画连线。 - 存为模板 ：存成一份知识文档，在知识中心可见，随时能翻出来复用。 送到目标模式之后怎么跑，见 Agent Autopilot 与目标模式；白板上的卡片怎么连，见 白板式逻辑链；模板存放的位置见 记忆、画像与知识库。 会话持久化 磨墨状态跟着会话走，不跟着屏幕走： - 刷新页面、切换会话再切回来，墨迹面板与追问进度自动恢复。 - 同一场会话不会因为重开而从头再问一遍。 - 收墨成稿后重新打开这一场，三个按钮仍在墨稿下方。 常见问题 追问到一半就想直接开跑，可以吗？ 可以。回复「收墨」立刻出稿，墨稿下方的按钮随即出现，点「送入目标模式」就开始执行。 磨墨会动我的文件吗？ 不会。磨墨只产出一份文字任务书，不碰文件、不跑命令；真正动手发生在你点了「送入目标模式」之后。 能同时进行多场磨墨吗？ 每场会话各自保存自己的磨墨状态，互不干扰；在另一场会话里开磨墨不会影响前一场的进度。 什么时候不适合用磨墨 磨墨是给\"还没想清楚\"的想法准备的。下面这些情况直接跳过它： - 需求已经很明确，甚至能自己写出验收标准——直接开目标模式执行。 - 只是一次性的问答或小改动——当作普通对话说清楚就行。 - 你手上已有一份现成规格文档——把文档作为附件发进来更省事。 磨墨本身不动你的文件、不跑命令，它只把想法研磨成文字。要真正动手，收墨后点「送入目标模式」。"},{"t":"内置工具与技能","u":"zh/08-tools-skills.html","s":"对话与自动化","h":["两类能力先分清","在哪管理：「扩展」页","内置工具总览","重点工具","file_edit：改动先给你看","terminal：受限沙箱里跑命令","bg_task：后台终端任务的三层唤醒","搜索、Git 与联网","工具怎么被调用","工具的开关","自定义 Skill","接入外部 MCP 服务器","自进化工具（工具工厂）"],"x":"这一页是能力清单：SLATE 自带哪些工具、模型怎么用它们、哪些能关掉，以及怎么用 SKILL.md 和外部 MCP Server 把能力接得更多。 两类能力先分清 - 内置工具 ：SLATE 自带的 Python 能力，开箱即用，不需要配置也不用安装。文件读写、跑命令、生成文档、联网搜索都在这一类。 - Skill ：由 SKILL.md 定义的外部知识与流程。你自己写、上传或从本地路径导入，用来告诉模型\"这件事该按什么步骤做\"。 内置工具是\"手\"，Skill 是\"做法\"。两者都能被模型按需使用，也都能用 @ 显式点名。 在哪管理：「扩展」页 顶栏「扩展」这一页是全部能力管理的入口，左栏七栏各管一类，数字与列表同源： 分栏 管什么 --- --- 技能 SKILL.md 技能的导入、新建、删除，含「GitHub」与「发现插件」两个来源 工具 自带工具清单（可查看，不可删除），关掉的功能连目录都不出现 模式 回复模式的四档内置（只读）与自定义模式的建 / 改 / 删：追加提示词、工具白名单、推理强度与轮数上限 新功能 工具工厂自己生产出来的工具：停用、看代码、查历史版本、回滚、撤销 MCP 外部 MCP Server 的连接与它带来的远程工具 专家包 角色设定 + 知识库 + 技能的组合，可新建与导入 zip Actions data/actions/ .yml 流程说明书的编辑、校验、回滚 原先这些散在设置页的「工具 / 技能」与「MCP Server」两块，现已整块搬进这一页；设置页只留配置，不再兼做能力管理。 内置工具总览 类别 工具 用途 --- --- --- 文件与代码 file tree 浏览目录：可递归、按通配符过滤、快速扫描 文件与代码 file peek 读取文件内容：自动识别多种编码，可按行范围或读取末尾 文件与代码 file create 创建新文件 文件与代码 file edit 差异预览式编辑：精确替换、按行号替换、插入、删除、复制与粘贴 文件与代码 file append 向已有文件末尾追加，用于超长文件分段续写 文件与代码 code search 项目内全局代码搜索：文本或正则，可缩小到子目录 文件与代码 repo stats 项目文件类型、体积与数量统计 文件与代码 todo scan 扫描项目里的 TODO / FIXME 待办标记 文件与代码 python api extract 提取 Python 库的公共 API 文档（签名、类方法、属性） 终端与后台任务 terminal 受限沙箱命令执行，支持多会话与跨命令状态保持 终端与后台任务 bg task 后台终端任务：起了就走，结束时叫醒模型 办公文档 doc write 生成 Markdown 技术文档或需求说明 办公文档 ppt create 生成 .pptx 演示文稿 办公文档 word create 生成 .docx Word 文档 办公文档 excel tool 生成 .xlsx 、读取表格、csv 与 xlsx 互转 办公文档 pdf tool PDF 元信息、文本与表格提取 办公文档 html render 生成 HTML 骨架 办公文档 html bundle 把网页的 css / js 内联合并成单个 html，便于分发 数据与图表 json tool JSON 校验、格式化、压缩与按路径读取 数据与图表 regex test 正则表达式测试与匹配结果预览 数据与图表 text summarize 文本摘要与关键词提取 数据与图表 chart create 生成 SVG 图表：柱状 / 条形 / 折线 / 饼图 数据与图表 qrcode create 生成 SVG 二维码 搜索与网页 web search 联网搜索，免 Key 搜索与网页 web fetch 获取网页正文，支持 JS 渲染页面与 PDF 代码与文档安全 code scan 代码安全扫描：硬编码密钥、SQL 注入、XSS、弱加密、调试残留 代码与文档安全 doc scan 文档安全扫描：PII、凭证、财务数据、机密标记、内网信息 Git git tool Git 仓库只读信息：分支状态、提交日志、变更统计、分支与远程列表 系统与环境 system info 系统元认知：日期时间、硬件配置、电量、网络状态 系统与环境 css color 生成 CSS 配色方案 自动化 browser automation 浏览器自动化：导航、截图、点击、输入、执行 JS 自动化 computer use 桌面自动化：鼠标键盘控制、窗口管理、图像定位 自动化 screenshot to code 截图转代码：分析截图并生成还原视觉的 HTML / CSS 生成类 image gen AI 图片生成，需在设置里配置模型与 API Key 生成类 video gen AI 视频生成，需在设置里配置模型与 API Key MCP 工厂 mcp factory 工具工厂：按描述自动生成新工具，产物落 data/evolved/ ，在「扩展 → 新功能」可停用 / 回滚 / 撤销 上表共 36 条，覆盖你在工具面板与 @ 提及候选里能看到的全部内置工具。其中 file append 与 file create 属于同一族：一个负责建、一个负责续，配合完成超长文件的分段写入。 重点工具 file edit：改动先给你看 - 改动以 diff 形式呈现：文件名、 +N -M 行数统计、红绿增删行，一屏看清动了什么。 - 卡片上有「接受」「拒绝」「复制 diff」三个按钮：接受才落盘，拒绝只是关掉这张卡（这一笔不写入，但同一轮里已经写进去的内容不由这张卡回滚）。 - 在「设置 → 上下文与项目」里勾选了「自动确认文件创建/修改」（默认勾选）时，预览没有报错的改动会直接写入，卡片显示「已自动应用」，不再给接受与拒绝。部分内容没命中时仍保留手动确认，避免只写进去半套。 - 这一轮真正落盘的文件，会在模型说完最后一句话后汇总成一张「本轮改了哪些文件」卡片，可以在那里逐文件看 diff、整轮撤销，见 Agent Autopilot 与目标模式。 - 编码安全：写入时保留原有的 UTF-8、BOM、GB18030、GBK、UTF-16 等编码，中文与 emoji 不会变成乱码。 terminal：受限沙箱里跑命令 - 单条命令超时 30 秒，输出超过 5 万字符会被截断，命令长度也有上限。 - 支持多个终端会话并存，可以创建、列出、关闭，也可以终止会话里跑着的进程。 - Windows 下每条命令一个独立 PowerShell 进程：多行块（ foreach / if ）与 && / 都可用； cd 与 $env: 这类会话状态跨命令保持。 - 交互式命令行（裸跑 python / node 这类等待输入的 REPL）拿不到输入就会退出或拖到超时，不适合在这里用。 - 命令窗口不会弹到屏幕上，原生命令的输出捕获支持中文与 emoji。 bg task：后台终端任务的三层唤醒 编译、训练、起一个实验服务器这类以分钟计的活，交给它，不占住对话： 1. 主动问 ：起完立刻返回任务标识、开头一小段输出与日志路径；之后用状态与日志查询去取新增输出。 2. 结束时叫醒 ：起任务时带 notify=true ，任务结束会把结果带着输出尾巴送回对话，模型被叫醒接着干。 3. 提前叫醒 ：再挂一个正则，输出命中就提前唤醒，不必等命令跑完。 它是\"起了就走\"的一次性任务，不接受交互式输入；可以给超时让它到点自动终止，也可以随时停掉整棵进程树。任务寿命等于 SLATE 进程寿命——关掉 SLATE 任务就没了，日志留在本机可事后查。 后台任务走的审批档位与终端完全一致，高危命令照样会弹窗问你，灾难级命令直接拒绝。 搜索、Git 与联网 - code search ：在项目里做全局代码搜索，支持文本与正则，默认从项目根开始，可以缩小到某个子目录。 - git tool ：只读——看分支与工作区状态、最近提交、变更统计、分支与远程列表。它不会替你做提交、推送这类写操作，那些命令由模型在终端里执行，同样受审批约束。 - web search ：免 Key 联网搜索，双引擎并发合并去重，取实时信息。 - web fetch ：抓取指定网页的标题、描述与正文，支持需要 JS 渲染的页面与 PDF。 工具怎么被调用 - 模型自主调用 ：你直接描述需求（\"把这份数据做成图表\"），模型自己决定用哪个工具，不需要点名。 - 对话里能看到 ：每笔调用都会变成一张工具卡，显示工具名、关键参数与执行状态，结果可展开查看。 - 高危命令要审批 ：命令与联网访问都过审批门。审批档位有手动审批 / 自动审批 / 完全访问 / 夜间模式四档，本场可随时切换；完全访问把命令与联网的门关掉，但 AI 缺条件时照旧弹选择题问你一句；夜间模式再往前一步，全托管——命令、联网与模型的反问都不弹窗口。灾难级命令在任何档位下都无条件拦截。规则见 设置与安全审批。 - 没做成会说实话 ：某项能力跑不动时（比如运行环境缺了依赖），工具卡显示的是失败，模型收到的也是一句失败说明——它会告诉你缺什么、需要你自己去装，不会把没生成的文件说成已经生成好了。安装依赖这件事由你在终端里做，模型不会代你去装东西。 - 后台任务有独立面板 ：正在跑的后台终端任务列在右栏任务中心，可以直接查看与停止。 工具的开关 「设置 → AI 辅助功能」是对话以外所有耗 Token 功能的开关。其中三项属于工具类： - 子代理并行（ subagent run ） - AI 图片生成 - AI 视频生成 关掉这三项中的任何一项，对应工具会 从工具目录里消失 ——模型看不到它，也就不会调用它。图片与视频生成还需要在「图片生成」「视频生成」区块单独配置模型与 API Key。 自定义 Skill SKILL.md 即插即用。入口是顶栏「扩展」→「技能」栏，这一栏只放技能；内置工具在旁边的「工具」栏，只读不可删除。 一份 Skill 的写法： - 一个目录里放一个 SKILL.md ，目录名就是技能名。 - 文件里写清这份技能的用途（ description: 一行）与要遵循的步骤、约定、注意事项。 - 除了 SKILL.md ，同一个目录里可以再放它需要的参考文档。 添加与删除： - 「新建技能」上传文件创建，「导入技能」从本地路径导入（给包含 SKILL.md 的目录，或单个 .md 文件）。 - 「GitHub」从仓库导入，「发现插件」扫描本机已安装的 Codex / Claude 标准插件目录。 - 技能可以删除，删除后模型与 @ 提及都不再看到它；内置工具不受影响。 接入外部 MCP 服务器 在顶栏「扩展」→「MCP」栏里点「＋ 添加 MCP Server」，填名称与地址即可接入外部 MCP 工具。列表会显示连接状态、它提供的工具数量与工具名，并提供连接、断开、删除。 接入后，它的工具会出现在工具目录与 @ 提及候选里，模型可以像用内置工具一样调用。 项目级覆盖 ：打开一个项目后，列表里每台服务器会多出一个项目按钮，可以「在本项目停用」「在本项目单独启用」「摘掉本项目掩码」，也可以只放行其中几个工具。这类覆盖只影响当前项目，别的项目照旧；服务器的地址与密钥始终留在全局配置里，不会写进你的项目目录。详见 项目、工作区与分项目配置。 想明确指定用哪个工具或技能，直接在输入框输入 @ 从候选里挑，发送时会把它的定义注入这一条消息。 @ 的候选范围与用法见 对话与消息。 自进化工具（工具工厂） mcp factory 让 SLATE 把自己缺的工具写出来给自己用。产物一律落在用户数据区 data/evolved/ ，一个工具两份文件： <name .py 是生成的代码， <name .json 是说明与参数清单。 - 为什么不写进 backend/skills/ ：那是程序自己的源码树——一次升级就把攒下的自产工具整个冲掉，而且程序不该在运行时改自己。 data/ 升级覆盖不到，重启后清单照读。 - 旧产物会自动搬过来 ：历史版本写进源码目录的生成物，启动时复制进 data/evolved/ （只复制，不删程序自己目录里的文件）。 - 管理入口 ：顶栏「扩展」→「新功能」栏。每条都写明当前状态：正常 / 已停用 / 代码有问题（带语法错误行号）/ 与内置工具同名（已由内置接管）。 - 停用是整条摘掉 ，从模型的工具体系里消失，不是「调用时再报错」；随时可以再启用。 - 覆盖、撤销、回滚都先留底 到 data/evolved/.history/ （每个工具最多 5 版）。条目弹窗里的「历史版本」可查看任一版原文并回滚；回滚本身也是写，编译不过的旧版本不许盖回去。 - 语法不过的生成代码根本不落盘 ：工厂只做编译自检，绝不替你执行一次；与内置工具同名的创建请求当场拒绝。 - 同名冲突时内置优先 ：升级若带来了同名内置工具，那份自产代码会被顶掉并在栏里标出来——看得见、随时撤得掉，不必跟新版较劲。 自产工具由模型编写，能力与副作用不随内置工具的审计走。用之前可以在「新功能」栏里点开看代码；不放心就停用，或者撤销——留底还在，随时回滚。"},{"t":"Actions 流程说明书","u":"zh/09-actions.html","s":"对话与自动化","h":["Actions 是什么","跟 Skill 的区别","文件放在哪","字段怎么写","一份最小的可抄示例","书写格式的硬约束","模型怎么用它","手动把流程发进对话","在白板上跑这条","在设置页编辑","让模型代写","留底与回滚","分项目覆盖","写坏的文件不会凭空消失"],"x":"同一件事干到第二遍，你又要重申顺序、口径和踩过的坑。Actions 就是治这个的：把「干某件事的必要流程」提前写成一份说明书，模型在需要时自己去读、照着推进，你不必每次都交代一遍。 Actions 是什么 一份 Action 就是一份流程说明书。它写清三件事：这件事什么时候该做、要按什么顺序做、做成什么样算过关。 写好之后，模型在智能体态下会先看到一份 Action 目录（每条只有 id、名称、一句话描述与适用时机），判断这次任务跟某份说明书吻合时，再读全文、按步骤实际执行。流程由你定义，模型只负责照做。 跟 Skill 的区别 两者都放在本机数据目录里、都在同一处管理，但回答的是不同的问题。 对比项 Action 流程说明书 Skill 技能 --- --- --- 本质 可执行的步骤约定 知识与角色 回答的问题 这件事该按什么顺序做 这类事该怎么做、以什么身份做 内容形态 有序步骤，每步可注明建议工具与验收点 一篇说明文档 SKILL.md 怎么用 模型检索目录 → 读全文 → 按步骤实际执行 写进 Skill 后，聊天中 @ 提及即注入上下文 上限 遵循严格的书写格式，写坏会报出行号 文档形式，写法自由 一句话：Skill 让模型「懂」，Action 让模型「按你的顺序动手」。两者可以同时用。内置工具与技能的完整清单见 内置工具与技能。 文件放在哪 一份流程一个文件，文件名去掉 .yml 就是它的 id（例如 weekly report.yml 的 id 是 weekly report ）。id 只允许小写字母开头的 a-z0-9 - ，最长 48 个字符，不必在文件里再写一遍。 - 全局那份：本机所有项目共用。 - 项目那份：放进当前项目的 .slate/actions/ 里，同名时顶掉全局那份。 不确定放哪就先放全局，需要时再在项目里覆盖一份。 字段怎么写 一份说明书由下面几个字段组成，都是给人写、给模型读的： 字段 写什么 模型靠它判断什么 --- --- --- name 流程名，不超过 40 字 目录里认人 description 一句话说明，不超过 120 字 这份说明书值不值得点开读全文 when 什么时候该套用，不超过 200 字 这次请求要不要按这份流程走 inputs 开工前要向你拿的输入项，最多 8 项 先问你什么，再往下做 steps 步骤清单，1 到 24 步 按什么顺序做、该调哪个工具、怎么算过关 output 产出要求：格式与落点 最终交付成什么、放到哪 tags 标签，最多 8 个，单个不超过 24 字 检索时匹配关键词 inputs 每一项可以写： 键 说明 --- --- key 输入项的名字，小写字母开头，只能用 a-z0-9 label 展示给人看的名称，不写就用 key type text / number / textarea / select 之一，不写按 text required 只能写 true 或 false options 仅 select 需要，最多 20 项 steps 每一步可以写： 键 说明 --- --- title 这一步做什么，必填，不超过 60 字 tool 建议使用的工具名，例如 git tool 、 doc write detail 这一步的具体要求，多行正文用 字面量块 check 这一步的验收点，不超过 200 字 output 里， format 写产出的文件或正文格式； destination 只能写 message （留在对话里）、 file （写文件）或 board （回白板）；写 file 时必须同时给 path 。 一份最小的可抄示例 把这份文件存成 weekly report.yml ，它就会出现在 Action 目录里。 书写格式的硬约束 Action 用的是一套精简的 YAML 子集，只支持你真正需要的写法。超出范围的写法不会被「猜着解释」，而是直接拒收并告诉你出在第几行。 - 缩进固定 2 个空格，最多嵌套两层。禁止 Tab 缩进。 - 数组用块式写法：每行一个 - ，同一层的 - 必须对齐。 - 多行正文用 或 - 字面量块，块内缩进要一致。 - 布尔值只认 true / false 。 - 不接受锚点与别名（ & 、 ）、多文档分隔符（ --- ）、折叠标量（ ）、流式写法（ [] 、 {} ）与未闭合的引号。 - 单份文件上限 64 KB；所有 detail 正文合计不超过 20000 字。 校验报错的形式是「第 N 行：原因」，改了再存即可。 Action 的内容会长期进入模型的系统提示，等于把 Key 直接摆给模型看。需要凭据时，写「引用某个环境变量或文件位置」，不要写明文。面板会对疑似凭证给出提醒，但不会替你拦下。 模型怎么用它 在智能体态下，模型会先拿到一份 Action 目录，最多列 20 条；超出的部分只报数量，并告诉它用 Action 检索去看全部。它的典型动作是三步：检索 → 读全文 → 按步骤执行。在对话态下没有工具可用，整份目录都不会注入。 需要强调的一点： 读到流程不等于做过流程 。步骤里标了建议工具的，模型仍要真的调用工具完成；它不会因为「读过这份文件」就宣称已经做完了。 手动把流程发进对话 不想等模型自己检索，你也可以直接点名： - 在输入框输入 @ ，从候选里选一份 Action，整份流程会注入这条消息。 - 注入有 6000 字上限，超出的部分会被截断，并提示模型用读取工具取全。 - 提及用的是 id（文件名），中文名与标签只作为搜索别名。 适合的场景是：你已经知道该用哪份流程，只想让它这一次严格照做。 在白板上跑这条 打开黑板 →「工作流」显示模式，每一份 Action 都会成为卡片墙上的一张卡，卡面写着共几步、几项输入、谁写的、产出落到哪。卡上的「跑这条」会以 @提及 的方式把它发进当前对话，「看步骤」可就地展开步骤清单，「置顶」把常用的排在前面。 工作流视图同时还能看到这次运行走到了哪一步，细节见 白板式逻辑链。 在设置页编辑 顶栏「扩展」→「Actions」栏，点「＋ 新建 Action」，或点已有条目进编辑器。 编辑器边写边校验，报错会指出第几行、什么原因；校验没过就保存不了。编辑器顶部会写明你正在改的是哪一份：全局那份，还是某个项目的覆盖版。 让模型代写 你也可以直接让模型把流程写下来——前提是它必须按要求声明 author: model ，面板据此给这份文件打上「模型代写」的标记。 在「询问」权限模式下，写入前会弹窗把原文给你看，你点允许才落盘；切到「自动」或「越权」则不再弹窗。无论哪一档，格式校验都在写入前执行，不合格的内容进不来。 挑「你已经跟模型重复交代过两遍以上」的流程写。写一次，之后每次它自己读、自己照做，省下的是往后每一次的复述。 留底与回滚 每次覆盖或删除之前，SLATE 会先把原文留底，每份最多保留 5 个版本。编辑器里的「历史版本」可以查看任意一版并一键回滚；回滚本身也是一次写入，会先校验再落盘。 留底跟着落点走：项目那份的历史留在项目自己的目录里，不会与全局那份混在一起。 分项目覆盖 在打开的某个项目里放一份同 id 的说明书（ 项目目录/.slate/actions/<id .yml ），它会顶掉全局那份，其他流程照旧可见，不会「一进项目流程就消失」。 列表里会标出哪一条是「本项目」那份，编辑器也会写明现在改的是哪一份。删掉项目那份，同名流程自动回落到全局那份——这不是把流程整个删了。项目与全局的关系详见 项目、工作区与分项目配置。 写坏的文件不会凭空消失 改坏一份文件，它不会被静默忽略：设置页的 Actions 列表里会连它一起列出，并附上「第 N 行：原因」；模型那边拿到的工具返回也会告诉它哪几份暂时不可用；工作流视图的卡片墙上则会提示有几份说明书解析失败。看到这类提示，照着行号改回去即可。"},{"t":"专家包","u":"zh/10-experts.html","s":"对话与自动化","h":["专家包是什么","五件套结构","从零做一个专家包","规则怎么写才有效","导入与导出","内置样例：创意写作导师","三路注入的区别","什么时候该用专家包","维护与更新","注意事项"],"x":"每换一个场景，你都要重新交代「你是谁、按什么规矩来、参考哪些资料」。专家包把这些收进一个文件夹：装一次，之后按需挂上，模型就以这位专家的身份和口气干活。 专家包是什么 专家包是一份 可分发 的角色配置：一个人怎么想、守什么规矩、知道什么、会哪些方法，全部打包在一个文件夹里。它可以只在你本机用，也可以压成 zip 分享给同事。 装载之后不写进对话历史，而是作为系统提示的一部分注入——所以换会话、换模型都还在，直到你把它摘下来。 五件套结构 一个专家包由五个部分构成： 部分 作用 该写什么 --- --- --- persona.md 人格：这位专家是谁 身份与经验、擅长解决什么、说话与思考的风格 rules.md 规则：必须遵守什么 判断口径、输出格式要求、绝不允许的做法 knowledge/ 知识：他掌握的资料 真会用到的资料摘录，一个主题一个文件 skills/ 技能：他会哪些方法 步骤化的方法论、检查清单、模板 data.json 元信息 名称、一句话描述、版本、作者 knowledge/ 与 skills/ 都是文件夹，可以放多个文件，文件名就是它的标签。 persona.md 决定「像不像那个人」， rules.md 决定「稳不稳定」。如果只写一份，优先写 rules.md ——人格让表达有味道，规则才是让它每次都能交付的东西。 从零做一个专家包 在专家包管理面板里点「新建」，然后按顺序填： 1. 写名称与描述 ：名称是它在各个下拉框里显示的名字，描述是一句话说明，都会随包一起导出。 2. 写人格 （人格页签）：这位专家是谁、擅长什么、用什么口气说话。写短句，写具体。 3. 写规则 （规则页签）：它必须怎么做、绝不允许怎么做、输出成什么格式。 4. 放知识文件 （知识页签）：逐个上传，文件名就是它的标签，比如「判断口径.md」「常见坑.md」。 5. 放技能文档 （技能页签）：把你常用的方法论、清单、模板放进来。 6. 保存 ：保存后立刻生效；想给别人用，点「导出」存成 zip。 规则怎么写才有效 规则不是口号，是可执行的约束。与其写「请专业一点」，不如写清「必须做什么、不许做什么、输出成什么样」。一份能用的规则大概长这样： 把它作为参照：条目越具体、越像检查清单，模型的执行就越稳定。 导入与导出 导出为一个 zip，导入也是同一个格式，可以直接发给同事。 - 导入的包必须包含 persona.md 、 rules.md 与 data.json 三个文件；这三者允许放在 zip 内的单一根目录里。 - 一个包的上限是解压后 50 MB、最多 500 个文件。 - 导入进来的是本机的一份副本，改它不会影响别人手里的原件。 在专家包管理面板里点「导入」选 zip，点「导出」把当前这位存成 zip。 SLATE 自带一个样例包「创意写作导师」，装好就能用。先拿它试一次对话，看看人格与规则的注入效果长什么样，再照着它的写法拆出你自己的专家。 内置样例：创意写作导师 样例包的名字是「创意写作导师」，定位是「帮助你把灵感打磨成故事的叙事专家：提供结构诊断、文风建议与具体改写示范」。 它的五件套里带了两个知识文件与一份技能文档，可以直接拿来当模板：把 persona.md 换成你的角色，把 rules.md 换成你的口径，知识文件换成你手上的资料。 三路注入的区别 同一份专家包，可以从三个地方挂上去，生效范围完全不同： 注入方式 在哪选 生效范围 注入什么 --- --- --- --- 对话输入区的专家下拉 对话输入区左侧 这场对话全程生效 人格 + 规则 + 知识文件的名字清单 团队成员卡 团队模式里每位成员的「专家包」下拉 只对这个角色的发言生效 人格 + 规则 聊天 @ 提及 在输入框输入 @ 选专家包 只对这一条消息生效 人格 + 规则 + 前 6 个知识文件的正文（每个最多 4000 字符） 区别的关键在最后两列：前两路只给知识文件的 名字 ，模型知道「有这个文件」但不一定读得到内容；只有 @ 提及那一路会把知识正文塞进这条消息，代价是这条消息的 Token 明显变大。 所以用法可以这样分工：要一个稳定的专家身份，用下拉框挂着；要它这一次真的翻某份资料，用 @ 提及。 什么时候该用专家包 - 跨多次会话、反复做同一类事 ：例如长期做技术方案评审、长期写对外文案。 - 有明确口径与禁忌 ：这些写进 rules.md 比每次口头强调更可靠。 - 要分享给别人 ：一个 zip 就是全部，收的人导入即可复现。 反过来，如果只是这一次想让它的口气变一变，直接在消息里说清楚就够了，不必专门建一个包。 维护与更新 - 改完就生效 ：在面板里保存后，如果这个包正挂在这场对话上，注入的内容会同步更新，不必重新选一遍。 - 文件可增减 ：知识文件与技能文档都可以随时上传和删除，删掉的会从名单里消失。 - 删除不可恢复 ：删掉一个专家包时会有一次确认，确认后无法撤销；想留底就先导出一份 zip。 - 建议留一份备份 ：手写的规则与整理好的知识资料都是心血，导出的 zip 就是最简备份。 注意事项 - 知识文件越大越费 Token 。 @ 提及最多读 6 个文件、每个截到 4000 字符，装得越多，每条消息的固定开销越大。 - 建议只放 真的会用到 的内容：一份「常用判断口径」比一本「素材全集」有用。 - 规则与人格要短而具体。写成大段散文，模型反而抓不住重点；用短句与清单写得越明确，它执行得越稳定。 - 一个包尽量只覆盖一类角色。需要多个角色时，做多个包，用团队模式分别挂上去更清楚（见 AI 团队与工作流）。 专家包挂在对话上是全程生效的。发现它的口径不合现在的任务，直接在下拉里切回「无专家」，别在同一场对话里硬掰——两种口径混在一起，模型会左右为难。"},{"t":"AI 团队与工作流","u":"zh/11-team.html","s":"协作与推演","h":["AI 团队解决什么","辩论怎么进行","发言的六种动作","角色与模型分工","内置团队预设","自定义团队","决策等你拍板","讨论摘要与收尾","中途停止","失败与缺席不算发言","切走了也找得回来","讨论记录去哪了","团队工作流：把流水线交给 DAG","内置工作流模板","工作流的导入、导出与删除"],"x":"一个人（哪怕是一个模型）想不到的地方，往往要靠另一个立场的角色来挑。团队模式让多个角色围绕同一议题多轮辩论，最后交出一份带拍板结论的摘要；工作流模式则把长任务拆成有依赖关系的节点，能并行的并行跑完。 AI 团队解决什么 它的价值不在「多几个声音」，而在 对抗 ：让一个角色负责务实、一个负责创新、一个负责拍板，观点互相顶一下，你拿到的不再是第一反应的答案，而是被质疑过一轮的答案。 适合的议题：方案取舍、技术选型、风险排查、产品方向、需要正反两面都看到的决定。 辩论怎么进行 在「对话」工作区标题栏左侧把模式从「对话」切到「团队」，就进入团队讨论视图： 1. 选一套团队预设，或自己配成员。 2. 在议题输入框写下要讨论的问题。 3. 选辩论轮数（默认 5 轮，可切 3 / 5 / 8 / 10 轮）。 4. 点「开始讨论」，各成员按顺序发言。 第一轮每位成员各自提案。之后每一轮，成员可以看到全部已有发言，选择提案、支持、反对、反驳或补充，也可以点名回给某位成员。整个过程按轮次逐条铺开，你随时能看到谁说了什么。 发言的六种动作 每一句发言都以一个动作开头，系统据此分类，摘要里的发言统计也来自这里： 动作 含义 --- --- 【提案】 提出新想法或新方案 【支持】 认同并强化某个已有观点 【反对】 明确不认同某一观点 【反驳】 针对某个观点的论证过程给出反驳 【补充】 在同一方向上补上被漏掉的部分 【决策】 给出最终方案，讨论就此结束 要定向回应某个人，在动作后写 @成员名 ，例如 【反驳】@性能专家 这个结论忽略了冷启动成本。 系统会把它记成一条指向对方的回应边。 每次发言都被要求简短（一到三句），既是为了读起来是辩论而不是论文，也是为了省 Token。 角色与模型分工 默认三位成员对应三种立场： 角色 立场 职责 --- --- --- 分析师 务实 关注可行性与成本 创意者 创新 关注可能性与用户体验 决策者 综合 汇总各方观点，给出最终方案与理由 只有决策者能拍板：其他成员即使写了【决策】也会被当成普通提案。决策者可以在任意一轮直接给【决策】收口结束讨论；如果轮次用尽仍无共识，会由决策者再补一轮最终决策。 模型分工是刻意设计的： 讨论位用轻量模型，决策位用重型模型 。讨论阶段发言次数多但每句都短，用轻量模型足够；最终方案只出一次，值得用更强的模型。 每一轮里每位成员都要把已有的全部发言读一遍。轮数乘以人数，成本是相乘的。三到五轮通常就能看出分歧在哪，把角色数量控制在真的需要的范围。 内置团队预设 SLATE 自带 9 套预设，选中即替换当前成员名单（成员之后仍可单独调整）： 预设 成员构成 --- --- 经典三人组 分析师 + 创意者 + 决策者 代码审查组 安全专家 + 性能专家 + 架构师 产品头脑风暴 产品经理 + 设计师 + 用户代言 + 技术负责人 技术选型 前端专家 + 后端专家 + 基础设施专家 + 技术总监 红蓝对抗 正方辩手 + 反方辩手 + 裁判 全栈评审 前端评审 + 后端评审 + 数据库评审 + 运维评审 + 技术总监 写作工坊 内容策划 + 文案写手 + 编辑 创业顾问团 市场顾问 + 技术顾问 + 财务顾问 + 运营顾问 + CEO 学术研讨 方法论专家 + 实证研究者 + 批判者 + 主持人 自定义团队 把预设当起点，逐张成员卡改就行： - 名称 ：直接改卡片上的名字，辩论记录里就用这个名字（ @ 回应也认它）。 - 模型 ：每张卡上选模型，可按「国外 / 国内 / 本地 / 自定义」分组挑。没配 Key 的模型会标注提示。 - 人设 ：卡上的角色设定文本就是这位成员的立场与风格，改它等于重新定义这个角色。 - 专家包 ：每张卡可选一个专家包，把人格与规则绑到这个角色上，见 专家包。 - 增删 ：顶部「＋」添加成员，卡片右上角「×」移除。自己加进来的成员默认是普通成员，不参与拍板。 想让某位成员负责拍板，用预设里的决策者位，或把成员改成决策者位再调整人选。 决策等你拍板 「决策需我拍板」默认开着：决策者给出【决策】之后，这场讨论不自己收口，而是停下来等你签字。 - 采纳拍板 ：以那条决策结束，摘要照常生成。 - 继续讨论 ，或者不选直接关掉弹窗：算「没签」，讨论带着「未拍板」往下再辩一轮，下一位的提示词里会写明这条决策还没被签字。 - 重开有次数上限，用尽后讨论自行收口——每一轮重开是全员再发言一次，不给它无限烧下去的口子。 拍板那一行是讨论里的一笔独立记录：它进下一位的上下文、进后端记录，但不算任何一位成员的发言，星图也不会把它数成谁的观点。 夜间模式（全托管）下不弹窗打断，决策直接采纳，但那一行照样画出来并写明是自动采纳——不会读成你点过头。不想被拦就在团队面板取消那个勾选，回到「决策即结束」。 讨论摘要与收尾 讨论结束时会自动生成一份「辩论摘要」，包含： - 议题与参与成员名单。 - 发言统计：共几条，以及提案 / 支持 / 反对 / 反驳 / 补充 / 决策各多少条。 - 最终方案与提出者；如果始终没有形成决策，则明确写「未达成明确决策」。 摘要之外，整场辩论的逐条发言也留在讨论区里，可以往下翻看。 中途停止 点「停止讨论」即可中断。 已完成的发言原样保留 ，不会因为停止而丢掉已经辩出来的成果。 被手动停止的这一场不生成摘要——没有结论就不编一个结论；但它照旧进团队历史，那一行标着「已中断」，点开看得见已经辩完的那几段。 失败与缺席不算发言 某位成员这一轮请求失败，或他绑的模型没配 API Key，那一格会明确标「失败」/「未参与」： - 这句报错不会进下一位的上下文——否则模型会把一条报错当成某人的主张接着反驳。 - 它不计入发言统计，星图里那位成员的发言数就是 0。 - 后端仍留一行 error / skipped，事后能追溯谁在哪一轮没说话；这两种状态也只能由系统标出，模型写【失败】是解析不出来的。 切走了也找得回来 辩论在你切到「对话」面板的这段时间继续跑。等你签字与这场收口，都会进右侧的任务中心，与后台终端任务、子代理批次排在同一张表里：那一行写着议题，等签字时标「等你拍板」，点行上的团队按钮跳回这一场重看，点停止中断的也正是这一场。 讨论记录去哪了 每次发言会同时落两个地方： - 本机历史 ：左侧「团队历史」列表，保留最近 50 场，列出时间、发言条数与大致 Token 用量，点一条即可回看。 - 后端团队会话 ：一场讨论对应一份会话记录、一份成员名册，以及每位成员各自一次事件账本记录。 后端那份是黑板 · 工作流视图里「团队星图」的数据源：按角色摆出最近一次讨论，谁回应了谁、谁动过哪些工具、谁派生了子代理，全部取自真实记录。如果写入后端失败，本机历史仍在，那条列表会标上「仅本机记录」；这场对话完全没有团队记录时，星图会直说，不会画一张装饰性的图。星图的读法见 白板式逻辑链。 团队工作流：把流水线交给 DAG 辩论是「一起想」，工作流是「分头干」。切到团队面板的「工作流」页签： 1. 选一套工作流模板。 2. 在下面填任务需求，它会作为 $input 传给各个节点。 3. 点「运行工作流」。 节点之间靠依赖边串起来：上游产出可以作为下游的输入（ $node id 形式引用）， 没有依赖关系的节点会同时执行 。每个节点跑起来后状态实时回显——等待、运行中、成功、失败、跳过；某一个节点失败或被跳过，依赖它的下游会自动跳过，而不是带着空输入硬跑。运行中可以随时点停止，未开始的节点会标为跳过。 节点的模型绑定按这个优先级取：绑定团队成员的角色（沿用它的模型与人设）→ 节点自己指定的模型 → 「设置 → AI 辅助功能」里「工作流」那一档指定的模型 → 当前模型。 整条流水线跑完，产物会自动归档进知识库，标题形如「工作流产物 · 模板名」，可在记忆面板里查看。 内置工作流模板 SLATE 自带 8 套模板： 模板 节点流水线 --- --- 标准开发流程 需求分析 → 任务拆解 → 编码实现 → 代码审查 → 总结交付 并行研究分析 需求分析 → 技术调研 / 风险评估 / 资源评估 → 汇总决策 代码审查流程 变更扫描 → 质量审查 / 安全审查 / 性能审查 → 汇总报告 文档生成流程 大纲规划 → 正文撰写 → 摘要提炼 → 交付整合 数据分析流程 数据理解 → 趋势分析 / 异常检测 → 统计摘要 → 决策建议 研究报告流程 课题定义 → 背景调研 → 方案对比 / 可行性分析 → 报告生成 产品需求流程 想法梳理 → 用户故事 → 功能拆解 → 优先级排序 → 需求文档 Bug 排查流程 Bug 描述 → 日志分析 / 代码分析 / 环境分析 → 根因定位 工作流的导入、导出与删除 工作流模板以 JSON 定义文件形式提供，含节点、依赖边与每个节点的提示词。其中「标准开发流程」与「并行研究分析」是内置模板，不可删除；其余自定义模板可以导入、导出与删除。删除只在模板层面生效，已经跑出来的产物仍留在知识库里。"},{"t":"白板式逻辑链","u":"zh/12-whiteboard.html","s":"协作与推演","h":["白板用来干什么","基本操作","卡片颜色就是它的分类","跟对话、团队、墨稿的联动","Mermaid 实时渲染","六种显示模式","Git 树视图","工具步骤卡自动记录","工作流视图：一屏四块","思考过程显示"],"x":"想不清楚的时候，把东西摆出来看。白板让你把灵感、功能与待办写成卡片，用连线标出依赖与数据流；它同时还是这场对话的仪表盘——工具跑到哪一步、这一场怎么控制，都能在同一屏看到。 白板用来干什么 三件事： - 把想法外化 ：想到什么先落成一张卡，不必先排序，之后再连线整理。 - 看清依赖 ：连线表示「谁依赖谁」「数据从哪流到哪」，箭头方向就是顺序。 - 当运行现场 ：工具执行会自动留下步骤卡，工作流视图还能就地控制这一场对话怎么跑。 白板与当前对话是联动的：团队辩论可以读到板上的卡片当背景，你也可以把墨稿投到白板上推演。 基本操作 顶栏「黑板」进入，默认就是自由画布。 - 新建卡片 ：顶栏「＋」按钮，或在流程视图里点「根卡」新建一个流程起点。 - 移动布局 ：直接拖动卡片。顶栏的「自动整理布局」会按连线关系分层排布（快捷键 Ctrl + L ）。 - 编辑卡片 ：双击卡片打开编辑弹窗，可改标题、正文、颜色与连线关系。 - 多选 ：按住 Shift （或 Ctrl / Cmd ）点击可加选与减选，多选后可用 Ctrl + D 复制、 Delete 删除。 - 连线 ：把工具切到「连线」，先点起点卡片再点终点卡片。连线就是依赖与数据流。 - 画笔与文字笔记 ：工具栏可切到画笔随手画，「文字」模式下在空白处点一下即可写一条笔记。 - 收起画笔痕迹 ：「撤销笔画」撤销最后一笔，「清除所有笔」一次清空。 - 导出与清空 ：顶栏可导出整块黑板（卡片、关联、颜色、Mermaid 源码与 JSON）；「清空黑板」会清掉所有卡片，按提示确认后再动手。 按 Esc 可随时取消选择并切回「选择 / 移动」工具。 卡片颜色就是它的分类 颜色不只影响观感，看板视图与 AI 整理都按它分类： 颜色 含义 --- --- 默认 未分类 红色 风险 橙色 待处理 黄色 想法 绿色 完成 蓝色 信息 紫色 创意 拿不准就先放默认，整理的时候再统一染一遍色——看板视图里换列就是换颜色。 跟对话、团队、墨稿的联动 白板不是一张孤立的图，它和几处能力是通的： - 模型可以直接读写白板 ：读取现有卡片、新增卡片、更新标题与正文、改依赖、改颜色，还有批量操作与清空。所以你可以直接说「把这几个想法整理成卡片并连上线」，它会动手摆。 - AI 整理成流程图 ：顶栏的整理按钮会把卡片标题、正文、依赖与颜色整体重构一遍，原有卡片 ID 保持不变。它属于「设置 → AI 辅助功能」里的「黑板 AI 整理」，可以单独关掉或指定模型。 - 团队辩论会读白板 ：发起团队讨论时，当前板上的卡片会作为背景资料交给各成员，让它们围绕你已经写下来的东西讨论，而不是重新猜。 - 墨稿可以投过来 ：磨墨模式收墨后，点「投到白板推演」就能把任务书铺成卡片继续推演，见 磨墨模式。 Mermaid 实时渲染 卡片与连线会自动生成一段 Mermaid 图（方向从左到右），并由 Mermaid.js 实时渲染成流程图。顶栏的图表按钮可以显示或隐藏这块渲染区；图随卡片改动即时重画。 如果渲染失败（例如卡片标题里有特殊字符），面板会就地提示失败原因，不会影响白板本身。 六种显示模式 顶栏有五个视图按钮，再加上默认的自由画布，一共六种看法。再点一次当前选中的按钮就回到自由画布。 模式 看什么 什么时候用 --- --- --- 主黑板（自由画布） 卡片、连线、画笔、文字笔记 发散整理，随手摆 流程 按依赖层级分阶段列出卡片，每张标出输入与输出条数 看清先后顺序与合流点 看板 按卡片颜色分列，拖进另一列即改分类 按状态推进一件事 纲要 以连线为层级展开的树，可逐层折叠 长清单、层级结构 Git 树 当前打开项目的仓库结构 摸清分支、提交与工作区状态 工作流 控制条 + 流程卡片墙 + 当前运行 + 团队星图 边跑边看 流程视图与看板视图里都带「排布」按钮，能把当前视图的层级或列顺序同步回画布。纲要视图会把没有连线的卡片单独列在「未连接卡片」下，提醒你还有孤立的想法。 Git 树视图 需要先打开一个项目，Git 树读的就是这个项目仓库的状态。它会识别并画出来： - 仓库本身、HEAD、当前分支、本地分支、远程分支、标签与 remote。 - worktree 列表，以及 stash。 - 工作区的三组计数：已暂存、已修改、未跟踪（各带文件数量，没有变更就写「无变更」）。 - 未推送的提交，以及最近的提交记录。 节点位置和整个视野都可以拖动，摆好的位置会记在本机，下次进来还是你摆的样子。没有打开项目时它会直说，而不是画一张空图。 工具步骤卡自动记录 每次工具执行都会自动在画布上生成一张步骤卡，你不必手动记。 - 卡片标题形如「步骤 N：<这一步在做什么 」，正文写这一步的参数摘要，跑完后换成结果摘录。 - 颜色标状态： 黄色 = 执行中，绿色 = 完成，红色 = 错误 ，已取消是默认灰色。 - 卡片之间会按调用顺序自动连成一条链。 - 状态变化只回写卡片正文与颜色，编号与位置不动，所以正在跑的时候整块板不会乱跳。 - 新一轮运行开始时，上一批步骤卡整批清掉，板上只留这一轮的。 在流程、看板、纲要这三种视图里，步骤卡默认是隐藏的，右上角的「显示工具步骤」可以把它们切出来看。 它记录的是「这一场实际调用了什么」，不吃 Token，也不占提示词预算。想看工具到底干了什么，看这里最可靠。 工作流视图：一屏四块 切到「工作流」模式，这一屏同时是控制台和现场。 控制条 ：停止、继续跑完、自动推进（目标模式开关）、回复模式（内置四档 + 自定义，可选任意一档）、思考强度（自动 / 关 / 低 / 中 / 高）。可选档位按当前模型的能力给出，上游不认的档位不会出现。这些控件不另存一份状态——它们写的就是聊天框顶部那套状态，两处永远不会不一致。旁边还有「去团队」按钮，直接跳到团队模式。 流程卡片墙 ：每一份 Action 流程说明书一张卡，卡面写着共几步、几项输入、谁写的（你写的 / 模型写的）、产出落到哪，以及标签。「跑这条」以 @提及 的方式把它发进当前对话，「看步骤」就地展开步骤清单，「置顶」把常用的排在前面（配合「只看置顶」把卡片墙收窄）。说明书解析失败时，这里会给出提示。写说明书的方法见 Actions 流程说明书。 当前运行 ：直接从运行记录投影出来——哪一步在跑、跑了多久、成没成。没有记录时它会说明白：本场还没有运行记录、或者本轮还在思考尚未调用工具。 团队星图 ：按角色摆出这场对话最近一次团队讨论，谁回应了谁、各自动过哪些工具、派生了哪些子代理，全部取自真实记录。点一位成员可以看它最近的发言序列。这场对话没有团队记录时，它会直说，不会画一张装饰性的图。 思考过程显示 模型在动手前的推理与思考过程会实时显示在一个可折叠面板里，思考完成后自动折叠，点标题栏可以再展开回看。它单独成一块，不会混进正式回答里。 卡片、文字笔记、画笔笔画、Git 节点位置以及工作流视图的置顶偏好，都保存在你这台机器上，刷新与重启之后还在。它们不随对话走，也不会被别的项目覆盖。"},{"t":"记忆、画像与知识库","u":"zh/13-memory-knowledge.html","s":"协作与推演","h":["为什么要有记忆","记忆面板在哪","长期记忆：攒下什么、怎么来","记忆的修正与清理","用户画像","记忆怎么进入对话","灵光：对话里值得留下的洞察","知识库","文库","三类信息的分工","Token 成本与开关"],"x":"为什么要有记忆 每开一条新对话就重新交代一遍\"我用 Python 和 TypeScript\"\"这个仓库不吃顺手重构\"\"回答别写长篇铺垫\"——这本身就是损耗。记忆与知识库把这类信息从\"每次重复说\"变成\"一次沉淀、长期复用\"：SLATE 在对话中自己攒下要点，下次开口之前就已经知道背景。 本页讲四件事：长期记忆怎么来怎么改、用户画像是什么、灵光捕获了什么、知识库怎么按归属分家。 记忆面板在哪 顶栏的「记忆与画像」按钮打开同一个面板，里面四个标签页： 标签页 管什么 --- --- 长期记忆 逐条沉淀下来的要点，可增删改 用户画像 角色、风格、技术栈等结构化信息 知识中心 知识文档的检索、添加与归属 文库 Markdown 笔记，支持 [[双链]] 、标签与反向链接 手机上在底部「记忆」Tab 里管理长期记忆，详见 移动端遥控与局域网访问。 长期记忆：攒下什么、怎么来 - 对话中自动沉淀 ：每轮回复结束后，SLATE 会静默读一遍最近的对话，把值得长期保留的信息提炼成记忆条目——你的偏好、做过的决策、项目背景、术语约定、明确约束。 - 手动添加 ：点「+ 添加记忆」写一条，并选一个分类。 - 从对话提取 ：点「从对话提取」，把当前这场对话整理成候选记忆。 - 自动去重 ：内容相同或高度相似的记忆不会被重复添加，提取结果会告诉你跳过了几条。 分类可选：偏好 / 决策 / 项目 / 术语 / 事实 / 通用 / 其他；另有架构 / 技术 / 规范 / 构建 / 日志 / 配置，供知识型条目使用。分类只影响整理与检索，不影响是否被用上。 记忆存在本机、跨会话持久化。每次对话都会把最近一批记忆和用户画像附加到上下文里，所以换一条新对话不必重新交代背景。 - 长期成立的偏好与习惯：「回答先给结论」「别自动提交」 - 已经拍板的决策，以及当时的理由 - 项目独有的背景、术语、目录约定 - 明确划出的边界：「这个仓库不引入新依赖」 一次性的任务、临时状态、寒暄、工具原始输出、纯代码片段都不值得记——自动提炼时也会把它们过滤掉。 记忆的修正与清理 信息会过时，记忆也得能改。三条路径： 1. 自动覆盖与删除 ：如果对话里你明确修正、推翻或废弃了某条已有记忆，提炼时会把那条改写成新内容（覆盖，条目本身留着），或直接清掉（删除）。每次提炼的结果会如实报出\"新增 / 覆盖 / 删除 / 跳过重复\"各几条。 2. 手动提炼一次 ：点「自动提炼画像」立即跑一次，不必等下一轮对话。 3. 逐条编辑 ：双击记忆内容改文字，点笔形按钮改分类，点 × 删除。删除会同时清掉它在知识库里的那份镜像文档。 修正过的记忆立刻参与后面的对话，不需要新开一条会话。 用户画像 画像是记忆里更结构化的一份：它不记具体某句话，而是描述\"你是谁、你习惯怎么协作\"。 字段 填什么 --- --- 角色 / 职业 全栈开发者、产品经理…… 工作风格 注重细节、快速原型、迭代式推进…… 技术栈偏好 Python、React、TypeScript…… 沟通习惯 简洁直接、多用示例…… 其他 任何你希望 SLATE 提前知道的信息 画像由对话与记忆自动提炼，通常只需新增或修正个别字段；也可以手填后点「保存画像」，「重置画像」会清空全部字段。它和长期记忆一起自动附加到每次对话里，并明确要求模型不要向你复述这些内容。 记忆怎么进入对话 记忆与画像不需要你手动\"挂载\"：每次发送消息时，SLATE 会挑出最近的一批长期记忆与用户画像，附加到这次对话的上下文里，并要求模型拿它们调整风格与内容，而不是当成话题复述。知识库走的是另一条路——只有开启自动检索、并且确实与当前问题相关时，命中的片段才会被注入，见下节。 三个实际含义： - 记忆不是越多越好 ：附加时会做长度截断，啰嗦的条目会挤占上下文预算。值得的留下，过期的清掉。 - 记忆是全局的 ：它不随项目切换而变，进到哪个项目里都还在（知识库则按归属分家）。 - 画像优先于纠正 ：填一次\"技术栈偏好是 TypeScript\"，比在每条对话里纠正模型省事。 灵光：对话里值得留下的洞察 「灵光」是对话结束时的一次自动收割。切换会话时，SLATE 会判断上一场里有没有真正值得跨对话复用的技术洞察—— - 重要的技术决策：选了什么方案、为什么、权衡了什么 - 非显而易见的问题解法 - 可复用的代码或架构模式 - 关键配置与环境设置的发现 - 值得记下来的工程经验教训 命中的内容会归档成一条标题形如「灵光 · 某某」的知识文档，参与后续的检索注入；普通问答、闲聊、工具输出、临时调试不会被收走。捕获到内容时会提示收了几条。这条链路可以在 设置 → AI 辅助功能 → 对话洞察捕获 里关掉。 知识库 知识库是\"按需检索\"的那一层：不常驻上下文，只在相关时把片段注入。 - 添加 ：点「+ 添加知识」填标题与内容，支持 Markdown。 - 检索 ：勾选「聊天时自动检索知识库」，旁边的「片段」决定每次最多注入几条（1–12，默认 5）；也可以在搜索框里按关键词当场搜。 - 来源 ：手动添加的笔记、长期记忆的镜像、灵光捕获的洞察、团队工作流产出的成果会陆续汇进来。 - 按归属分家 ：开着项目时添加知识，会先问你「存进本项目」还是「存为全局」。同名（标题忽略大小写与首尾空白）时项目那条顶掉全局那条，全局其余照旧可见；别的项目的文档不会进入当前视野。完整规则见 项目、工作区与分项目配置。 - 删除 ：在项目视野里删不掉全局那份（那会带走别的项目共用的文档）。先收起项目，回到只有全局的视野里删。 文库 面板的第四个标签页是文库：一个 Markdown 笔记库。文件夹与笔记树形排列，左侧写源码、右侧实时预览。笔记可以打标签（逗号分隔），也可以用 [[笔记名]] 建立双链；选中一篇时，底部会列出 反向链接 ——哪些笔记提到了它。搜索框按标题找，标签栏按标签过滤。适合放那些\"想留着，但不适合塞进知识片段\"的长资料。 三类信息的分工 长期记忆 用户画像 知识文档 --- --- --- --- 来源 对话中自动提炼，或手动添加 由记忆与交互历史提炼，也可手填 手动添加、记忆镜像、灵光、工作流产物 存哪 SLATE 本机，跨会话持久化 SLATE 本机设置 SLATE 本机知识库，按全局 / 项目分归属 什么时候用上 每次对话附加最近一批 每次对话附加 开启自动检索后按相关度注入片段 怎么改 面板内增删改 面板内五栏编辑或重置 搜索、添加、删除 Token 成本与开关 记忆蒸馏、对话洞察、上下文压缩这些动作本身也要调用模型，所以它们都在「设置 → AI 辅助功能」里挂号：逐项可关，也可以单独指定一个更便宜的模型。与记忆相关的三档是记忆自动蒸馏、记忆手动提取、对话洞察捕获。关掉即一次模型都不发。详见 设置与安全审批。 关掉自动蒸馏不影响已经攒下的记忆，也不影响手动添加与编辑，只是不再自动新增、覆盖或删除条目。已经入库的知识与灵光照常参与检索。"},{"t":"项目、工作区与分项目配置","u":"zh/14-projects.html","s":"项目与数据","h":["项目是什么","打开、在册与切换","项目栏与文件树","工作区：一个项目收进多个文件夹","分项目宪法","分项目覆盖：Actions、知识库与 MCP","Better Project Understanding","Code Review","跨项目并行运行"],"x":"项目是什么 SLATE 把\"一个文件夹\"当作一个项目来看待与操作。打开一个目录之后，文件树、Git 信息、终端的工作目录、代码搜索的范围都跟着它走。 项目的身份跟着文件夹的绝对路径走：同一个文件夹重新打开会回到同一个项目，会话、宪法、现场（上次停在哪条会话、草稿、看板、默认模型）都还在，历史不会散成两堆。 打开、在册与切换 - 顶栏项目栏的「打开项目…」按钮打开弹窗：上面是「在册项目」清单，开过的项目都留在这儿，点一下就回去，接着上次的会话和草稿继续；下面是目录路径输入框与磁盘快捷选择。 - 打开后，项目栏显示的 项目名本身就是切换器 ：点一下弹出「在册项目」，可以切到别的项目、把常用的固定、或从最近移除（移除只摘索引，不删除任何文件与会话）。 - 项目栏的 × 是 收起当前项目 ，不是删除：它仍留在在册清单里，会话、宪法、磁盘上的文件都不动，从项目名处随时切回。 - ↻ 重新打开并刷新当前项目；工作区会重开它的宿主目录。 - 在册项目数量有上限，到顶时先淘汰最久没打开过的归档项，固定项永不淘汰。 项目栏与文件树 项目栏里另有三颗按钮：Better Project Understanding、Code Review、项目设置（直接跳到设置页的宪法区块）。 左侧文件树支持逐层进入、面包屑回跳与「..」返回上级；点文件就地预览，预览头上的 ＋ 会把内容片段插入聊天输入框，插入前会截取一段，避免一次灌入整份大文件。 工作区：一个项目收进多个文件夹 有些工作本来就跨目录：前端一个仓库、后端一个仓库、文档又一个目录。工作区让它们在同一个项目里出现，而不必来回开项目。 - 在「打开项目」弹窗里填名称、一行一个文件夹路径，点「新建工作区」。 - 成员目录显示为文件树上方的胶囊：点一下切成 当前工作文件夹 ，胶囊上的 × 从工作区移除（磁盘上的文件不动），＋ 把一个文件夹加进来。 - 项目名旁会挂一个「工作区」徽标，悬停能看到包含几个文件夹、当前是哪一个。 为什么同一时刻只在一个根上工作：文件写入、Git 操作、终端命令都需要唯一确定的落点。如果同时\"在多个根上\"，一次 git status 或一次文件写入该落在哪儿就成了碰运气。所以工作区收的是 视野 ，不是并发——切换当前根只需要点一下，但任何时刻的动作都清楚地落在你选中的那个目录上。 分项目宪法 宪法是给模型的项目规则（技术边界、代码规范、架构约束）。它可以只属于某一个项目： - 打开着项目时保存宪法，会写进这个项目的 .slate/config.json ，只对这个项目生效；它跟着仓库走，交给同事也还在。 - 项目里没写宪法时，回落全局那份。两份各存各的落点，谁也不覆写谁。 - 设置页宪法框下方那行字会写明这次改的是谁：「正在编辑项目「X」的宪法……」或「正在编辑全局宪法……」。提示词工厂右侧的「项目宪法」读的也是当前生效的那份，见 提示词工厂。 字段名与格式： rules 是一个字符串数组，每条一句话。没有 rules 或数组为空时不注入任何规则。内容不是合法 JSON 时保存会被拒绝，并提示你格式不对。 分项目覆盖：Actions、知识库与 MCP 与宪法同一个口径： 全局一份打底，项目里那一份覆盖它 。三件事的覆盖粒度不同： 覆盖对象 覆盖粒度 项目里放哪儿 怎么摘掉覆盖 --- --- --- --- Actions 流程说明书 同 id 顶掉：项目里放一份同名流程，本项目用这一份；全局那份在别的项目照旧 .slate/actions/<id .yml 删掉项目那份，即回到全局 知识库 同标题顶掉 + 合并：项目那批排前面并顶掉同名全局文档，全局其余照旧可见 归属记在知识库里，不进你的仓库 删掉项目那条知识 MCP 服务器 掩码：只记这台开不开、哪些工具准用，不改全局配置 .slate/config.json 的 mcp.servers 摘掉掩码，即回到跟全局 列表与编辑器都会标明你面对的是哪一份：项目那份带「本项目」徽标，编辑器里写明\"现在改的是哪一份\"。Action 编辑器里还有「在本项目另存一份」，把手上的全局那份另存为项目覆盖版；改的是项目那份时，删除按钮会说「摘掉覆盖」，并提示摘掉后本项目回到全局那一份。 项目的 .slate/config.json 里只记这台服务器开不开（以及可选的工具白名单），地址与密钥始终留在 SLATE 本机的 MCP 配置里： tools 不写或为空表示全部放行。把整个仓库推到远端，也不会带上任何 URL 与密钥。 Better Project Understanding 新接手一个项目时最费时间的不是写代码，而是搞清\"这堆目录到底怎么运转\"。这个功能让 AI 按预算读一遍项目，产出两份文档： - 导览·百科 ：项目概述、技术栈、目录结构解读、核心模块、入口与启动方式（详细档还会梳理数据流与调用链）。 - 规则手册 ：技术边界、代码规范、架构约束、运行与构建命令。每条规则都要求从项目里真实存在的证据推导出来，写成可直接执行的祈使句。 扫描预算是三档： 档位 目录树深度 精读文件 输出规模 --- --- --- --- 简略 2 层 6 个关键文件 约 900 字 平衡 3 层 16 个关键文件 约 1800 字 详细 5 层 36 个关键文件 约 3200 字 精读按优先级挑选：README、依赖清单与构建配置、核心入口文件优先，同级取路径更短的，避免深层生成物挤掉入口文件。生成过程可随时取消；只依据扫到的证据写，无法确定的内容会明确标注\"待确定\"。 结果持久化在项目的 .slate/config.json 里，下次打开项目直接就能看，不必重新生成。两份文档可切换查看、单独复制，也可以换档位重新生成。这一档是全表里最贵的 AI 功能，可以在 设置 → AI 辅助功能 里关掉，或单独指定一个便宜些的模型。 Code Review Code Review 读 git diff ，让 AI 从四个维度做结构化审查。取 diff 有三种方式，适合不同场景： 模式 读什么 什么时候用 --- --- --- 未暂存变更 工作区里改了但还没 git add 的内容 提交前自查手上这一批改动 已暂存变更 已经 git add 、准备提交的内容 把关即将进入提交的那一批 提交范围 指定起点到终点（终点默认 HEAD）之间的提交 复盘一次提交或一段分支上的几笔改动 四个审查维度：代码质量（命名、风格、逻辑、重复、错误处理）、安全性（注入、敏感信息泄露、认证授权、输入处理）、性能（复杂度、无谓计算、内存风险、N+1 查询）、可维护性（耦合、接口设计、注释、测试暗示）。结果有三种视图： - 完整报告 ：总体评价 + 四个维度的分析，Markdown 排版。 - 行级评论列表 ：按严重程度排序的卡片，严重程度分「严重 / 重要 / 建议 / 信息」四档；点卡片上的 文件:行号 会把定位复制到剪贴板，方便你回到编辑器里跳过去。 - 四维度卡片 ：四个维度并排，各自摘要与相关评论条数。 报告可以整份复制；换模式或换范围重新审一次即可。前提是这个目录确实是 Git 仓库并且有变更——不是 Git 仓库会直接告诉你，没有变更则明说\"无需审查\"。变更过大时会截断，并在摘要里提示。所用模型可以在 设置 → AI 辅助功能 → 代码审查 里单独指定。 跨项目并行运行 切换项目不再打断正在生成的对话。相关设置都在 设置 → 多任务与项目： 设置 默认 说明 --- --- --- 切走会话时让它继续跑 开 离开的那一场在后台继续，进度与结局落进右栏任务中心；关掉即旧行为：切换会话就中断这一场 同时最多跑几场 2 跨项目并行的总上限，可选 1–4 同一个项目内最多几场 1 同项目默认串行，可选 1–3 超出上限的发送不会被吞掉：它会进队列，输入框上方与右栏任务中心都会写明「排队中」以及还有几场在等，前一场收工后自动接上。停止按钮在任何设置下都只停屏幕上这一场。 后台那一场用它自己的项目现场干活——文件、终端、宪法、知识库片段都按它所属的项目走，不会写进你现在正看着的那个项目。每条会话的 Token 各记各的，用量条只反映屏幕上这一场。 写进项目 ：只对这一个仓库成立的规则、流程与知识——\"这个仓库用 pnpm\"\"改前必须先跑 lint\"\"这套部署脚本的坑\"。它们跟着代码走，换机器、交接同事都还在。 留在全局 ：跨项目成立的东西——你偏好的表达方式、通用工作流、常用的资料摘录。全局那份是所有项目的兜底，项目里没写时就用它。 判断方法很简单：把这份内容复制到另一个项目里用，会不会产生误导？会，就写进项目。"},{"t":"提示词工厂","u":"zh/15-prompt-factory.html","s":"项目与数据","h":["提示词工厂干什么","界面上有哪三块","产出交给谁","组装顺序","任务模板","输出语气","生成结果长什么样","宪法从哪来","与磨墨模式的分工","与目标模式的分工","生成后怎么用"],"x":"提示词工厂干什么 把一个已经想清楚的任务，整理成一份可以直接粘给外部 Coding Agent 的 Prompt：上下文、项目规则、约束、交付要求各就各位，不必每次从头手写。 SLATE 在这里的角色是 组织者 ，不是执行者：它负责把上下文与约束摆好，真正的代码修改交给外部工具去做。 界面上有哪三块 区域 有什么 你怎么用 --- --- --- 左侧工作台 目标 Agent / 任务类型 / 输出语气三个下拉，任务描述、相关上下文、约束条件三个输入框 主要在这里写东西 右侧栏 项目宪法、可引用文件、质量检查 勾文件、核对缺项 下方结果区 生成的 Prompt，以及复制、下载 Markdown、保存为素材三颗按钮 生成后在这里取走 工作台顶部还有两颗：引用最近对话、清空。 产出交给谁 生成时先选目标 Agent，不同预设会改写「执行对象」与「交付要求」两段： 目标 Agent 生成时追加的定位 交付要求 --- --- --- Codex 在当前代码库中实现、验证并说明结果 直接完成修改，最后说明改动文件、验证方式与剩余风险 Claude Code 按现有工程风格完成代码修改 先读相关文件，再做最小必要修改，最后给出测试结果 Cursor 作为编辑器内任务指令使用 给出清晰的编辑步骤、目标文件与需要保留的行为 通用 Agent 交给任意 Coding Agent 执行 输出可执行方案、必要修改与验证清单 组装顺序 点「生成 Prompt」后，SLATE 按下表顺序拼装正式文本。空段落会被整段略过，所以生成结果里没有哪一段是占位的。 顺序 段落 填什么 怎么写更好 --- --- --- --- 1 # 任务 要做什么、为什么做、怎样算完成（必填） 把验收标准直接写进任务里；只写\"优化一下\"最容易被自由发挥 2 # 执行对象 交给谁、让它怎么交付 由目标 Agent 预设决定，可换预设 3 # 工作方式 先探索还是直接动手、要不要先审风险 由「输出语气」决定：直接执行 / 谨慎审计 / 先探索再实现；并固定要求\"阅读现有代码后再行动，优先沿用项目已有模式\" 4 # 项目宪法 当前生效的宪法规则，逐条编号 来自项目或全局那一份，见 项目、工作区与分项目配置；没有规则时不出现 5 # 重点文件 你勾选的文件路径清单 给路径比粘贴正文更省 Token，外部 Agent 会自己去读 6 # 已知上下文 报错、接口约定、关键代码片段、最近对话摘要 只放对方看不到的信息；这里的内容会原样带过去 7 # 约束条件 不能改什么、必须保留什么、要遵守哪些风格或流程 与宪法互补：宪法管长期，这里管这一单 8 # 交付要求 由预设决定，另附固定两条 固定要求：不修改与任务无关的文件、遇到已有未提交改动先理解并兼容；完成后说明验证命令或无法验证的原因 任务模板 不想从空白开始，可以先选一个任务类型，它会预填任务描述与约束条件： 任务类型 预填的骨架 --- --- 功能实现 实现 / 期望行为 / 验收标准；约束：遵循现有架构与风格、改动范围尽量小、完成后跑检查 Bug 修复 问题现象 / 复现路径 / 期望结果；约束：先定位根因、不回滚无关改动、补上能覆盖该问题的验证 代码审查 审查对象 / 重点关注；约束：按严重程度列问题，每条带位置、风险与修复建议 重构整理 重构目标 / 保持不变的行为 / 希望改善的问题；约束：外部行为兼容、不引入大型新依赖、沿用既有模式 模板只在对应输入框为空时填入，不会覆盖你已经写好的内容。 输出语气 「输出语气」只影响生成结果里 # 工作方式 那一段，与任务类型互不干扰： 输出语气 生成时写进 Prompt 的那句 --- --- 直接执行 请直接进入执行，不需要长篇方案铺垫 谨慎审计 请先审计风险和边界，再做修改 先探索再实现 请先给出 2-3 个可行方向，再选择最稳妥方案执行 三种语气都会附上同一句要求：阅读现有代码后再行动，优先沿用项目已有模式。 生成结果长什么样 结果就是一份按上面顺序拼好的 Markdown，段落用 # 标题分隔： 拿到手就是正式文本，可以直接整份贴给外部工具，不需要再补前言。 宪法从哪来 右侧栏的「项目宪法」显示当前生效的那份规则，点「插入项目宪法」会把它们按条编号写进约束条件。规则来自项目自己的 .slate/config.json ；项目没写则用全局那份，切换项目时右侧栏会跟着换。完整口径见 项目、工作区与分项目配置。 与磨墨模式的分工 两者都在\"把想法变成文本\"，差别在起点： 磨墨模式 提示词工厂 --- --- --- 起点 一句粗糙想法，还不知道具体要什么 任务已经明确，只是要一个交付格式 做什么 AI 三段式追问（接墨 → 磨墨 → 收墨），一次问一个，把缺口问出来 把已知信息填进固定骨架，拼成正式文本 产出 结构化任务书（墨稿）：目标 / 受众 / 交付物 / 验收标准 / 边界 / 建议路径 / 遗留问题 一份可以直接交给外部 Agent 的 Prompt 下一步 送入目标模式执行、投到白板推演、存为模板复用 复制、下载或存为素材，交给外部工具执行 分不清时看一句话：还说不清要什么，去 磨墨模式；已经说得清了，来工厂。 与目标模式的分工 目标模式让 SLATE 自己动手：它有自己的工具（读写文件、跑命令、查 Git），按六阶段闭环跑到验证通过为止，见 Agent Autopilot 与目标模式。提示词工厂不执行任何东西，它产出的是 给别人执行的 Prompt ——你想用 Claude Code、Codex 或 Cursor 来干活，就用工厂。 生成后怎么用 1. 逐段核对 ：生成结果显示在下方，从 # 任务 往下看一遍，重点检查验收标准与边界有没有漏。 2. 复制 / 下载 / 存素材 ：结果区右上角三颗按钮分别是复制、下载 Markdown、保存为素材。素材会进你的提示词素材库，下次可以直接取用。 3. 迭代 ：改一处输入再点一次「生成 Prompt」即可——输出是整份重新拼的，不会叠加。想推倒重来点「清空」。 右侧的「质量检查」会在你写的过程中实时给四项提示：任务是否足够明确、是否包含上下文或文件、是否声明了约束边界、是否选了目标 Agent。四项全亮不保证结果一定好，但缺项通常意味着这份 Prompt 还得回来补。 两个速记入口：「引用最近对话」把当前会话最近几条消息摘要塞进已知上下文；「插入勾选文件」读取右侧勾选的文件内容（一次最多 8 个，每个取前 6000 字符）。文件列表读的是当前项目目录，没打开项目时会提示你先打开项目。 外部 Agent 最怕两件事：不知道什么时候算干完，不知道哪里不能碰。 - 验收标准写成可判定的事实 ：\" npm test 全绿\"比\"测试通过\"清楚；\"接口返回字段与签名不变\"比\"保持兼容\"清楚。 - 边界写成明确的禁令与保护 ：\"不要动 src/legacy/ 下的文件\"\"保留现有公共 API 签名\"\"不新增依赖\"。 - 顺带写清不做什么 ：说明\"这次只改这一个文件\"，比让 Agent 自己猜改动范围安全得多。"},{"t":"任务清单与定时任务","u":"zh/16-schedule.html","s":"项目与数据","h":["任务清单是什么","清单面板怎么看","清单由谁建、状态怎么变","待办与移动端","定时任务是干什么的","新建一条定时任务","无人值守的提示词怎么写","三种调度方式","事件驱动触发","结果去哪了","怎么管理","什么时候不会执行"],"x":"任务清单是什么 目标模式跑多步骤任务时，模型会先建一份 TODOLIST，把大任务拆成可核对的小项，再逐项推进。这份清单不是装饰：未全部了结之前，模型不被允许宣称完成。 清单按对话隔离——每条会话各有一份，互不干扰，重开这条会话时还在。 清单面板怎么看 清单实时显示在消息区右侧栏： - 头部报 任务清单 {done}/{total} ，旁边补上「受阻 n」与「进行中 n」，并有一条进度条。 - 每一项带状态标记：待办、进行中、已完成、受阻。 - 每完成一项或一批，模型会立即同步状态，进度不必等收尾才更新。 顶栏的「任务清单」按钮可以整栏收起，把宽度还给消息区，再点一下展开。折叠之后进度不至于彻底隐身——按钮悬停会报 {done}/{total} 。这一场没有清单时按钮置灰。折叠状态记在你本机：刷新、重启都保持，清单继续往下跑也不会擅自把栏撑回来。 清单由谁建、状态怎么变 清单不是你手动维护的东西。目标模式下遇到多文件、多步骤、排查或实现类任务时，模型会先建清单再动手；简单的单步任务会跳过清单直接做，所以看不到清单也是正常的。 每一项的状态随执行推进变化： 状态 含义 --- --- 待办 还没轮到 进行中 正在做这一项 已完成 做完了，并且验证过 受阻 被外部条件挡住（缺依赖、等确认、环境不具备），模型会在正文里说明原因 全部条目了结之前，模型不被允许写收尾汇报；系统会把剩下的条目念回给它，催它继续推进。 待办与移动端 手机端底部有「待办」Tab（面板标题「待办 / 定时」），可以查看、新建、立即运行、删除定时任务；在电脑上建的任务，手机打开就是同一份列表。详见 移动端遥控与局域网访问。 定时任务是干什么的 定时任务 = 一条预设提示词 + 一个触发时机。到点后由 SLATE 本地执行，把提示词交给指定模型跑一遍，结果落成一条独立会话——不需要你守在旁边，也不占用你正在用的那场对话。 新建一条任务需要四样东西：名称、提示词（到点后发给模型的那段话）、模型、模式。 新建一条定时任务 入口在聊天输入框左侧的 ＋ 菜单里（选「定时任务」），面板标题是「定时 / 事件任务」；手机端在底部「待办」Tab 点「＋ 新建」。 1. 填 任务名称 ，它会出现在那次执行生成的会话标题里，写成能一眼认出的名字。 2. 写 提示词 ：到点发给模型的那段话，写清要它产出什么。 3. 选 模型 ：下拉里按国产 / 国际 / 本地 / 自定义分组，默认是你当前用的模型。 4. 选 模式 ：每天 / 单次 / 间隔，再填时间点或间隔分钟数。 5. 点「+ 添加任务」，任务出现在下方列表里，随时可以停用或删除。 无人值守的提示词怎么写 定时任务每次都是新会话，没有前文可以依赖；执行时的系统提示也要求模型直接、完整地执行指令并输出结果，不要反问。所以提示词要自包含： - 写清产出格式 ：例如\"用 Markdown 列出三块：昨天完成的事项、未完成的事项、今天的建议顺序\"。 - 写清信息从哪来 ：例如\"读工作目录下的 TODO.md 与最近的 Git 提交记录\"，而不是\"接着上次说的继续\"。 - 不要写需要你回答才能往下走的分支 ——到点时没人回应它。 - 长度与结构写死在提示词里 ，否则输出长短全凭模型当天的状态。 三种调度方式 调度方式 怎么算时间 适合什么 --- --- --- 单次 指定一个时间点，只执行一次 一次性的提醒或一次性汇总；跑完任务自动停用 每天 指定一个时间点，每天执行；今天这个点还没到就等，到了且今天没跑过就跑 每日汇总、每天收工前的例行检查 固定间隔 每 N 分钟执行一次（默认 60 分钟，最小 1 分钟） 需要反复盯着的短动作，比如隔一段时间看一眼构建状态 事件驱动触发 除了看时钟，任务还可以被事件推着走：事件发生时自动执行，结果同样归档成独立会话。三种事件来源： 事件 触发条件 可配置项 --- --- --- 文件变更 监听路径下有文件被新建或修改 监听的文件 / 目录路径（每行一个，不填按任务所属项目的根目录）；冷却时间 Git push 检测 指定仓库的 HEAD 发生变化 仓库路径；冷却时间 Webhook 接收 外部系统向该任务的回调地址发起请求 密钥（用于校验来源，可选；请求正文会作为上下文注入提示词） 冷却时间是最小触发间隔（默认 60 秒，最小 10 秒），目的是防止一次批量改动把任务反复刷起来。事件任务的提示词里会自动附上触发上下文——变更的文件、最新提交、Webhook 内容——所以模型知道\"这次是被什么叫起来的\"。 事件类型与监听路径随任务一起保存，改完即生效。文件变更与 Git 检测在 SLATE 启动时会先记一份当前快照，所以刚启动不会把之前的历史改动当成新事件误触发。 结果去哪了 每次执行都会新建一条独立会话，标题带前缀与时间： 会话落在任务所属的项目名下——任务是在哪个项目里建的，结果就挂在哪个项目，切到别的项目后按项目筛选也不会错认。点进会话就是完整的提示词与模型回复。 怎么管理 任务列表里每一行都能看到：调度方式 · 所用模型 · 上次运行结果（跑过会写运行时间，失败会直接写原因，没跑过显示\"尚未运行\"）。 - 勾选框启用 / 停用，随时挂起而不必删除。 - ▶ 立即运行，不等下一次到点；已经在执行的任务不会被重复触发。 - × 删除任务。 什么时候不会执行 定时任务由本地调度器执行，所以有几个边界值得先知道： - 只有 SLATE 在运行时才会到点执行。应用关闭期间不会执行；重新打开后，当天时间点已过且今天还没跑过的任务会补跑一次。 - 所用模型必须已经配置好 API Key，并且必须是 OpenAI 兼容的模型。拿不到 Key 或模型不兼容时，原因会写进任务状态，不会静默失败。 - 如果「设置 → AI 辅助功能」里关掉了「定时与事件任务」，任务不再执行；触发记录仍保留，状态里会写明是被这一项关掉的。详见 设置与安全审批。 适合 ：不需要你中途拍板的固定动作。汇总昨天的进展、定时检查仓库状态、隔一段时间看一眼构建结果、有文件变更就提醒——这类任务模型一次答复就能收尾。 不适合 ：需要你上来做选择的长任务。定时任务不会中途停下来问你，也不会等你确认再继续。把需要决策的部分写成\"列出选项、说明各自代价\"，把决定权留给自己，比让它替你拍板稳妥。"},{"t":"数据、备份与存储","u":"zh/17-data.html","s":"项目与数据","h":["你的数据都在自己机器上","历史会话管理","消息级操作","一键备份与恢复","存储空间管理","跨设备同步与本机偏好","卸载或换电脑：迁移三步"],"x":"这一页解决三个问题：你的数据存在哪里、怎么备份与恢复、存储变大了该动哪一项。SLATE 是本地优先的工具，对话、记忆与设置都留在你自己这台机器上，API Key 也只在调用模型时使用。 你的数据都在自己机器上 数据分两层：一层在数据库里，一层在数据目录的文件与文件夹里。你只需要记住「本机数据目录」这个粒度，删除或拷贝时整个目录一起处理。 存的是什么 存在哪一层 说明 --- --- --- 会话与消息 本机数据目录 · 数据库 标题、消息正文、所用模型、Token 用量 长期记忆与用户画像 本机数据目录 · 数据库 自动沉淀的记忆条目，跨会话召回 提示词素材 本机数据目录 · 数据库 提示词工厂沉淀下来的片段 知识库文档 本机数据目录 · 数据库 知识片段与「灵光」归档 流程说明书 / 专家包 / 自定义技能 本机数据目录 · 文件与文件夹 流程说明书是单个文件，专家包与技能各自一个目录 偏好设置 本机数据目录 · 配置文件 主题、模型与密钥、审批模式、各项开关 备份文件 本机数据目录 · 备份文件夹 每次「下载备份」都会再留一份在本机 本机数据目录就是你的全部家当。要么用「下载备份」导出一份 JSON，要么直接把整个数据目录拷到新机器上。 历史会话管理 侧栏「任务」列表里，每一条会话都带着自己的信息与操作： - 搜索框 ：输入即过滤标题；输入 2 个字符以上还会做内容全文检索，命中后以「内容命中 · N 条」列出会话标题与上下文摘录，点一下直接跳到那场会话。 - 悬停按钮 ：鼠标移到会话上，右侧出现三个按钮——重命名、导出 Markdown（按钮上标着 MD ）、删除（ × ）。导出会生成一份包含全部问答的 Markdown 文件，文件名带会话标题与日期。 - 归档 ：会话行的操作里有一颗「归档」，按下去这一场就从「任务」栏离场，消息一条不动。正在生成的那场不能归档（先停下来再收）。归档的会话在「设置 → 会话归档」里逐条恢复或删除——恢复后它重新出现在任务栏，删除会连同消息一起清掉、找不回来。 - 排序下拉 ：在侧栏头部，可切换最近更新 / 按项目 / 按状态 / 创建时间 / 按用量。 - 管理 ：侧栏头部的「管理」按钮打开「管理历史会话」弹窗，逐条勾选后可「删除选中」，也可以直接「清空全部」；顶部「全部」勾选框一次选中当前所有会话。 删除会话、删除消息、清空全部都无法撤销。删除前建议先在「数据备份」里导出一份。 消息级操作 在消息区，每条消息右上角都有一排小按钮： 按钮 作用 适用范围 --- --- --- 复制 复制这条消息的正文 所有消息 编辑内容 就地改成输入框，保存后写回并重新渲染 用户消息 删除此消息 从这场会话里移除这一条 用户消息 重新生成 以这条之前的历史重新请求一次回答 最后一条助手消息 编辑或删除消息只影响这一条，不会动到其他会话。想了解对话本身的用法，见 对话与消息。 一键备份与恢复 一次导出，把「对话、记忆、素材与设置」全部收进一个 JSON 文件。 备份包含什么 - 全部会话与消息、长期记忆、提示词素材，以及用量记录。 - 本机偏好设置：模型与 API Key、自定义模型、主题、审批模式、AI 辅助功能开关等。 - 所以备份文件里含你的 API Key，不要随手发给别人。 什么时候该备份 - 卸载、重装或换电脑之前。 - 准备执行「清空对话」这类不可逆操作之前。 - 在做大批量整理、删除会话之前。 导入会发生什么 - 会话、消息、记忆与素材按 ID 合并：已存在的数据会跳过，不会覆盖你现有的内容。 - 本机偏好会被备份里那一份替换，导入完成后页面会自动重载以应用设置。 - 导入前会弹一次确认，确认后才会开始。 备份是「合并进当前数据」，不是「清空再恢复」。你当前这台机器上的偏好设置会被备份里的那份覆盖；如果两边都存了你想保留的模型密钥，导入后可能不符合预期。拿不准就先导出一份当前的备份，再导入旧的。 存储空间管理 在「设置 → 数据备份」区域下方是「存储空间」，先显示总占用，再按分项列出各自的大小。 分项 里面是什么 可以做什么 什么时候该用 --- --- --- --- 对话历史数据库 会话与消息、长期记忆、提示词素材、用量 压缩、清空对话 删掉很多会话后想回收空间；或历史太杂想从头开始 内置浏览器缓存 内置浏览器运行产生的缓存文件 清理缓存 页面显示异常，或这一项明显变大 自定义技能 你导入或新建的技能文档 不可清理 建议保留 配置文件 主题、模型与密钥、各项开关 不可清理 建议保留 其他 知识库、日志、流程说明书、专家包等 不可清理 建议保留 三个操作的区别： - 压缩 ：把数据库里已删除数据占用的空间真正还给磁盘，不动任何现有内容。适合「刚删了很多会话」之后。 - 清空对话 ：删除全部会话与消息，长期记忆、知识库与偏好设置不受影响。不可恢复，操作前会有一次确认。 - 清理缓存 ：清掉内置浏览器的缓存文件。运行中被占用的文件会在下次启动后彻底释放。 跨设备同步与本机偏好 如果你用手机遥控同一个 SLATE，一部分偏好会跟着你走，另一部分刻意只留在这台机器上。 偏好 是否跨设备 --- --- 侧栏任务列表排序 跨设备同步 主题与明暗、通用 UI 模式 跨设备同步 模型与 API Key、自定义模型、当前模型 跨设备同步 审批模式、推理强度、AI 辅助功能开关 跨设备同步 通知开关、联网搜索、图片与视频生成配置 跨设备同步 阅读进度与未读标志（需要操作 / 出错 / 进行中 / 已完成未查看） 只留本机 任务清单栏的折叠状态 只留本机 Continue Autopilot 与多任务并行相关开关 只留本机 阅读进度只在本机，是因为「我有没有看过这一场」是这台设备的感受：在另一台设备上看过，不应该让这边也变成已读。 卸载或换电脑：迁移三步 1. 在旧机器上打开「设置 → 数据备份」，点「下载备份」，确认拿到 SLATE-backup-日期.json 。 2. 在新机器上装好 SLATE 并启动，进入「设置 → 数据备份」。 3. 点「导入备份」，选择刚才那个 JSON，确认后等待页面自动重载。 迁移后如发现知识库文档、流程说明书、专家包或自定义技能没有跟过来，可以把旧机器上的本机数据目录整个拷到新机器对应位置——它们不在那份 JSON 里，而是以文件与文件夹的形式保存。模型与 API Key 若未随备份导入，在新机器的模型配置里重新填一次即可。 备份是某一时刻的快照。反复在同一份旧备份上叠加导入，并不会合并出「最新状态」，还会让新旧记录混杂。要持续同步，直接用局域网遥控访问同一台机器，见 移动端遥控与局域网访问。"},{"t":"移动端遥控与局域网访问","u":"zh/18-mobile.html","s":"移动与安全","h":["能干什么","怎么连上","为什么桌面界面没变","五个底部 Tab","遥控时的分工","什么时候用遥控、什么时候回到电脑前","移动端对话的能力","移动端设置能改什么","鉴权与风险","连不上怎么排查"],"x":"这一页解决「人离开电脑，但活还没干完」的问题：手机或平板连上同一个局域网，就能用浏览器遥控桌面上的 SLATE，界面会自动切换成专门的移动端样子。SLATE 启动时就已经把局域网访问开好了，不需要额外配置。 能干什么 - 查看正在跑的对话与工具调用进度，接着聊下去。 - 新建会话、切换会话、重命名与删除。 - 增删改长期记忆，查看与管理待办（定时任务）。 - 切换模型、填 API Key、切换深色模式。 - 高危命令与文件改动会弹底部面板让你确认，和桌面是同一套判断规则。 怎么连上 1. 让手机与电脑连在同一个局域网（同一个 Wi-Fi）。 2. 在电脑上打开「设置 → 局域网遥控」，会看到一条访问地址与一个二维码。 3. 手机扫码，或把地址复制到手机浏览器打开，即可进入专属移动端界面。 地址里的端口默认是 8001；这个端口被占用时 SLATE 会自动向后找一个可用端口，所以以设置页显示的地址为准。顶栏的「遥控」按钮也能直接跳到这一块设置。 为什么桌面界面没变 移动端界面是独立入口，只有手机与平板这一类设备访问局域网地址时才会进入。你在电脑浏览器里看到的仍然是完整的三栏桌面工作台，功能、布局、快捷键都不受影响，属于零回归。 在地址后面加上 slate mobile=0 再打开，就会停留在桌面界面。平板上屏幕够大、又想用完整布局时可以这样进。 五个底部 Tab Tab 主要用途 具体能做什么 --- --- --- 对话 接着干活 看流式输出、思考折叠卡与工具卡片，继续追问或停止本轮 会话 找历史 会话列表，新建、切换、重命名、删除 记忆 管长期记忆 增删改查，新建时选择分类 待办 管定时任务 查看下次触发、启用停用、立即运行、删除、新建 设置 调整本机偏好 模型切换、推理强度、密钥、外观、遥控信息 对话 ：输入框固定在底部，回车发送、 Shift + Enter 换行，输入 @ 弹出提及候选。消息按气泡呈现，模型的思考过程收在可折叠卡片里，工具调用显示成带状态的工具卡；生成过程中上翻不会被自动滚动打断。 会话 ：与桌面共用同一份后端数据，手机上改过的标题、新建的会话，回到电脑刷新就能看到；反过来也一样。在手机上继续过的那场会话，桌面上旧的「已完成未查看」之类标志会自动作废，不会留下过时的结论。 记忆 ：一条条列出长期记忆，点进去可编辑内容或改分类，也可以直接删除；新建记忆时填内容并选一个分类，之后在对话里会被自动召回。 待办 ：每个定时任务是一行，可查看它的时间或间隔、临时启用或停用、点「立即运行」立刻执行一次，以及删除。新建时依次填名称、提示词、模型、模式与时间或间隔。 设置 ：一屏之内改完手机上最需要的几件事，改完立即生效。 遥控时的分工 移动端是「遥控器」，不是第二份数据： - 数据仍是同一份：会话、记忆、待办都存在桌面那台机器的本机数据目录里，手机只是操作它。 - 任务仍在电脑上跑：模型请求与工具执行发生在电脑端，手机只是发起与查看。所以关掉电脑上的 SLATE，遥控就结束了。 - 运行时请不要让电脑休眠：休眠会中断正在跑的生成与后台任务。 - 桌面上的窗口不需要保持在前台，但应用要处于运行状态。 什么时候用遥控、什么时候回到电脑前 场景 建议 --- --- 长任务跑到一半要出门 用遥控看进度，必要时补一句指令 只想改个标题、删个会话、看看记忆 遥控足够 批量整理历史、往模板里塞大段上下文 回桌面，屏幕大、操作快 管理技能、编辑流程说明书、配置 MCP Server 回桌面，这些区块只在桌面设置页 多人协作、把界面投给别人看 不建议，遥控地址等于钥匙 移动端对话的能力 - 流式输出 ：逐字出现，和桌面同一套协议；上翻时不会被自动滚动打断，贴底时自动跟随。 - 工具循环 ：与桌面共用同一条 Agent 循环逻辑，模型能观察、执行、验证、汇报；界面同源，工具卡的标题不会退化成裸英文工具名。 - 高危命令确认 ：命中写死的高危规则时，弹出底部面板，写明命令与触发规则，并附一句由模型生成的命令目的说明，由你决定批准还是拒绝。 - 文件变更确认 ：文件编辑与新建会先给你看差异，选择接受才落盘，拒绝则不写入。 - @ 提及 ：输入 @ 弹出候选浮层，可提及技能、工具与项目文件，支持上下键选择、Tab 或点击插入。 - 停止真取消 ：点停止会连同工具调用的流式通道一起关闭，后端终止对应的子进程与在跑任务。 移动端设置能改什么 设置项 说明 --- --- 模型选择 按国产 / 国际 / 本地 / 自定义分组列出，点一下即切换，并显示每个模型的上下文预算 推理强度 档位随当前模型能力给出（自动 / 关 / 低 / 中 / 高），与桌面输入框那个选择器是同一份状态 密钥 为当前模型填入或清除 API Key 外观 深色模式开关；顶部状态条还有一个快捷的主题切换按钮。电脑上开了自定义主题时，这一项会写明「已锁定」，点切换只会说明原因，不会换色 遥控信息 显示带授权的访问地址与二维码，可一键复制 鉴权与风险 局域网访问靠地址里自带的授权码放行：只有通过设置页给出的那条地址（或它的二维码）进入的设备才能访问，地址里没有授权码的访问会被拦在一张「需要授权」的页面上。授权码保存在设备上，之后再用同一浏览器访问不必重复扫码。 这条带授权码的地址就是钥匙。任何拿到它的设备，只要连进同一个局域网，就能打开并操作你的 SLATE——读文件、跑命令、看全部历史会话。不要在群里、论坛或截图里暴露这条地址。不再需要遥控时，关掉电脑端的 SLATE 即可让地址失效。 安全建议 ：这是把本机服务开放到局域网，建议只在你自己能信任的网络里使用。在咖啡厅、酒店、机场这类公共 Wi-Fi 下，关闭 SLATE 或不要使用遥控功能。 连不上怎么排查 1. 手机和电脑是否在同一个 Wi-Fi ：手机用的是蜂窝数据、或连了另一个频段的访客网络，都不算同一个局域网。 2. 电脑防火墙是否拦了端口 ：第一次开放局域网访问时，系统可能弹出防火墙询问，需要选择允许；若当时点了拒绝，去防火墙设置里为 SLATE 放行。 3. 地址是否用了电脑的局域网 IP ：要用设置页显示的那条地址，而不是本机回环地址。 4. 是否被访客网络隔离 ：不少路由器默认开启「AP 隔离」，同一 Wi-Fi 下设备之间也互相看不见，需要在路由器设置里关掉，或换成手机热点。 按现象对照排查会更快： 现象 最可能的原因 怎么办 --- --- --- 页面一直转圈打不开 电脑不在运行，或端口被防火墙拦了 确认电脑上 SLATE 在运行；为它放行防火墙 打开后停在「需要授权」页 地址漏了授权码 回到设置页复制完整地址，或重新扫码 平板打开后是桌面界面 平板被判为桌面设备 属预期行为，功能不受影响；想用移动端界面请用手机浏览器打开同一条地址 昨天能连，今天不行 电脑换了网络，IP 变了 重新打开设置页，用新显示的地址 只有一台手机连不上 访客网络隔离或系统限流 检查路由器 AP 隔离，或换手机热点试一次 其他还要看的：设置页若提示「遥控服务未启动」，重启应用即可；地址里的端口被别的程序占用时 SLATE 会自动换一个，所以始终以设置页当下显示的地址为准。 电脑换了 Wi-Fi、路由器重新分配了地址，访问链接就会跟着变；端口被占用时也会换。所以换网络之后别用旧地址，回到设置页重新看一眼再扫码。"},{"t":"设置与安全审批","u":"zh/19-settings.html","s":"移动与安全","h":["设置页分区总览","AI 辅助功能","Continue Autopilot","任务完成通知","主题与语言","关于区块","安全与审批"],"x":"这一页说明设置页里各分区分别管什么，以及 SLATE 动手之前会怎么征求你的同意。设置页从顶栏「设置」进入，左栏导航由页面上的区块现场生成、与区块标题一一对应，顶部还有一格搜索：输入关键词只留下含这个词的区块并把命中的那一行描出来，回车或点击直接跳过去，清除后原样回来。 设置页分区总览 分区 管什么 --- --- 模型配置 添加自定义模型与内置模型密钥、配置 Base URL、每个模型一行「最大上下文」（滑杆 + 精确数字框 + 探测窗口）、可选启用 Responses API 模式 API 测试 用一条最短请求验证某个模型的密钥与端点是否可用 用量统计 全部对话的累计 Token 用量，随对话自动更新 上下文与项目 上下文 Token 上限、单次输出上限、文件写入是否自动确认、项目宪法 自动推进 回复疑似停顿时的补调策略，以及 Continue Autopilot AI 辅助功能 对话以外的耗 Token 功能逐项开关与单独选模型 通知 大任务结束时的提示音与系统通知 后台任务 长耗时命令走独立通道后的唤醒开关：任务有消息时自动叫醒模型、每个对话最多自动续跑几次 多任务与项目 切走会话时是否让它后台继续跑、同时最多跑几场 通用 UI 极简 Codex 风格布局开关（实验性） 自定义主题 配色（四个源色推导整套）、字体（系统字体族 + 导入自己的字体文件）、板块透明度、背景图与遮罩浓度，可从 Wallpaper Engine 取当前壁纸；10 套基础预设，开启时明暗切换锁定 联网搜索 默认搜索引擎、网页 JS 渲染策略 图片生成 生成类工具的模型与 API Key 视频生成 生成类工具的模型与 API Key 审批模式 每个对话默认的审批档位 数据备份与恢复 备份导出与导入 会话归档 从「任务」栏归档起来的会话，在这里逐条恢复或删除 存储空间 存储占用与管理 局域网遥控 遥控地址与二维码 打字小游戏 顺手测手速，题库可自定义 关于 SLATE 版本、检查更新、新手引导、官网与 GitHub 「模型能用什么」——技能、内置工具、回复模式、新功能（工具工厂自己生产的工具）、MCP、专家包、Actions——全在顶栏「扩展」那一页管理，设置页不再兼做能力清单。详见 内置工具与技能。 AI 辅助功能 除对话、团队对话与提示词工厂之外，每一处会自己去找模型要结果的功能都在这里挂号，共 15 项。每一项都能单独关掉，也能单独指定模型；不指定时跟随当前主模型。 功能 触发方式 关掉后 --- --- --- 上下文压缩摘要 自动 不再自动压缩，手动点「压缩」也不发请求 记忆自动蒸馏 自动 不再自动沉淀长期记忆 记忆手动提取 手动 记忆面板的「从当前对话提取」不再发请求 对话洞察捕获 自动 不再自动生成灵光卡片 自动会话标题 自动（首轮跑完） 新会话保持「首条消息前 30 字」的占位标题，不再起名 代码理解文档 手动 扫描项目后不再生成导览与规则手册 代码审查 手动 只取 diff，不再做四维度审查 黑板 AI 整理 手动 黑板内容不再交给模型重排 提示词优化 手动 输入框那颗按钮照常点，但不再发请求，只提示一句「已关闭」 子代理并行 模型调用 工具从工具目录消失，模型看不到也就不会调 高危命令目的说明 自动 审批弹窗照常拦，只是不再生成那句命令目的 团队工作流执行 手动 工作流不再逐节点请求模型 定时与事件任务 自动 任务不再执行，触发记录仍保留 AI 图片生成 模型调用 工具从工具目录消失 AI 视频生成 模型调用 工具从工具目录消失 列表上方会实时汇总「共 15 项，已关闭 N 项」。关掉即一次模型请求都不发，这是最直接、也最可控的节流手段。 逐项关掉你根本用不上的功能，比事后压缩上下文更省。日常只做对话与工具执行的话，压缩、蒸馏、洞察、图片、视频这些都可以先关掉，需要时再打开。 Continue Autopilot 在「自动推进」区块里，默认开启。它的作用是：Autopilot 或目标模式的轮数用完了，并不等于活干完了。如果末轮还在执行工具，就把上限往后推 8 轮（最多推 3 次）让手上的活做完；如果末轮空手停笔，就把清单里剩下的项念回给模型，催它继续推进或明确写下任务完成。 散场之后还有一层：这一场不是因为「完成」退出的、而是因为轮数用尽停下的，只要它不在手动审批档（自动审批 / 完全访问 / 夜间模式），SLATE 就替你按下那颗「继续跑完」——等这一场彻底空下来（压缩、蒸馏这些收尾还要占几秒）再发，一场最多自动续两次，界面 toast 会写明「轮数用尽 · 已自动续跑（第 1/2 次）」。手动审批档不在此列：那一档的语义就是每一步都要人点头，所以它照旧只把「继续跑完」条挂出来等你。额度用尽、或续跑没接上时，那颗手动按钮仍然在原地。 关掉它就回到旧行为：轮数用完即停，需要你自己说一声继续。这个开关只留在本机，不随局域网遥控同步。相关机制见 Agent Autopilot 与目标模式。 任务完成通知 大任务结束时（目标模式、团队讨论、工作流跑完）可以给你一个提醒，两个开关互相独立： 开关 默认 说明 --- --- --- 任务完成时播放提示音效 开启 短促提示音，无需任何授权 任务完成时发送系统通知 关闭 需要浏览器授予通知权限，未授权时区块下方会给出提示 两个都关掉也不影响任务本身，只是不再提醒。适合把长任务挂后台、你去做别的事的场景，开着能在收工时立刻回到屏幕前。 主题与语言 - 明暗主题 ：用顶栏的「明暗」按钮一键切换，移动端在顶部状态条右侧也有同一个按钮。选择会跨设备同步，手机上切了，电脑上刷新即一致。 - 自定义主题 ：这一区块只填四个源色——底色、面板色、正文色、强调色——其余三十几个颜色令牌由它们推导出来。为什么不逐个开放：令牌逐个改，很容易改出一版正文读不清的四不像，而源色只有四个，推导时按亮度分深浅两套、按对比度兜底，配色就必然成套。代码块在任何配色下都保持深底亮字，危险 / 完成 / 需要操作三种状态色整组跟随底色亮度切换，不跟着你的强调色走，否则红绿含义就认不出来了。模型品牌 mark 也不参与这套推导：有彩色原件的按品牌原色画，没有的固定一枚中性灰，你挑的墨色染不到它们。 - 10 套基础预设 ：纸墨、夜金、雾蓝、深海、松墨、苔绿、陶土、暮紫、石墨、铅字。点预设等于把四个源色一次填好，之后你照样可以只改其中一格；一改色板，这一套就不再是那个预设，高亮会自动清掉。 - 字体 ：正文与代码各一个下拉，候选先列系统里已有的字体族（含「跟随系统」）；下拉下面那颗「导入字体」把自己手上的 .woff2 / .woff / .ttf / .otf（单个文件、16MB 以内）导进来，导入后它和系统字体一样出现在两个下拉里，清单逐条报出大小并可单独移除。字体文件只存在本机 data/theme/fonts/ ，不进对话记录、也不复制到另一台设备；只导入一个字重时，粗体与斜体由浏览器模拟。字体栈末尾带通用族兜底，换一台没装也没导入这字体的机器不会糊成默认衬线。 - 板块透明度 ：一个「板块不透明度」滑杆（55%–100%，默认 100%），管的是面板、卡片、输入框与助手气泡这一层底色的不透明度。100% 就是实色，行为与旧版逐像素相同；调低会透出页面底色，铺了背景图时也能透出图。你自己的气泡和代码块始终保持实色，正文不会因此读不清。它和遮罩浓度各管一层：板块取两者里更低的那一档，不会两层各降一次把面板洗没。 - 背景图 ：挑一张本机的 PNG / JPG / GIF / WebP（不超过 4MB），只有这一张存在本机的数据目录里，不随局域网遥控上传，也不进备份。铺上之后界面会变为半透明骑在图上，「遮罩浓度」滑杆调的就是底色的不透明度（40%–95%，默认 72%）：低于 40% 正文就直接骑在图上，高于 95% 等于没有图。填图与摘图都走顶栏那个「移除」按钮，摘掉后底色回到实色。 - Wallpaper Engine ：装过 Wallpaper Engine 的机器上，这里多一颗「取当前壁纸」——把它正在用的那张壁纸的预览图取过来当上面的背景图，旁边「重新检测」用来在它被关掉或换过目录后重新认一次。这一步是只读的：SLATE 只看 Wallpaper Engine 写在自己目录里的配置和 preview 图，不启动它、也不改它的任何设置。它换了壁纸这里不会跟着换，要再点一次；视频与场景壁纸取到的是那张静态预览图，只有动态预览时才取 gif（那张会在界面后面一直动）。状态那一行只报壁纸名、文件大小和来源屏号，不报本机的绝对路径。 - 开启自定义主题时不能切明暗 ：整套令牌是从那四个源色推导的，再切一次明暗底版只会两套色互相矛盾。这时明暗按钮会变淡并写明「锁定」，点它不会换色，而是弹一句提示告诉你去哪关掉自定义主题；移动端同样。把自定义主题关掉，一切痕迹（注入样式、颜色、字体、背景图）当场摘干净，明暗切换随即复活。 - 界面语言 ：安装时选择简体中文或 English，应用运行中不提供切换入口。English 界面翻的是界面文案本身——按钮、区块标题、输入框里的灰字提示（placeholder）与悬浮提示都算；模型生成的内容与你自己输入的文字、以及你贴进对话的代码始终原样呈现，不会被翻译。品牌字标「砚」也保持原样。 - 想换语言，重新运行安装程序并选择另一种语言即可；如果只是想让某台设备换个语言，同样以安装时选定为准。 背景图和 data/theme/fonts/ 里那些字体文件都只存在这台机器的数据目录里，不写进备份、也不跟着同步。换一台设备或重装后，同一个开关会显示「本机还没有背景图」「本机还没有导入的字体」，重新挑一份即可——配色、透明度与字体选择不受影响，照常生效。 关于区块 「关于」把版本与更新相关的操作集中在一处： - 当前版本 ：显示你现在运行的是哪一版。 - 检查更新 ：启动时也会自动检查一次。发现新版本会显示当前版本与最新版本、更新说明摘要，以及「下载更新」「更新说明」两个按钮，都会用系统浏览器打开。 - 新手引导 ：忘了三栏工作台怎么用，点这里重看一遍。 - 项目链接 ：官网与 GitHub 仓库，同样用系统浏览器打开。 安全与审批 SLATE 遵循「零自主修改」准则：你说改，它就真去改；你不说，它绝不擅自碰。为了让这条准则落地，动手前会经过几道门。 审批模式 （「设置 → 审批模式」可设默认档，单个对话还可在输入框的审批模式选择器里随时改，改过的那一场就不再跟着默认档变）： 模式 语义 --- --- 手动审批 执行命令与访问网络都先问过你 自动审批 只有命中高危规则的才问，其余直接放行 完全访问 命令与联网都不问了；但模型缺条件时仍会弹选择题问你一句。灾难级命令照样由后端拦死，这一档在选择器上标红 夜间模式 全托管：命令、联网都不问，连模型「缺条件时想问你一句」也不问——它自己选定方案继续。适合挂着跑完再看 夜间模式这一档对用户的承诺是「没有任何东西在等人」：审批弹窗不会等你批准，选择题也不会等你回来选。因此灾难级命令仍然在任何模式下被后端强制拦死——那一道不是「问」，是「不许」。 高危命令 采用写死规则判定，前后端双层拦截：前端拦一次，后端再独立拦一次，绕过界面也拦得住。命中后弹窗会写清命令原文与触发的规则，并附一句由模型生成的命令目的说明，由你决定批准还是拒绝；被拒绝时，模型会收到明确的拒绝理由，并被要求不要重试。灾难级命令（如清空根目录、格式化磁盘）在任何模式下都无条件禁止。 文件编辑 走差异预览：改动以差异卡片呈现，接受才落盘，拒绝则不写入。设置里有一个「自动确认文件创建/修改」开关，默认开启——也就是常规写入无需你每次点接受；想每一笔都亲自过目，把它关掉即可恢复逐笔确认。 操作 需要审批 自动放行 --- --- --- 执行终端命令 手动审批与自动审批下按规则确认 完全访问与夜间模式 启动后台任务 同上 完全访问与夜间模式 联网搜索、抓取网页、浏览器自动化 手动审批下逐条确认 自动审批、完全访问与夜间模式 模型缺条件时向你提问（选择题弹窗） 手动、自动审批与完全访问下等你选完再继续 夜间模式（改为让模型自行拍板） 读取文件、浏览目录、搜索代码等只读操作 不经审批 任何模式 创建与编辑文件 取决于「自动确认文件创建/修改」开关 该开关开启时 灾难级命令 任何模式都无条件禁止 无 访问凭据文件（如密钥、证书、环境变量文件） 任何模式都禁止 无 透明防护 ：路径穿越会被拦截，系统敏感目录不可读写；凭据类文件禁止访问；工具输出超过上限会自动截断，单文件操作也有大小上限；上传的文件名会被消毒。这些都在后台默默生效，不需要你配置。 自动审批适合你已经熟悉模型会做什么、盯着屏幕的场景；完全访问再往前一步，连高危确认也不弹——它只留下「模型缺条件时要问你一句」，适合你认可它大范围动手、但还得由你定方向；夜间模式适合你离开屏幕、让它自己跑完再看结果的场景——正因为它不会回头问你，任务描述里把目标与验收标准写清楚更重要，方向选偏了它会自己接着往下走。拿不准就用手动审批，多一两次点击，换来的是每一笔环境改动都经过你的许可。 想了解工具有哪些、流程说明书怎么写，见 内置工具与技能；想了解备份和存储清理，见 数据、备份与存储。"},{"t":"常见问题","u":"zh/20-faq.html","s":"常见问题","h":["按症状速查","模型连不上，或提示 API Key 无效怎么办","为什么模型不调用工具，只是嘴上说要做什么","终端命令被拦下来了","中文或 emoji 出现乱码","局域网或手机连不上 SLATE","数据太多了怎么清理","怎么省 Token，或者关掉用不到的 AI 功能","会话太多，又不舍得删怎么办","工具列表里少了几个工具","自定义流程说明书校验不过","辩论中途想停怎么办","切换项目会不会打断正在跑的对话","移动端怎么设访问密码","怎么换界面语言","换电脑或重装后怎么迁移数据","生成的提示词怎么用在 Claude Code、Codex 或 Cursor 里","我能用它做什么，不适合做什么","任务列表上那些小标志是什么意思","对话很长了会不会超出模型窗口","磨墨模式产出的墨稿接下来怎么用","长期记忆会不会记错","白板上的卡片是谁建的","定时任务到点了却没执行","想给一段重复流程写个说明书，该从哪下手","我能让 SLATE 直接用我自己的技能吗","还有问题？"],"x":"这一页收集使用中最常遇到的疑问，按「先看哪一页」的方式给出答案。每条都能点进对应的页面看完整说明。 按症状速查 你看到的 先看哪一页 --- --- 报错、连不上、Key 无效 模型接入与 API Key 模型只说要做什么，不动手 Agent Autopilot 与目标模式 命令、联网、文件写入被拦 设置与安全审批 手机连不上、遥控打不开 移动端遥控与局域网访问 磁盘占用大、想清历史 数据、备份与存储 工具少了几项、想省 Token 设置与安全审批 流程说明书保存不了 Actions 流程说明书 换机、重装、搬数据 数据、备份与存储 不少「没反应」其实不是故障：可能是审批弹窗正等你点批准，也可能是这一场在排队（排队中的任务在右栏任务中心与输入框上方都看得见）。先扫一眼屏幕上有没有待处理的提示，再判断是不是真卡住了。 模型连不上，或提示 API Key 无效怎么办 先到「设置 → 模型配置」确认这个模型已经填好 API Key。不确定是密钥问题还是网络问题时，用「设置 → API 测试」跑一条最短请求，它会走真实对话同一条链路并给出结果。自定义端点要注意 Base URL 与请求模式是否匹配（OpenAI 兼容 / Anthropic / curl 直连）。本地模型则要确认 Ollama 或 LM Studio 已经在运行。详见 模型接入与 API Key。 为什么模型不调用工具，只是嘴上说要做什么 先检查「设置 → 自动推进」是否被关掉了——它专门负责在模型只表态不动手时催它补上工具调用。再确认「Continue Autopilot」是否开启，它会让轮数用尽的最后一刻继续推进。另外看看是不是有审批弹窗正等你点「批准」，没批准模型是不会往下走的。完整机制见 Agent Autopilot 与目标模式。 终端命令被拦下来了 命中写死的高危规则时（删除文件、提权、关机重启、强制推送等）会弹窗让你确认，批准前还能看到一句由模型生成的命令目的说明。灾难级命令在任何模式下都无条件禁止，这是设计如此。如果同类命令频繁被问，可把这一场或默认档位切到自动审批，只拦高危。见 设置与安全审批。 中文或 emoji 出现乱码 文件读取与编辑会自动识别 UTF-8、UTF-8 BOM、GB18030、GBK、UTF-16 等常见编码，中文、emoji 与全角符号会原样保留；旧编码表达不了新字符时，工具会安全升级写入编码而不是丢字。终端输出对原生命令做了编码安全处理，Windows 下也不会再弹出黑窗乱码。若仍然遇到乱码：先确认文件不是被外部程序以别的编码改写过，再用文件读取工具重新查看一次；必要时让内容以 UTF-8 重新保存。 局域网或手机连不上 SLATE 按顺序排查四件事：手机与电脑是否在同一个 Wi-Fi；电脑防火墙是否拦了端口（首次开启时要点允许）；地址是否用的是设置页显示的那一条（必须带授权码）；路由器是否开启了访客网络或 AP 隔离。若设置页提示「遥控服务未启动」，重启应用即可。见 移动端遥控与局域网访问。 数据太多了怎么清理 到「设置 → 数据备份 → 存储空间」先看总占用与分项。删掉一批会话后可以点「压缩」把空间真正还回来；想彻底重来可以「清空对话」，它只删会话与消息，不动长期记忆与偏好。内置浏览器缓存变大时点「清理缓存」。清理前建议先导出一份备份，详见 数据、备份与存储。 怎么省 Token，或者关掉用不到的 AI 功能 到「设置 → AI 辅助功能」，那里列着对话以外的 15 项耗 Token 功能，逐项可关，关掉后一次请求都不会为它发出。日常只做对话与工具执行的话，压缩摘要、记忆蒸馏、对话洞察、自动会话标题、提示词优化、图片与视频生成这些可以先关。列表上方会实时汇总已经关掉了几项。见 设置与安全审批。 会话太多，又不舍得删怎么办 「任务」栏每一行的操作里有一颗「归档」：按下去这场就从任务栏离场，但消息一条不动——归档不是删除。正在生成的那场不能归档，先停下来再收。要拿回来去「设置 → 会话归档」，逐条「恢复」它就重新出现在任务栏；那里的「删除」才是连消息一起清掉、找不回来的那一个。见 数据、备份与存储。 工具列表里少了几个工具 先看「设置 → AI 辅助功能」里对应功能的开关：子代理、图片生成、视频生成这三项关掉后，工具会直接从工具目录消失，模型看不到也就不会调用。图片与视频生成还需要在「图片生成 / 视频生成」区块里配好模型与 API Key，没配好同样不可用。若挂了外部 MCP Server，连接断开时它提供的工具也会暂时不在。见 内置工具与技能。 自定义流程说明书校验不过 流程说明书用的是极简格式：2 空格缩进、块数组、竖线字面量块。Tab 缩进、锚点、多文档等写法一律会被拒收，报错会写明「第 N 行：原因」，按住这个行号改就能过。校验没过是保存不了的，编辑器会边写边校验。完整字段与写法见 Actions 流程说明书。 辩论中途想停怎么办 团队面板上有停止按钮，点一下即中断后续发言，已经说完的内容会完整保留，不会丢成果。这一场也会进团队历史并标「已中断」，点开还能接着看——没有结论的场次不会被编出一份结论。停完之后你还可以接着追问，或换一组角色重新开一场。见 AI 团队与工作流。 切换项目会不会打断正在跑的对话 默认不会。离开的那一场会在后台继续生成，进度与结局落在右栏任务中心；同一项目内默认串行，跨项目可以并行，同时最多跑几场可以在「设置 → 多任务与项目」里调。如果你希望恢复「切换即中断」的旧行为，把该区块里的开关关掉即可。见 项目、工作区与分项目配置。 移动端怎么设访问密码 SLATE 的局域网访问不是用「设一串密码」的方式保护的，而是靠地址里自带的授权码：只有用设置页给出的那条地址或它的二维码进入的设备才能访问，不带授权码的访问会被自动拦下。因此保护办法是——不要把带授权码的地址或二维码发给别人，发出去等于把钥匙交出去。不需要遥控时关掉电脑上的 SLATE 即可让地址失效。见 移动端遥控与局域网访问。 怎么换界面语言 界面语言在安装时选定（简体中文或 English），应用运行中不提供切换入口，所以想换语言需要重新运行安装程序并选择另一种语言。切换语言只影响界面文案，模型生成的内容与你的输入不会被翻译。相关说明见 设置与安全审批。 换电脑或重装后怎么迁移数据 三步：在旧机器上「设置 → 数据备份 → 下载备份」拿到一个 JSON；在新机器上「设置 → 数据备份」点「导入备份」选中它；确认后页面自动重载。导入是按 ID 合并，已存在的数据会跳过，但本机偏好会被备份里那份替换。知识库文档、流程说明书、专家包与自定义技能不在这个 JSON 里，需要把本机数据目录一并带过去。见 数据、备份与存储。 生成的提示词怎么用在 Claude Code、Codex 或 Cursor 里 提示词工厂产出的是可交付的纯文本 Prompt，把它整段复制，粘贴到外部 Coding Agent 的对话框或项目说明文件里即可，格式不需要再改。想让它更贴合某个工具的习惯，可以在工厂里把交付要求与约束写清楚再生成。见 提示词工厂。 我能用它做什么，不适合做什么 它适合把灵感整理成结构化方案、驱动本机工具干活、把重复流程写成说明书反复使用，也适合生成高质量 Prompt 交给外部 Coding Agent 执行。不适合当纯粹的云端助手、不适合多人在线协作，也不适合在不受信任的公共网络里开放局域网遥控。见 认识 SLATE。 任务列表上那些小标志是什么意思 每条会话会按当下处境给一个标志：需要操作（上一场被停止或还有下一步）、出错、进行中（带呼吸动画，只在这一场真的在生成时出现）、已完成未查看（点进去即清除）。标志记的是「上一场怎么结束的」，如果这场会话在手机遥控那边被继续过，旧标志会自动作废。见 界面总览：三栏工作台。 对话很长了会不会超出模型窗口 不会直接超。上下文预算用满时会自动把旧消息压成一段摘要，压缩阈值与用量条分母都按你给这个模型设的预算算。想手动压一次，就在对话里点「压缩」。预算在「设置 → 上下文与项目」与模型行上的「最大上下文」里调，本地端点还能一键探测它的真实窗口，见 设置与安全审批。 磨墨模式产出的墨稿接下来怎么用 墨稿有三键：送入目标模式执行、投到白板推演、存为模板复用。想先看看再决定，就投到白板；想直接开干，就送进目标模式。见 磨墨模式。 长期记忆会不会记错 会，所以它可改可删。在记忆面板里逐条编辑内容或直接删除，过时信息被修正后，后续对话召回的就是新内容。开启记忆自动蒸馏时，每轮回复后都会静默读一遍最近对话，45 秒内不重复。见 记忆、画像与知识库。 白板上的卡片是谁建的 两种来源：模型在干活时自动建的（工具执行会记成带状态颜色的步骤卡片），以及你手动拖拽新建的。卡片、连线与步骤卡都能自己编辑和清理。见 白板式逻辑链。 定时任务到点了却没执行 先确认任务处于启用状态，再检查「设置 → AI 辅助功能」里的「定时与事件任务」是否被关掉了——关掉后任务不再执行，但触发记录仍会保留。结果会归档到带前缀的独立会话里，去会话列表找那一场即可。见 任务清单与定时任务。 想给一段重复流程写个说明书，该从哪下手 把它写成 Actions 流程说明书，模型在智能体态下会自动看到目录，按需读取全文再照流程推进。写坏的版本不会凭空消失，会连同原因一起列出来，每次覆盖或删除都会留底、可一键回滚。见 Actions 流程说明书。 我能让 SLATE 直接用我自己的技能吗 可以。把技能写成一份 SKILL.md 就能被识别，之后在聊天里用 @ 提及，它就会被注入这条消息的上下文。专家包则是「人格 + 规则 + 知识 + 技能」的整套，可导入可导出。见 内置工具与技能 与 专家包。 还有问题？ 上面的页面都没解决时，欢迎到项目的 GitHub 仓库提 Issue，附上你的系统版本、操作步骤与报错原文，会更容易定位问题： - 仓库地址： https://github.com/CaryWang1234/SLATE - 也能从「设置 → 关于」用「GitHub」按钮直接打开。"}]};
